PRJNA1211805					78	WGA	pubmed	41572341.0	41572341.0	Cross-sectional study characterizing the porcine faecal microbiome in commercial farms.	Ornelas MAS	Manzanilla EG	Mammalia	BACKGROUND: The gut microbiome is regarded as an important source of information to better understand and improve health and growth in animals. However, there is a knowledge gap concerning the effects of management and health in commercial farms on the porcine faecal microbiome. This study aimed to identify the main factors associated with differences in microbiota alpha and beta diversity, resistome and functional profiles between and within farms and to study the associations between microbiome and farm characteristics. The faecal microbiomes at four production stages were examined in 18 farms, using shotgun sequencing. RESULTS: Microbiota richness increased with age while evenness and Simpson diversity decreased. Production stage was associated with microbiota composition, resistome and functional profiles, and most differences in the microbiome were observed between weaned pigs and older animals. Microbiota richness, diversity and evenness were higher in farms that purchased feed and in those with lower salmonella prevalence. Microbiota composition was associated with use of zinc oxide and medicated feed in young pigs and with health, biosecurity and feed form in older pigs. Feed form was associated with resistome, functional profiles and especially with core microbiota, as dry fed pigs showed higher abundances of Streptococcus alactolyticus. CONCLUSIONS: Consistent patterns of alpha and beta diversity were observed across farms with contrasting characteristics, suggesting that the faecal microbiota in commercial pig farms is primarily driven by diet and age, rather than health. Associations between microbiome, salmonella prevalence and diet suggested that diet induced changes in the microbiome may be a determining factor in gut colonization and infection by pathogens. SUPPLEMENTARY INFORMATION: The online version contains supplementary material available at 10.1186/s40813-025-00480-3.	2026.0	Porcine health management	YES		METAGENOMIC	YES	Animalia	Chordata	Artiodactyla	Suidae	Sus	Sus scrofa domesticus	10.1186/s40813-025-00480-3				WGA:78	0	1	Sus scrofa		gut metagenome; synthetic metagenome	DNA extraction Negative Control; Library Preparation Negative Control; Positive Control of mock community DNA extraction; feces	Metagenome or environmental sample from gut metagenome; Metagenome or environmental sample from synthetic metagenome	78.0	https://doi.org/10.1186/s40813-025-00480-3	True	True	Sus scrofa	True	pig	True	pig	False		False	ENA + paper	Faecal samples were collected from pigs at four production stages across 18 commercial farms and subjected to shotgun sequencing.	UNIFORM	78	METAGENOMIC:78	78	0	REVIEW	yes	high	gut_explicit	single_clear	Sus scrofa	False	mismatch	GUT: yes (high) — Title explicitly states 'porcine faecal microbiome'; abstract confirms 'gut microbiome' study examining 'faecal microbiomes' using 'shotgun sequencing'; library_source is METAGENOMIC (community DNA); biome label includes 'gut metagenome'; isolation source lists 'feces'. || BODY_SITE: gut_explicit — Title and abstract explicitly reference 'faecal microbiome' and 'faecal microbiomes'; biome label includes 'gut metagenome'; isolation source explicitly lists 'feces'. || HOST: Sus scrofa [single_clear] — Host species field states '1 distinct' species: Sus scrofa (domestic pig); title confirms 'porcine' (pig) study; abstract describes study across 18 farms but all samples are from the same host species. || METHOD: mismatch — Abstract and title state 'shotgun sequencing' was used, but ENA fields show WGS/shotgun sample count: 0 and library_strategy_mix lists only WGA (whole genome amplification, n=78). WGA is not equivalent to shotgun sequencing. This discrepancy suggests either metadata recording error or method description mismatch. || SUMMARY: This is a genuine animal (pig) gu