Salmonids are important sources of protein for a large proportion of the human 
population. Mycoplasma species are a major constituent of the gut microbiota of 
salmonids, often representing the majority of microbiota. Despite the frequent 
reported dominance of salmonid-related Mycoplasma species, little is known about 
the phylogenomic placement, functions and potential evolutionary relationships 
with their salmonid hosts. In this study, we utilise 2.9 billion metagenomic 
reads generated from 12 samples from three different salmonid host species to I) 
characterise and curate the first metagenome-assembled genomes (MAGs) of 
Mycoplasma dominating the intestines of three different salmonid species, II) 
establish the phylogeny of these salmonid candidate Mycoplasma species, III) 
perform a comprehensive pangenomic analysis of Mycoplasma, IV) decipher the 
putative functionalities of the salmonid MAGs and reveal specific functions 
expected to benefit the host. Our data provide a basis for future studies 
examining the composition and function of the salmonid microbiota.

