PRJNA542998	mice			China	201	WGS	pubmed	33382948.0	33382948.0	Exposure to soil environments during earlier life stages is distinguishable in the gut microbiome of adult mice.	Liu W	Zhang H	Mammalia	Environmental exposure during earlier life stages can govern the assembly and development of gut microbiota, yet it is insufficiently understood. In this study, ex-germ-free mice were cohoused with distinct soil-microbiota (from desert, steppe, and forest) beddings within 60 days after birth and subsequently transferred to new soil beddings from 60 to 90th day. Using metagenomic shotgun sequencing, firstly, we found soil microbes from natural environments (birthplace) greatly influenced the gut community assembly in the housing experiment. About 27% microbial species and 12% functional components that associated with birthplaces at Day 60 were still discriminatory of birthplaces after transferring mice to new environments. Moreover, prior soil-exposure types are associated with the magnitude of temporal microbiome change due to environmental shifts. The appropriate soil-exposure (e.g., steppe) might help mice gut microbiome adapt to changing environments or host development. Our study demonstrated the continuous soil-exposure history earlier is associated with the gut microbiome individuality and development later.	2021.0	Gut microbes	YES		METAGENOMIC	YES	Animalia	Chordata	Rodentia	Muridae	Mus	musculus	10.1080/19490976.2020.1830699				WGS:201	201	0			feces metagenome	mice fecals	D13240; D13241; D13242; D13243; D13244	201.0	https://doi.org/10.1080/19490976.2020.1830699	True	True	mice	True	mice	True	mouse (model of environmental exposure effects on gut microbiota development)	True	environmental exposure effects on gut microbiota development	False	ENA + paper	[model of environmental exposure effects on gut microbiota development] Metagenomic shotgun sequencing was performed on fecal samples from germ-free mice exposed to different soil-microbiota beddings during early life stages.	UNIFORM	201	METAGENOMIC:201	201	201	KEEP	yes	high	gut_explicit	single_clear	Mus musculus (mice)	False	agree	GUT: yes (high) — Title states 'gut microbiome of adult mice'; abstract describes metagenomic shotgun sequencing of gut microbiota assembly; biome label explicitly 'feces metagenome'; isolation source 'mice fecals'. This is a genuine animal gut microbiome study. || BODY_SITE: gut_explicit — Biome label field: 'feces metagenome'; isolation source field: 'mice fecals'; abstract: 'gut microbiota' and 'gut community assembly'. Gut origin is explicitly stated. || HOST: Mus musculus (mice) [single_clear] — Host field states 'mice'; title specifies 'adult mice'; abstract describes experimental design with 'ex-germ-free mice' cohoused with soil beddings. Single host species, no multi-host survey structure. All samples (201 WGS) are from the same host species in a controlled experimental setting. || METHOD: agree — Abstract explicitly states 'metagenomic shotgun sequencing'; ENA library_strategy_mix shows 'WGS:201' (whole-genome shotgun); library_source is 'METAGENOMIC' (community DNA, not single organism). Methods and metadata are consistent. || SUMMARY: This is a rigorous experimental study of mouse gut microbiota assembly following early-life soil environmental exposure, using shotgun metagenomics on fecal samples. Single clear host (mice), explicit gut site, genuine microbiome study, and method metadata fully aligned.	none					No reporting errors detected. Paper and deposit are fully consistent: mice as host, fecal samples as intended gut microbiome study.	1		False		True	False
PRJNA599288	missing			China:Hangzhou	12	WGS	pubmed	35499050.0	35499050.0	Genetic divergence and functional convergence of gut bacteria between the Eastern honey bee Apis cerana and the Western honey bee Apis mellifera.	Wu Y	Zheng H	Insecta	INTRODUCTION: The functional relevance of intra-species diversity in natural microbial communities remains largely unexplored. The guts of two closely related honey bee species, Apis cerana and A. mellifera, are colonised by a similar set of core bacterial species composed of host-specific strains, thereby providing a good model for an intra-species diversity study. OBJECTIVES: We aim to assess the functional relevance of intra-species diversity of A. cerana and A. mellifera gut microbiota. METHODS: Honey bee workers were collected from four regions of China. Their gut microbiomes were investigated by shotgun metagenomic sequencing, and the bacterial compositions were compared at the species level. A cross-species colonisation assay was conducted, with the gut metabolomes being characterised by LC-MS/MS. RESULTS: Comparative analysis showed that the strain composition of the core bacterial species was host-specific. These core bacterial species presented distinctive functional profiles between the hosts. However, the overall functional profiles of the A. cerana and A. mellifera gut microbiomes were similar; this was further supported by the consistency of the honey bees' gut metabolome, as the gut microbiota of different honey bee species showed rather similar metabolic profiles in the cross-species colonisation assay. Moreover, this experiment also demonstrated that the gut microbiota of A. cerana and A. mellifera could cross colonise between the two honey bee species. CONCLUSION: Our findings revealed functional differences in most core gut bacteria between the guts of A. cerana and A. mellifera, which may be associated with their inter-species diversity. However, the functional profiles of the overall gut microbiomes between the two honey bee species converge, probably as a result of the overlapping ecological niches of the two species. Our findings provide critical insights into the evolution and functional roles of the mutualistic microbiota of honey bees and reveal that functional redundancy could stabilise the gene content diversity at the strain-level within the gut community.	2022.0	Journal of advanced research	YES		METAGENOMIC	YES	Animalia	Arthropoda	Hymenoptera	Apidae	Apis	cerana	10.1016/j.jare.2021.08.002				WGS:12	12	0			insect gut metagenome	eastern honey bee gut; western honey bee gut	MIMS Environmental/Metagenome sample from insect gut metagenome	12.0	https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jare.2021.08.002	True	False		True	Apis cerana; Apis mellifera	True	Apis cerana; Apis mellifera	False		False	paper only	Shotgun metagenomic sequencing was performed on gut samples from honey bee workers of two species collected from four regions of China.	NO_HOST_DATA	0	METAGENOMIC:12	12	12	KEEP	yes	high	gut_explicit	multi_host	Apis cerana; Apis mellifera	False	agree	GUT: yes (high) — Abstract explicitly states 'The guts of two closely related honey bee species, Apis cerana and A. mellifera, are colonised by a similar set of core bacterial species' and methods describe 'shotgun metagenomic sequencing' of 'gut microbiomes'. Isolation source confirms 'eastern honey bee gut; western honey bee gut'. This is a genuine animal gut microbiome study. || BODY_SITE: gut_explicit — Title specifies 'gut bacteria'. Abstract repeatedly references 'guts' and 'gut microbiota'. Biome label states 'insect gut metagenome'. Isolation sources explicitly list 'eastern honey bee gut' and 'western honey bee gut'. || HOST: Apis cerana; Apis mellifera [multi_host] — Paper title and abstract indicate a comparative study OF two distinct honey bee species: 'Apis cerana and the Western honey bee Apis mellifera'. Abstract states objective is to 'assess the functional relevance of intra-species diversity of A. cerana and A. mellifera gu