PRJEB79352	Gallus gallus			United Kingdom	240	WGS	pubmed	39881387.0	39881387.0	Impact of commercial gut health interventions on caecal metagenome and broiler performance.	Pangga GM	Gundogdu O	Aves	BACKGROUND: Maintaining gut health is a persistent and unresolved challenge in the poultry industry. Given the critical role of gut health in chicken performance and welfare, there is a pressing need to identify effective gut health intervention (GHI) strategies to ensure optimal outcomes in poultry farming. In this study, across three broiler production cycles, we compared the metagenomes and performance of broilers provided with ionophores (as the control group) against birds subjected to five different GHI combinations involving vaccination, probiotics, prebiotics, essential oils, and reduction of ionophore use. RESULTS: Using a binning strategy, 84 (≥ 75% completeness, ≤ 5% contamination) metagenome-assembled genomes (MAGs) from 118 caecal samples were recovered and annotated for their metabolic potential. The majority of these (n = 52, 61%) had a differential response across all cohorts and are associated with the performance parameter - European poultry efficiency factor (EPEF). The control group exhibited the highest EPEF, followed closely by the cohort where probiotics are used in conjunction with vaccination. The use of probiotics B, a commercial Bacillus strain-based formulation, was determined to contribute to the superior performance of birds. GHI supplementation generally affected the abundance of microbial enzymes relating to carbohydrate and protein digestion and metabolic pathways relating to energy, nucleotide synthesis, short-chain fatty acid synthesis, and drug-transport systems. These shifts are hypothesised to differentiate performance among groups and cycles, highlighting the beneficial role of several bacteria, including Rikenella microfusus and UBA7160 species. CONCLUSIONS: All GHIs are shown to be effective methods for gut microbial modulation, with varying influences on MAG diversity, composition, and microbial functions. These metagenomic insights greatly enhance our understanding of microbiota-related metabolic pathways, enabling us to devise strategies against enteric pathogens related to poultry products and presenting new opportunities to improve overall poultry performance and health. Video Abstract.	2025.0	Microbiome	YES		METAGENOMIC	YES	Animalia	Chordata	Galliformes	Phasianidae	Gallus	gallus	10.1186/s40168-024-02012-7				WGS:240	240	0			Gallus gallus		MP01_L1; MP01_L3; MP02_L1; MP02_L3; MP03_L1	240.0	https://doi.org/10.1186/s40168-024-02012-7	True	True	Gallus gallus	True	broiler	True	broiler	False		False	ENA + paper	Caecal samples were sequenced from broiler chickens across three production cycles.	UNIFORM	240	METAGENOMIC:240	240	240	KEEP	yes	high	gut_explicit	single_clear	Gallus gallus	False	agree	GUT: yes (high) — Paper title explicitly states 'caecal metagenome' (caecum is part of avian gut); abstract confirms 'metagenomes...from 118 caecal samples' and describes a microbiome study of broiler chicken intestinal content across multiple intervention cycles. || BODY_SITE: gut_explicit — Title and abstract both explicitly name 'caecal' (caecum, a major gut compartment in birds); abstract states 'caecal samples'; sample titles (MP01_L1, etc.) do not contradict gut origin. || HOST: Gallus gallus [single_clear] — Host field states 'Gallus gallus' (domestic chicken); abstract confirms 'broilers' studied across three production cycles with multiple treatment groups but all from the same species; biome label confirms 'Gallus gallus'. || METHOD: agree — Paper describes metagenome-assembled genomes (MAGs) recovered from caecal samples using a binning strategy on 118 caecal samples; ENA metadata shows library_strategy_mix 'WGS:240' (shotgun metagenomics), library_source 'METAGENOMIC' (community DNA, not single organism), and 240 WGS samples total, consistent with metagenomic sequencing of microbial communities