PRJNA358755	Panda	212257.0		China: wolong	207	WGA	pubmed	31635158.0	31635158.0	Comparative Study of Gut Microbiota in Wild and Captive Giant Pandas (Ailuropoda melanoleuca).	Guo W	Li Y	Magnoliopsida	"Captive breeding has been used as an effective approach to protecting endangered animals but its effect on the gut microbiome and the conservation status of these species is largely unknown. The giant panda is a flagship species for the conservation of wildlife. With integrated efforts including captive breeding, this species has been recently upgraded from ""endangered"" to ""vulnerable"" (IUCN 2016). Since a large proportion (21.8%) of their global population is still captive, it is critical to understand how captivity changes the gut microbiome of these pandas and how such alterations to the microbiome might affect their future fitness and potential impact on the ecosystem after release into the wild. Here, we use 16S rRNA (ribosomal RNA) marker gene sequencing and shotgun metagenomics sequencing to demonstrate that the fecal microbiomes differ substantially between wild and captive giant pandas. Fecal microbiome diversity was significantly lower in captive pandas, as was the diversity of functional genes. Additionally, captive pandas have reduced functional potential for cellulose degradation but enriched metabolic pathways for starch metabolism, indicating that they may not adapt to a wild diet after being released into the wild since a major component of their diet in the wild will be bamboo. Most significantly, we observed a significantly higher level of amylase activity but a lower level of cellulase activity in captive giant panda feces than those of wild giant pandas, shown by an in vitro experimental assay. Furthermore, antibiotic resistance genes and virulence factors, as well as heavy metal tolerance genes were enriched in the microbiomes of captive pandas, which raises a great concern of spreading these genes to other wild animals and ecosystems when they are released into a wild environment. Our results clearly show that captivity has altered the giant panda microbiome, which could have unintended negative consequences on their adaptability and the ecosystem during the reintroduction of giant pandas into the wild."	2019.0	Genes	YES		METAGENOMIC	YES	Viridiplantae	Streptophyta	Malpighiales	Pandaceae		Panda	10.3390/genes10100827				WGA:196; WGS:11	11	0			gut metagenome	feces	Metagenome or environmental sample from gut metagenome	207.0	https://doi.org/10.3390/genes10100827	True	True	Panda	True	giant panda	True	giant panda	False		False	ENA + paper	Fecal samples were sequenced from wild and captive giant pandas using 16S rRNA and shotgun metagenomics.	DIVERSE	207	METAGENOMIC:207	207	11	KEEP	yes	high	gut_explicit	multi_host	Ailuropoda melanoleuca (Giant Panda)	False	agree	GUT: yes (high) — Paper title states 'Comparative Study of Gut Microbiota in Wild and Captive Giant Pandas'; abstract confirms 'shotgun metagenomics' study of gut microbiome; isolation source explicitly 'feces'; biome label 'gut metagenome'; library_source 'METAGENOMIC' (community DNA). || BODY_SITE: gut_explicit — Biome label explicitly 'gut metagenome'; isolation source 'feces'; sample titles reference 'gut metagenome'; abstract discusses 'gut microbiome' throughout. || HOST: Ailuropoda melanoleuca (Giant Panda) [multi_host] — Paper title states 'Comparative Study of Gut Microbiota in Wild and Captive Giant Pandas' — explicitly comparing two groups (wild vs. captive) of the same species. This is a genuine comparative study OF multiple hosts' guts, not an incidental survey. Host field 'Panda' and species binomial in title confirm identity. || METHOD: agree — Abstract states study uses '16S rRNA marker gene sequencing and shotgun metagenomics'; ENA library_strategy_mix shows WGS:11 (shotgun) samples, consistent with stated shotgun metagenomics approach. The 196 WGA samples represent the 16S component. || SUMMARY: This is a rigorous comparative gut microbi