PRJNA598094	Apis cerana japonica	292787.0		Japan:Chiba	40	WGS	pubmed	32531278.0	32531278.0	Vast Differences in Strain-Level Diversity in the Gut Microbiota of Two Closely Related Honey Bee Species.	Ellegaard KM	Engel P	Insecta	Most bacterial species encompass strains with vastly different gene content. Strain diversity in microbial communities is therefore considered to be of functional importance. Yet little is known about the extent to which related microbial communities differ in diversity at this level and which underlying mechanisms may constrain and maintain strain-level diversity. Here, we used shotgun metagenomics to characterize and compare the gut microbiota of two honey bee species, Apis mellifera and Apis cerana, which diverged about 6 mya. Although the host species are colonized largely by the same bacterial 16S rRNA phylotypes, we find that their communities are host specific when analyzed with genomic resolution. Moreover, despite their similar ecology, A. mellifera displayed a much higher diversity of strains and functional gene content in the microbiota compared to A. cerana, both per colony and per individual bee. In particular, the gene repertoire for polysaccharide degradation was massively expanded in the microbiota of A. mellifera relative to A. cerana. Bee management practices, divergent ecological adaptation, or habitat size may have contributed to the observed differences in microbiota genomic diversity of these key pollinator species. Our results illustrate that the gut microbiota of closely related animal hosts can differ vastly in genomic diversity while displaying similar levels of diversity based on the 16S rRNA gene. Such differences are likely to have consequences for gut microbiota functioning and host-symbiont interactions, highlighting the need for metagenomic studies to understand the ecology and evolution of microbial communities.	2020.0	Current biology : CB	YES		METAGENOMIC	YES	Metazoa	Arthropoda	Hymenoptera	Apidae	Apis	Apis cerana	10.1016/j.cub.2020.04.070				WGS:40	40	2	Apis cerana japonica; Apis mellifera		insect gut metagenome		MIMS Environmental/Metagenome sample from insect gut metagenome	40.0	https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cub.2020.04.070	True	True	Apis cerana japonica	True	honey bee	True	Apis mellifera; Apis cerana	False		False	ENA + paper	Shotgun metagenomics was performed on gut microbiota samples from two honey bee species.	DIVERSE	40	METAGENOMIC:40	40	40	KEEP	yes	high	gut_explicit	multi_host	Apis cerana japonica; Apis mellifera	False	agree	GUT: yes (high) — Paper explicitly states 'shotgun metagenomics to characterize and compare the gut microbiota of two honey bee species'; biome label confirms 'insect gut metagenome'; sample titles specify 'insect gut metagenome'. || BODY_SITE: gut_explicit — Title states 'gut microbiota'; abstract repeatedly uses 'gut microbiota'; biome label 'insect gut metagenome'; sample titles reference 'insect gut metagenome'. || HOST: Apis cerana japonica; Apis mellifera [multi_host] — Host field lists 'Apis cerana japonica'; abstract explicitly states study compares 'two honey bee species, Apis mellifera and Apis cerana'; host species field confirms '2 distinct' species (Apis cerana japonica; Apis mellifera). This is a genuine comparative study OF two hosts' guts, not an incidental survey. || METHOD: agree — Abstract states 'shotgun metagenomics'; library_strategy_mix shows 'WGS:40' (40 WGS samples); library_source is 'METAGENOMIC' (community DNA, correct for metagenomics); 40 WGS samples align with stated approach. || SUMMARY: This is a rigorous multi-host comparative gut microbiome study of two honey bee species using shotgun metagenomics. All metadata fields (biome label, sample titles, host species count) align with the paper's explicit focus on strain-level diversity in honey bee gut microbiotas.	multihost_issue				Deposit host_species_list shows TWO DISTINCT species: Apis cerana japonica and Apis mellifera. Paper presents these as TWO SEPARATE SPECIE