PRJEB76898	Podarcis muralis	64176.0		Spain	940	WGS	pubmed	41784373.0	41784373.0	Grass supplementation to a pellet-based diet fails to enrich gut microbiomes with wild-like functions in captive-bred hares.	Aizpurua O	Alberdi A	Lepidosauria	Reintroducing captive-bred animals into the wild often faces limited success, with the underlying causes frequently unclear. One emerging hypothesis is that maladapted gut microbiota may play a significant role in these challenges. To investigate this possibility, we employed genome-resolved metagenomics to analyze the taxonomic and functional differences in the gut microbiota of 45 wild and captive European hares (Lepus europaeus), as well as to assess the impact of fresh grass supplementation to a pellet-based diet aimed at pre-adapting captive hares to wild conditions. Our analyses recovered 860 metagenome-assembled genomes, with 87% of them representing novel species. We found significant taxonomic and functional differences between the gut microbiota of wild and captive hares, notably the absence of Spirochaetota in captive animals and differences in amino acid and sugar degradation capacities. While grass supplementation induced some minor changes in the gut microbiota, it did not lead to statistically significant shifts toward a more wild-like microbial community. The increased capacity for degrading amino acids and specific sugars observed in wild hares suggests that, instead of bulk grass, dietary interventions tailored to their specific dietary preferences might be necessary for pre-adapting hare gut microbiota to wild conditions.IMPORTANCEThis study sheds light on the role of gut microbiota in the success of reintroducing captive-bred animals into the wild. By comparing the collection of 860 near-complete genomes of wild and captive European hares, we identified significant taxonomic and functional differences, including the absence of key microbial groups in captive hares. Grass supplementation to a pellet-based diet yielded limited success in restoring a microbiota similar to that of wild counterparts, highlighting the need for more tailored approaches to mimic natural diets. With 87% of recovered microbial genomes representing novel species, this research also enriches our understanding of microbial diversity in wildlife. These findings emphasize that maladapted gut microbiota may hinder the survival and adaptation of reintroduced animals, suggesting that microbiome-targeted strategies could improve conservation efforts and the success of animal rewilding programs.	2026.0	Microbiology spectrum	YES		METAGENOMIC	YES	Metazoa	Chordata	Squamata	Lacertidae	Podarcis	Podarcis muralis	10.1128/spectrum.03691-25				WGS:940	940	22	Calotriton asper; Canis lupus familiaris; Chalcides striatus; Geospizopsis unicolor; Lepus europaeus; Lissotriton helveticus; Natrix astreptophora; Perisoreus infaustus; Plecotus auritus; Podarcis filfolensis; Podarcis gaigeae; Podarcis liolepis; Podarcis milensis; Podarcis muralis; Podarcis pityusensis; Psittacula echo; Salamandra atra; Sciurus carolinensis; Sciurus vulgaris; Strix aluco; Trichosurus vulpecula; Zonotrichia capensis		Calotriton asper; Podarcis muralis; feces metagenome; indoor metagenome; oral metagenome		AAA01; AAA02; AAA04; AAA08; AAA11	940.0	https://doi.org/10.1128/spectrum.03691-25	True	True	Podarcis muralis	True	European hares	True	European hares (Lepus europaeus)	False		False	ENA + paper	Sequenced samples came from the gut microbiota of 45 wild and captive European hares.	DIVERSE	930	METAGENOMIC:938; GENOMIC:2	938	940	REVIEW						False		GUT:  () —  || BODY_SITE:  —  || HOST:  [] —  || METHOD:  —  || SUMMARY: PARSE_ERROR	host_mismatch; gut_sample_issue; multihost_issue	paper: Lepus europaeus (European hares) vs deposit: Podarcis muralis (wall lizard) listed as primary host; 22 distinct host species across samples including Lepus europaeus		Biome labels include 'oral metagenome' and 'indoor metagenome' alongside 'feces metagenome'. The paper i