PRJNA1088536	Bos taurus	9913.0		USA	112	WGS	pubmed	39030654.0	39030654.0	Effects of dietary zinc on the gut microbiome and resistome of the gestating cow and neonatal calf.	Drake MJ	Redding LE	Mammalia	Zinc is an essential trace element required in the diet of all species. While the effects of zinc have been studied in growing calves, little is known about the effect of zinc on the microbiota of the gestating cow or her neonatal calf. Understanding factors that shape the gut health of neonatal animals and evaluating the effect of dietary supplements in adult gestating animals is important in promoting animal health and informing feeding practices. The aims of this study were to determine the effect of dietary zinc on the microbiota and resistome of the gestating cow and calf. Gestating cows received standard (40 ppm) or high (205 ppm) dietary zinc levels from dry off to calving. Fecal samples were collected from cows upon enrollment and at calving and from neonatal calves. Fecal samples underwent 16S rRNA sequencing and a subset also underwent shotgun metagenomic sequencing. The effect of zinc supplementation on the diversity and composition of the cow and calf microbiome and resistome was assessed. Alpha and beta diversity and composition of the microbiota were significantly altered over time but not by treatment in the cows, with alpha diversity decreasing and 14 genera found at significantly higher relative abundances at calving compared to enrollment. Levels of 27 antimicrobial resistance genes significantly increased over time. Only a small number of taxa were differentially expressed at calving in treatment and control groups, including Faecalibacterium, Bacteroides, Turicibacter, and Bifidobacterium pseudolongum. No effect of the dam's treatment group was observed on the diversity or composition of the neonatal calf microbiota. The calf resistome, which was relatively rich and diverse compared to the cow, was also unaffected by the dam's treatment group. The impact of high levels of dietary zinc thus appeared to be minimal, with no observed changes in alpha or beta diversity, and few changes in the relative abundance of a small number of taxa and antimicrobial resistance genes.	2024.0	Animal microbiome	YES		METAGENOMIC	YES	Metazoa	Chordata	Artiodactyla	Bovidae	Bos	Bos taurus	10.1186/s42523-024-00326-3				WGS:112	112	1	Bos taurus		gut metagenome		MIMS Environmental/Metagenome sample from gut metagenome	112.0	https://doi.org/10.1186/s42523-024-00326-3	True	True	Bos taurus	True	gestating cow and neonatal calf	True	gestating cow and neonatal calf	False		False	ENA + paper	Fecal samples were collected from gestating cows at enrollment and calving, and from neonatal calves for 16S rRNA and shotgun metagenomic sequencing.	UNIFORM	112	METAGENOMIC:112	112	112	KEEP	yes	high	gut_explicit	multi_host	Bos taurus (gestating cow and neonatal calf)	False	agree	GUT: yes (high) — Paper title and abstract explicitly state 'gut microbiome' of gestating cow and neonatal calf. Abstract describes fecal sampling from cows and calves to study microbiota and resistome. Biome label confirms 'gut metagenome'. || BODY_SITE: gut_explicit — Biome label states 'gut metagenome'. Sample titles specify 'gut metagenome'. Abstract confirms fecal samples collected from cows and neonatal calves. No skin, oral, or other body site mentioned. || HOST: Bos taurus (gestating cow and neonatal calf) [multi_host] — Host field: Bos taurus. Study explicitly compares microbiomes of two different age/physiological groups: gestating cows (adults) and their neonatal calves. This is a genuine comparative study OF multiple host individuals representing different life stages, not an incidental trace sample. Abstract states 'aims of this study were to determine the effect of dietary zinc on the microbiota...of the gestating cow and calf.' || METHOD: agree — ENA fields show library_strategy_mix WGS:112 with 112 shotgun samples from metagenomic library source. Abstract describes 