PRJEB47976	Homo sapiens			Germany	175	WGS	pubmed	35516807.0	35516807.0	Signature of Alzheimer's Disease in Intestinal Microbiome: Results From the AlzBiom Study.	Laske C	Willmann M	Mammalia	BACKGROUND: Changes in intestinal microbiome composition have been described in animal models of Alzheimer's disease (AD) and AD patients. Here we investigated how well taxonomic and functional intestinal microbiome data and their combination with clinical data can be used to discriminate between amyloid-positive AD patients and cognitively healthy elderly controls. METHODS: In the present study we investigated intestinal microbiome in 75 amyloid-positive AD patients and 100 cognitively healthy controls participating in the AlzBiom study. We randomly split the data into a training and a validation set. Intestinal microbiome was measured using shotgun metagenomics. Receiver operating characteristic (ROC) curve analysis was performed to examine the discriminatory ability of intestinal microbiome among diagnostic groups. RESULTS: The best model for discrimination of amyloid-positive AD patients from healthy controls with taxonomic data was obtained analyzing 18 genera features, and yielded an area under the receiver operating characteristic curve (AUROC) of 0.76 in the training set and 0.61 in the validation set. The best models with functional data were obtained analyzing 17 GO (Gene Ontology) features with an AUROC of 0.81 in the training set and 0.75 in the validation set and 26 KO [Kyoto Encyclopedia of Genes and Genomes (KEGG) ortholog] features with an AUROC of 0.83 and 0.77, respectively. Using ensemble learning for these three models including a clinical model with the 4 parameters age, gender, BMI and ApoE yielded an AUROC of 0.92 in the training set and 0.80 in the validation set. DISCUSSION: In conclusion, we identified a specific Alzheimer signature in intestinal microbiome that can be used to discriminate amyloid-positive AD patients from healthy controls. The diagnostic accuracy increases from taxonomic to functional data and is even better when combining taxonomic, functional and clinical models. Intestinal microbiome represents an innovative diagnostic supplement and a promising area for developing novel interventions against AD.	2022.0	Frontiers in neuroscience	YES		METAGENOMIC	YES	Animalia	Chordata	Primates	Hominidae	Homo	sapiens	10.3389/fnins.2022.792996				WGS:175	175	0			Homo sapiens		Stool Sample from Participant #1; Stool Sample from Participant #10; Stool Sample from Participant #105; Stool Sample from Participant #106; Stool Sample from Participant #108	175.0	https://doi.org/10.3389/fnins.2022.792996	True	True	Homo sapiens	False		True	human	False		False	ENA + paper	Shotgun metagenomic sequencing was performed on intestinal microbiome samples from 75 amyloid-positive AD patients and 100 cognitively healthy elderly controls.	UNIFORM	175	METAGENOMIC:175	175	175	KEEP	yes	high	gut_explicit	single_clear	Homo sapiens	True	agree	GUT: yes (high) — Title states 'Intestinal Microbiome', abstract explicitly describes 'intestinal microbiome' measurement via 'shotgun metagenomics' in human subjects. Humans are animals; human gut microbiome studies are valid animal gut studies per instructions. || BODY_SITE: gut_explicit — Sample titles state 'Stool Sample from Participant #...' and abstract explicitly investigates 'intestinal microbiome'. Stool is a direct proxy for gut microbial composition. || HOST: Homo sapiens [single_clear] (HUMAN) — Host field: 'Homo sapiens'. Abstract states study enrolled '75 amyloid-positive AD patients and 100 cognitively healthy controls' (175 total human subjects). Single host species, multiple individual samples from distinct human participants. || METHOD: agree — Abstract states 'Intestinal microbiome was measured using shotgun metagenomics'. ENA fields confirm: library_strategy_mix is WGS (shotgun) with 175 samples; library_source is METAGENOMIC (community DNA, not single organism). Methods and metadata align. || SUMMARY: This is a well-documented human gut microbiome study using shotgun metagenomics on stool samples from 175 cognitively normal and Alzheimer's disease participants. It is a valid animal (human) gut study with explicit gut sampling and clear methodology alignment.	none					No reporting errors detected; paper and deposit metadata are fully consistent for a human stool microbiome study of Alzheimer's disease.	1		False		True	False
