PRJNA987743	Bos indicus	9915.0		Brazil	104	WGS	pubmed	38163076.0	38163076.0	Rumen and fecal microbiomes are related to diet and production traits in Bos indicus beef cattle.	Conteville LC	Regitano LCA	Mammalia	BACKGROUND: Ruminants harbor a complex microbial community within their gastrointestinal tract, which plays major roles in their health and physiology. Brazil is one of the largest producers of beef in the world and more than 90% of the beef cattle herds are composed of pure and crossbred Nelore (Bos indicus). Despite its importance to the Brazilian economy and human feeding, few studies have characterized the Nelore microbiome. Therefore, using shotgun metagenomics, we investigated the impact of diet on the composition and functionality of the Nelore microbiome, and explored the associations between specific microbial taxa and their functionality with feed efficiency and methane emission. RESULTS: The ruminal microbiome exhibited significantly higher microbial diversity, distinctive taxonomic profile and variations in microbial functionality compared to the fecal microbiome, highlighting the distinct contributions of the microbiomes of these environments. Animals subjected to different dietary treatments exhibited significant differences in their microbiomes' archaeal diversity and in the abundance of 89 genera, as well as in the functions associated with the metabolism of components of each diet. Moreover, depending on the diet, feed-efficient animals and low methane emitters displayed higher microbial diversity in their fecal microbiome. Multiple genera were associated with an increase or decrease of the phenotypes. Upon analyzing the functions attributed to these taxa, we observed significant differences on the ruminal taxa associated with feed efficient and inefficient cattle. The ruminal taxa that characterized feed efficient cattle stood out for having significantly more functions related to carbohydrate metabolism, such as monosaccharides, di-/oligosaccharides and amino acids. The taxa associated with methane emission had functions associated with methanogenesis and the production of substrates that may influence methane production, such as hydrogen and formate. CONCLUSION: Our findings highlight the significant role of diet in shaping Nelore microbiomes and how its composition and functionality may affect production traits such as feed efficiency and methane emission. These insights provide valuable support for the implementation of novel feeding and biotechnological strategies.	2023.0	Frontiers in microbiology	YES		METAGENOMIC	YES	Metazoa	Chordata	Artiodactyla	Bovidae	Bos	Bos indicus	10.3389/fmicb.2023.1282851				WGS:104	104	1	Bos indicus		gut metagenome	Feces of Animal 1460; Feces of Animal 1462; Feces of Animal 1464; Feces of Animal 1468; Feces of Animal 1476; Feces of Animal 1479; Feces of Animal 1480; Feces of Animal 1481; Feces of Animal 1485; Feces of Animal 1493; Feces of Animal 1494; Feces of Animal 1495; Feces of Animal 1496; Feces of Animal 1498; Feces of Animal 1500; Feces of Animal 1501; Feces of Animal 1502; Feces of Animal 1503; Feces of Animal 1504; Feces of Animal 238	1460_Feces; 1460_Rumen; 1462_Feces; 1462_Rumen; 1464_Feces	104.0	https://doi.org/10.3389/fmicb.2023.1282851	True	True	Bos indicus	True	Bos indicus beef cattle	True	Nelore (Bos indicus)	False		False	ENA + paper	Shotgun metagenomic sequencing was performed on rumen and fecal samples collected from Nelore beef cattle.	UNIFORM	104	METAGENOMIC:104	104	104	KEEP	yes	high	gut_explicit	single_clear	Bos indicus	False	agree	GUT: yes (high) — Abstract explicitly states 'using shotgun metagenomics, we investigated the impact of diet on the composition and functionality of the Nelore microbiome' and describes 'ruminal microbiome' and 'gastrointestinal tract' microbial communities. Biome label confirms 'gut metagenome'. || BODY_SITE: gut_explicit — Sample titles include '1460_Rumen' and '1460_Feces'; isolation sources explicitly state 'Feces of A