PRJNA1206810	feces metagenome			China:jining	9	WGS	pubmed	40635073.0	40635073.0	The development and excretion of Toxoplasma gondii oocyst manipulate the gut microbiota in its definitive host.	Zhao GH	Yin K	Mammalia	BACKGROUND: Oocysts serve as the primary source of Toxoplasma infection. Therefore, understanding oocyst development and exploring effective strategies to prevent oocyst excretion are crucial for controlling toxoplasmosis. METHODS: In this study, shotgun metagenomics was employed to characterize the functional and compositional changes in the gut microbiota of cats during oocyst development. The Spearman correlation test was utilized to analyze the correlation between differential Kyoto Encyclopedia of Genes and Genomes (KEGG) pathways and carbohydrate-active enzymes (CAZymes) in key bacteria regulating oocyst excretion. RESULTS: The results revealed that group A (sexual initiation stage) displayed a lower number of functional genes, which were restored to normal levels in group B (oocyst excretion stage), compared with group C (Toxoplasma-uninfected samples). The abundance of 39 KEGG pathways, 106 CAZymes, and 98 virulence factors (VFs) varied significantly among the three groups. The atrazine degradation pathway, associated with sexual development, was upregulated in group B. CAZymes involved in restoring the intestinal mucosal barrier and VFs related to iron metabolism, antibiotic resistance, and suppression of host immunity were enriched in group B. Sexual initiation and oocyst excretion resulted in reduced gut bacterial diversity and microbiota dysbiosis. Probiotics and bacteria related to linoleic acid (LA) uptake were dominant in both group A and group B. Bacteroides stercoris was the most significantly upregulated bacterium and could influence the expression of carbohydrate-binding modules (CBMs) and glycoside hydrolases (GHs) in group B. CONCLUSIONS: During the oocyst development/excretion stage, the function and composition of the cat gut microbiota changed significantly. In addition, Bacteroides stercoris may play a crucial role in oocyst excretion by regulating key candidates of CBMs and GHs. Our findings lay the foundation for investigating the regulatory mechanisms of oocyst excretion.	2025.0	Parasites & vectors	YES		METAGENOMIC	YES	Animalia	Chordata	Carnivora	Felidae	Felis	catus	10.1186/s13071-025-06925-6				WGS:9	9	0			feces metagenome	Laboratory grown cat feces 0d for C1; Laboratory grown cat feces 0d for C2; Laboratory grown cat feces 0d for C3; Laboratory grown cat feces 10d for B1; Laboratory grown cat feces 10d for B2; Laboratory grown cat feces 10d for B3; Laboratory grown cat feces 5d for A1; Laboratory grown cat feces 5d for A2; Laboratory grown cat feces 5d for A3	Metagenome or environmental sample from feces metagenome	9.0	https://doi.org/10.1186/s13071-025-06925-6	True	False		True	cat	True	cat	False		False	paper only	Shotgun metagenomic sequencing was performed on gut samples from cats during different stages of Toxoplasma gondii oocyst development and excretion.	NO_HOST_DATA	0	METAGENOMIC:9	9	9	KEEP	yes	high	gut_explicit	single_clear	Felis catus (cat)	False	agree	GUT: yes (high) — Paper title states 'gut microbiota in its definitive host' and abstract explicitly describes 'shotgun metagenomics was employed to characterize the functional and compositional changes in the gut microbiota of cats'. Isolation sources confirm 'Laboratory grown cat feces' samples. || BODY_SITE: gut_explicit — Title and abstract both explicitly reference 'gut microbiota'. Isolation sources specify 'cat feces', which is a gut-derived sample. Biome label states 'feces metagenome'. || HOST: Felis catus (cat) [single_clear] — Paper title and abstract unambiguously state 'its definitive host' referring to Toxoplasma gondii, which is cats. Isolation sources consistently specify 'cat feces' (samples C1, C2, C3, B, etc.). No multi-host comparison is described—this is a longitudinal study of oocyst development in a single 