ased into a wild environment. Our results clearly show that captivity has altered the giant panda microbiome, which could have unintended negative consequences on their adaptability and the ecosystem during the reintroduction of giant pandas into the wild."	2019.0	Genes	YES		METAGENOMIC	YES	Animalia	Chordata	Carnivora	Ursidae	Ailuropoda	melanoleuca	10.3390/genes10100827				WGA:61; WGS:15; AMPLICON:1	15	2	Lynx lynx; Panthera pardus		gut metagenome	gut	Metagenome or environmental sample from gut metagenome	77.0	https://doi.org/10.3390/genes10100827	True	True	Lynx lynx	True	giant panda (Ailuropoda melanoleuca)	True	giant panda (wild and captive)	False		False	ENA + paper	Fecal samples were sequenced from wild and captive giant pandas using 16S rRNA and shotgun metagenomics.	DIVERSE	77	METAGENOMIC:77	77	15	REVIEW						False		GUT:  () —  || BODY_SITE:  —  || HOST:  [] —  || METHOD:  —  || SUMMARY: PARSE_ERROR	host_mismatch; multihost_issue	paper: Ailuropoda melanoleuca (giant panda) vs deposit: Lynx lynx (Eurasian lynx); Panthera pardus (leopard)			Deposit sample-level data explicitly lists two distinct host species: Lynx lynx and Panthera pardus. The paper presents a single-host study (giant panda only).	Critical host identity error: deposit contains felid species (lynx, leopard) data while paper studies only giant pandas; additionally, multiple host species in deposit contradicts the single-species study design.	2	1.0	False		True	False
