PRJEB40824	Sus scrofa	9823.0		Poland	6	WGS	pubmed	33270717.0	33270717.0	A metagenomic glimpse into the gut of wild and domestic animals: Quantification of antimicrobial resistance and more.	Skarżyńska M	Wasyl D	Mammalia	Antimicrobial resistance (AMR) in bacteria is a complex subject, why one need to look at this phenomenon from a wider and holistic perspective. The extensive use of the same antimicrobial classes in human and veterinary medicine as well as horticulture is one of the main drivers for the AMR selection. Here, we applied shotgun metagenomics to investigate the AMR epidemiology in several animal species including farm animals, which are often exposed to antimicrobial treatment opposed to an unique set of wild animals that seems not to be subjected to antimicrobial pressure. The comparison of the domestic and wild animals allowed to investigate the possible anthropogenic impact on AMR spread. Inclusion of animals with different feeding behaviors (carnivores, omnivores) enabled to further assess which AMR genes that thrives within the food chain. We tested fecal samples not only of intensively produced chickens, turkeys, and pigs, but also of wild animals such as wild boars, red foxes, and rodents. A multi-directional approach mapping obtained sequences to several databases provided insight into the occurrence of the different AMR genes. The method applied enabled also analysis of other factors that may influence AMR of intestinal microbiome such as diet. Our findings confirmed higher levels of AMR in farm animals than in wildlife. The results also revealed the potential of wildlife in the AMR dissemination. Particularly in red foxes, we found evidence of several AMR genes conferring resistance to critically important antimicrobials like quinolones and cephalosporins. In contrast, the lowest abundance of AMR was observed in rodents originating from natural environment with presumed limited exposure to antimicrobials. Shotgun metagenomics enabled us to demonstrate that discrepancies between AMR profiles found in the intestinal microbiome of various animals probably resulted from the different antimicrobial exposure, habitats, and behavior of the tested animal species.	2020.0	PloS one	YES		METAGENOMIC	YES	Metazoa	Chordata	Artiodactyla	Suidae	Sus	Sus scrofa	10.1371/journal.pone.0242987				WGS:6	6	5	Gallus gallus; Meleagris gallopavo; Rodentia; Sus scrofa; Vulpes vulpes		metagenome	intestines content	DTU2018-1753-PRJ-metagenome-wild-1; DTU2018-1754-PRJ-metagenome-farm-2; DTU2018-1755-PRJ-metagenome-farm-3; DTU2018-1756-PRJ-metagenome-farm-4; DTU2018-1757-PRJ-metagenome-wild-5	6.0	https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0242987	True	True	Sus scrofa	False		True	chickens; turkeys; pigs; wild boars; red foxes; rodents	False		False	ENA + paper	Fecal samples were sequenced from farm animals (chickens, turkeys, pigs) and wild animals (wild boars, red foxes, rodents).	DIVERSE	6	METAGENOMIC:6	6	6	KEEP	yes	high	gut_explicit	multi_host	Sus scrofa, Gallus gallus, Meleagris gallopavo, Rodentia, Vulpes vulpes	False	agree	GUT: yes (high) — Title states 'gut of wild and domestic animals'; abstract explicitly investigates 'AMR epidemiology in several animal species'; isolation_source field states 'intestines content'; library_source is METAGENOMIC (community DNA, not host genome). || BODY_SITE: gut_explicit — Isolation source explicitly stated as 'intestines content'; paper title and abstract both reference 'gut'; no non-gut body sites mentioned. || HOST: Sus scrofa, Gallus gallus, Meleagris gallopavo, Rodentia, Vulpes vulpes [multi_host] — Host field lists Sus scrofa; however, Host species in samples field shows 5 distinct species (Gallus gallus, Meleagris gallopavo, Rodentia, Sus scrofa, Vulpes vulpes). Abstract confirms 'several animal species' including 'farm animals' and 'wild animals' with different feeding behaviors (carnivores, omnivores). This is a genuine comparative study across multiple host species, not an incidental multi-host survey. || METHOD: agree — Paper states 'applied shotgun metagenomics'; ENA library_strategy_mix shows WGS:6 (6 WGS/shotgun samples); library_source is METAGENOMIC, consistent with community DNA from gut samples. || SUMMARY: This is a legitimate animal gut metagenomics study comparing antimicrobial resistance across five distinct animal host species (both domestic and wild) using shotgun WGS of intestinal content. The multi-host design is a deliberate comparative framework, not incidental sampling.	multihost_issue				Deposit sample-level data explicitly lists 5 distinct host species (Gallus gallus, Meleagris gallopavo, Rodentia, Sus scrofa, Vulpes vulpes) while paper treats them as a comparative set of multiple animal species investigated together.	The study samples multiple distinct host species (5 confirmed in deposit); this is appropriate and transparent in both sources, but constitutes a legitimate multihost_issue per the strict protocol.	1		False		True	True
