PRJEB66439	Canis lupus familiaris	9615.0	Intestines	Norway	472	WGS	pubmed	38481663.0	38481663.0	Defining a metagenomic threshold for detecting low abundances of Providencia alcalifaciens in canine faecal samples.	Aardal AM	Llarena AK	Mammalia	INTRODUCTION: Acute haemorrhagic diarrhoea syndrome (AHDS) in dogs is a condition of unknown aetiology. Providencia alcalifaciens is suspected to play a role in the disease as it was commonly found in dogs suffering from AHDS during a Norwegian outbreak in 2019. The role of this bacterium as a constituent of the canine gut microbiota is unknown, hence this study set out to investigate its occurrence in healthy dogs using metagenomics. MATERIALS AND METHODS: To decrease the likelihood of false detection, we established a metagenomic threshold for P. alcalifaciens by spiking culture-negative stool samples with a range of bacterial dilutions and analysing these by qPCR and shotgun metagenomics. The detection limit for P. alcalifaciens was determined and used to establish a metagenomic threshold. The threshold was validated on naturally contaminated faecal samples with known cultivation status for P. alcalifaciens. Finally, the metagenomic threshold was used to determine the occurrence of P. alcalifaciens in shotgun metagenomic datasets from canine faecal samples (n=362) collected in the HUNT One Health project. RESULTS: The metagenomic assay and qPCR had a detection limit of 1.1x103 CFU P. alcalifaciens per faecal sample, which corresponded to a Cq value of 31.4 and 569 unique k-mer counts by shotgun metagenomics. Applying this metagenomic threshold to 362 faecal metagenomic datasets from healthy dogs, P. alcalifaciens was found in only 1.1% (95% CI [0.0, 6.8]) of the samples, and then in low relative abundances (median: 0.04%; range: 0.00 to 0.81%). The sensitivity of the qPCR and shotgun metagenomics assay was low, as only 40% of culture-positive samples were also positive by qPCR and metagenomics. DISCUSSION: Using our detection limit, the occurrence of P. alcalifaciens in faecal samples from healthy dogs was low. Given the low sensitivity of the metagenomic assay, these results do not rule out a significantly higher occurrence of this bacterium at a lower abundance.	2024.0	Frontiers in cellular and infection microbiology	YES		METAGENOMIC	YES	Metazoa	Chordata	Carnivora	Canidae	Canis	Canis lupus	10.3389/fcimb.2024.1305742				WGS:472	472	3	Blank sample;blank sample; Canis lupus familiaris; Mock community;synthetic metagenome	Intestines	Canis lupus familiaris; blank sample; synthetic metagenome		Blank_sample_DNA_H1H_10_G6; Blank_sample_DNA_H1H_11_G6; Blank_sample_DNA_H1H_12_G6; Blank_sample_DNA_H1H_13_G6; Blank_sample_DNA_H1H_14_G6	472.0	https://doi.org/10.3389/fcimb.2024.1305742	True	True	Canis lupus familiaris	True	dog	True	dog	False		False	ENA + paper	Sequenced samples were faecal (stool) samples collected from canine subjects, including healthy dogs from the HUNT One Health project and dogs with acute haemorrhagic diarrhoea syndrome.	DIVERSE	426	METAGENOMIC:472	472	472	KEEP	yes	high	gut_explicit	single_clear	Canis lupus familiaris	False	agree	GUT: yes (high) — Paper title explicitly states 'canine faecal samples'; abstract states 'stool samples' from dogs; body_site field lists 'Intestines'; library_source is METAGENOMIC (community DNA). This is a genuine animal gut microbiome study using shotgun metagenomics. || BODY_SITE: gut_explicit — Body site field explicitly states 'Intestines'; paper title mentions 'faecal samples'; abstract mentions 'stool samples'. Gut origin is unambiguous. || HOST: Canis lupus familiaris [single_clear] — Host field clearly states 'Canis lupus familiaris' (domestic dog). Paper title and abstract both focus on canine samples. While sample list includes blank samples and mock communities, the biological samples are from a single host species (healthy dogs in a comparative study of AHDS). This is a genuine comparative study OF dog gut microbiota, not an incidenta