PRJNA1218713	Sus scrofa	9823.0		Spain	172	WGS	pubmed	41618383.0	41618383.0	From predisposition to recovery: field evidence of interactions between the gut microbiota and Brachyspira hyodysenteriae infection.	Pérez-Pérez L	Carvajal A	Mammalia	Restrictions on antibiotics use have increased interest in the gut microbiota relationship to host health, particularly in enteric infections. The present field study, performed on two farms with endemic swine dysentery (SD) infection, characterises the faecal microbiota in 102 faecal samples from 13 diseased and 13 non-diseased pigs by shotgun metagenomic sequencing. The samples were collected during four samplings, which allowed us to monitor the animals before, during and after the clinical disease to investigate the role of the gut microbiota in disease outcome, assess the impact of infection on microbial composition and evaluate the microbiota evolution following recovery. Samples collected before disease demonstrated that SD susceptible pigs had lower microbial diversity, with significantly lower abundance of Treponema rectale, Prevotella spp. or Ruminiclostridium E compared with SD resistant pigs, which remained healthy. Marked alterations in microbial species composition and their functional profiles were evident during clinical disease. Brachyspira hyodysenteriae, Dysosmobacter sp. BX15, Acetivibrio ethanolgignens and Mucispirillum sp. 910586745 were significantly increased in abundance, which was associated with an increase of functions such as Bacteroides capsular polysaccharide transcription antitermination proteins or pterin carbinolamine dehydratase. No changes in the microbiota were observed after the disease when compared with non-diseased pigs, thus evidencing a restoration of the microbiota composition after therapeutic treatment and recovery. The study demonstrates that the microbiota may play a relevant role in SD disease outcome and evidences the changes that occur during clinical disease do not persist over time after pig therapeutic treatment.	2026.0	Veterinary research	YES		METAGENOMIC	YES	Metazoa	Chordata	Artiodactyla	Suidae	Sus	Sus scrofa	10.1186/s13567-025-01646-1				WGS:172	172	1	Sus scrofa		pig gut metagenome		MIMS Environmental/Metagenome sample from pig gut metagenome	172.0	https://doi.org/10.1186/s13567-025-01646-1	True	True	Sus scrofa	False		True	pig	False		False	ENA + paper	Metagenomic sequencing was performed on 102 faecal samples collected from 13 diseased and 13 non-diseased pigs on two farms.	UNIFORM	172	METAGENOMIC:172	172	172	KEEP	yes	high	gut_explicit	single_clear	Sus scrofa	False	agree	GUT: yes (high) — Paper title explicitly states 'gut microbiota' relationship study; abstract describes 'faecal microbiota in 102 faecal samples from 13 diseased and 13 non-diseased pigs by shotgun metagenomic sequencing'; biome label 'pig gut metagenome'; sample titles specify 'pig gut metagenome' || BODY_SITE: gut_explicit — Abstract explicitly states 'faecal samples' and 'faecal microbiota'; biome label 'pig gut metagenome'; sample titles state 'pig gut metagenome'; body site field empty but isolation source and sample metadata unambiguously reference gut origin || HOST: Sus scrofa [single_clear] — Host field explicitly states 'Sus scrofa'; host species in samples field confirms '1 distinct' species 'Sus scrofa'; abstract refers to 'pigs' (n=26 individual animals: 13 diseased, 13 non-diseased); consistent across title and biome labels || METHOD: agree — Abstract states 'shotgun metagenomic sequencing'; ENA library_strategy_mix field shows 'WGS:172' with 172 WGS samples; library_source 'METAGENOMIC' (community DNA) aligns with metagenomic approach; no contradiction detected || SUMMARY: This is a genuine, well-documented animal gut microbiome study using WGS shotgun metagenomics on pig faecal samples collected longitudinally across disease states. Single clear host (Sus scrofa), explicit gut/faecal sampling, and methods align perfectly with ENA metadata.	none					No