PRJNA1123238	Canis lupus familiaris	9615.0		USA:Ohio,Columbus	67	WGS	pubmed	39149494.0	39149494.0	Evaluating urine volume and host depletion methods to enable genome-resolved metagenomics of the urobiome.	Lewis ZJ	Hale VL	Mammalia	BACKGROUND: The gut microbiome has emerged as a clear player in health and disease, in part by mediating host response to environment and lifestyle. The urobiome (microbiota of the urinary tract) likely functions similarly. However, efforts to characterize the urobiome and assess its functional potential have been limited due to technical challenges including low microbial biomass and high host cell shedding in urine. Here, to begin addressing these challenges, we evaluate urine sample volume (100 ml - 5 mL), and host DNA depletion methods and their effects on urobiome profiles in healthy dogs, which are a robust large animal model for the human urobiome. We collected urine from seven dogs and fractionated samples into aliquots. One set of samples was spiked with host (canine) cells to model a biologically relevant host cell burden in urine. Samples then underwent DNA extraction followed by 16S rRNA gene and shotgun metagenomic sequencing. We then assembled metagenome assembled genomes (MAGs) and compared microbial composition and diversity across groups. We tested six methods of DNA extraction: QIAamp BiOstic Bacteremia (no host depletion), QIAamp DNA Microbiome, Molzym MolYsis, NEBNext Microbiome DNA Enrichment, Zymo HostZERO, and Propidium Monoazide. RESULTS: In relation to urine sample volume, 3 3.0 mL resulted in the most consistent urobiome profiling. In relation to host depletion, individual (dog) but not extraction method drove overall differences in microbial composition. DNA Microbiome yielded the greatest microbial diversity in 16S rRNA sequencing data and shotgun metagenomic sequencing data, and maximized MAG recovery while effectively depleting host DNA in host-spiked urine samples. As proof-of-principle, we then mined MAGs for core metabolic functions and environmental chemical metabolism. We identified long chain alkane utilization in two of the urine MAGs. Long chain alkanes are common pollutants that result from industrial combustion processes and end up in urine. CONCLUSIONS: This is the first study, to our knowledge, to demonstrate environmental chemical degradation potential in urine microbes through genome-resolved metagenomics. These findings provide guidelines for studying the urobiome in relation to sample volume and host depletion, and lay the foundation for future evaluation of urobiome function in relation to health and disease.	2024.0	Research square	YES		METAGENOMIC	YES	Metazoa	Chordata	Carnivora	Canidae	Canis	Canis lupus	10.21203/rs.3.rs-4688526/v1				WGS:67	67	2	Canis lupus familiaris; Homo sapiens		canine metagenome; synthetic metagenome	neg.control; pos.control; urine	Metagenome or environmental sample from canine metagenome; Metagenome or environmental sample from synthetic metagenome	67.0	https://doi.org/10.21203/rs.3.rs-4688526/v1	True	True	Canis lupus familiaris	False		True	dog	False		False	ENA + paper	Sequenced samples came from urine collected from seven healthy dogs.	DIVERSE	58	METAGENOMIC:67	67	67	EXCLUDE	no	high	non_gut	multi_host	Canis lupus familiaris (primary); Homo sapiens (secondary reference)	False	agree	GUT: no (high) — Paper title states 'urobiome' (urinary tract microbiota), not gut. Abstract explicitly: 'The urobiome (microbiota of the urinary tract)' and 'we collected urine from seven dogs'. Isolation source confirms 'urine', not fecal or intestinal material. This is a urinary tract study, not a gut microbiome study. || BODY_SITE: non_gut — Title and abstract clearly focus on urine and urobiome. Isolation source field lists 'urine' as the primary sample source. No gut/fecal samples mentioned. || HOST: Canis lupus familiaris (primary); Homo sapiens (secondary reference) [multi_host] — Host field declares 'Canis lupus familiaris'. Host species in samples lists two distinct species: 'Canis lupus familiaris; Homo sapiens'. The abstract states samples were 'spiked with host (canine) cells' for methodology and mentions dogs 'as a robust large animal model for the human urobiome', indicating humans are a comparative reference, not primary subjects. Canis lupus familiaris is the primary experimental host. || METHOD: agree — Paper describes WGS/shotgun metagenomics of urine samples ('genome-resolved metagenomics'). ENA library_strategy_mix shows WGS:67 (all 67 samples are WGS). library_source is METAGENOMIC, consistent with community DNA from urine. Methods align with declared sequencing approach. || SUMMARY: This is a legitimate animal metagenomics study (dogs as primary host), but the body site is urinary tract, not gut, making it ineligible for a gut-specific microbiome catalog. The multi-host classification reflects the use of canine urine with spiked canine cells and human reference context, but Canis lupus familiaris remains the primary experimental organism.	none					No genuine reporting errors detected. Paper and deposit are consistent: dogs (Canis lupus familiaris) are the correct host for a urobiome study, urine is the appropriate sample type, and synthetic controls explain the secondary human reference in metadata.	1		False		True	False
PRJEB66438	Canis lupus familiaris	9615.0	Intestines	Norway	33	WGS	pubmed	38481663.0	38481663.0	Defining a metagenomic threshold for detecting low abundances of Providencia alcalifaciens in canine faecal samples.	Aardal AM	Llarena AK	Mammalia	INTRODUCTION: Acute haemorrhagic diarrhoea syndrome (AHDS) in dogs is a condition of unknown aetiology. Providencia alcalifaciens is suspected to play a role in the disease as it was commonly found in dogs suffering from AHDS during a Norwegian outbreak in 2019. The role of this bacterium as a constituent of the canine gut microbiota is unknown, hence this study set out to investigate its occurrence in healthy dogs using metagenomics. MATERIALS AND METHODS: To decrease the likelihood of false detection, we established a metagenomic threshold for P. alcalifaciens by spiking culture-negative stool samples with a range of bacterial dilutions and analysing these by qPCR and shotgun metagenomics. The detection limit for P. alcalifaciens was determined and used to establish a metagenomic threshold. The threshold was validated on naturally contaminated faecal samples with known cultivation status for P. alcalifaciens. Finally, the metagenomic threshold was used to determine the occurrence of P. alcalifaciens in shotgun metagenomic datasets from canine faecal samples (n=362) collected in the HUNT One Health project. RESULTS: The metagenomic assay and qPCR had a detection limit of 1.1x103 CFU P. alcalifaciens per faecal sample, which corresponded to a Cq value of 31.4 and 569 unique k-mer counts by shotgun metagenomics. Applying this metagenomic threshold to 362 faecal metagenomic datasets from healthy dogs, P. alcalifaciens was found in only 1.1% (95% CI [0.0, 6.8]) of the samples, and then in low relative abundances (median: 0.04%; range: 0.00 to 0.81%). The sensitivity of the qPCR and shotgun metagenomics assay was low, as only 40% of culture-positive samples were also positive by qPCR and metagenomics. DISCUSSION: Using our detection limit, the occurrence of P. alcalifaciens in faecal samples from healthy dogs was low. Given the low sensitivity of the metagenomic assay, these results do not rule out a significantly higher occurrence of this bacterium at a lower abundance.	2024.0	Frontiers in cellular and infection microbiology	YES		METAGENOMIC	YES	Metazoa	Chordata	Carnivora	Canidae	Canis	Canis lupus	10.3389/fcimb.2024.1305742				WGS:33	33	1	Canis lupus familiaris	Intestines	Canis lupus familiaris		Dog 1_sample_49_H1; Dog 1_sample_50_H1; Dog 2_sample_51_H2; Dog 2_sample_52_H2; Dog 2_sample_53_H2	33.0	https://doi.org/10.3389/fcimb.2024.1305742	True	True	Canis lupus familiaris	True	dog	True	dog	False		False	ENA + paper	Faecal samples were collected from healthy dogs in the HUNT One Health project and subjected to shotgun metagenomics sequencing.	UNIFORM	33	METAGENOMIC:33	33	33	KEEP	yes	high	gut_explicit	single_clear	Canis lupus familiaris	False	agree	GUT: yes (high) — Paper title explicitly states 'canine faecal samples'; abstract describes investigation of P. alcalifaciens 'in healthy dogs using metagenomics' on stool samples; body_site field lists 'Intestines'; library_source is 'METAGENOMIC' indicating community DNA from gut microbiota. || BODY_SITE: gut_explicit — Body site field explicitly states 'Intestines'; paper title and abstract refer to 'faecal samples' and 'stool samples'; sample titles reference Dog samples with no alternative body site indicators. || HOST: Canis lupus familiaris [single_clear] — Host field states 'Canis lupus familiaris'; host species in samples confirms '1 distinct' species; sample titles identify individual dogs ('Dog 1', 'Dog 2') but all belong to the same species; biome label confirms 'Canis lupus familiaris'. || METHOD: agree — Paper describes shotgun metagenomics methodology; ENA library_strategy_mix shows 'WGS:33' with 33 WGS/shotgun samples; library_source 'METAGENOMIC' aligns with community DNA approach described in abstract; method of spiking and sequencin