PRJEB35362					17	WGS	pubmed	32337106.0	32337106.0	CoproID predicts the source of coprolites and paleofeces using microbiome composition and host DNA content.	Borry M	Warinner C	Mammalia	Shotgun metagenomics applied to archaeological feces (paleofeces) can bring new insights into the composition and functions of human and animal gut microbiota from the past. However, paleofeces often undergo physical distortions in archaeological sediments, making their source species difficult to identify on the basis of fecal morphology or microscopic features alone. Here we present a reproducible and scalable pipeline using both host and microbial DNA to infer the host source of fecal material. We apply this pipeline to newly sequenced archaeological specimens and show that we are able to distinguish morphologically similar human and canine paleofeces, as well as non-fecal sediments, from a range of archaeological contexts.	2020.0	PeerJ	YES		METAGENOMIC	YES	Animalia	Chordata	Primates	Hominidae	Homo	sapiens	10.7717/peerj.9001				WGS:17	17	0			gut metagenome; human gut metagenome; soil metagenome		AP3-C197S163; AP4-A6-2860; AP5-798-162; AP6-LPZ702; AP7-6-28353	17.0	https://doi.org/10.7717/peerj.9001	True	False		False		True	human; canine	False		False	human-by-biome-label	Sequenced samples are ancient paleofeces (coprolites) from archaeological contexts, sourced from human and canine origins.	NO_HOST_DATA	0	METAGENOMIC:17	17	17	KEEP	yes	high	gut_explicit	multi_host	human and canine (dog)	True	agree	GUT: yes (high) — Abstract explicitly states 'Shotgun metagenomics applied to archaeological feces (paleofeces)' and describes 'human and animal gut microbiota from the past.' Biome labels include 'gut metagenome' and 'human gut metagenome.' Samples are paleofeces (ancient fecal material), which are gut-derived. || BODY_SITE: gut_explicit — Title and abstract repeatedly reference 'coprolites and paleofeces' (fecal material). Abstract states 'to infer the host source of fecal material' and mentions 'human and canine paleofeces.' Biome labels explicitly include 'gut metagenome.' || HOST: human and canine (dog) [multi_host] (HUMAN) — Abstract states: 'we are able to distinguish morphologically similar human and canine paleofeces, as well as non-fecal sediments, from a range of archaeological contexts.' This is a comparative study specifically designed to differentiate between human and animal (canine) sources. The study includes both human and non-human animal gut samples. || METHOD: agree — ENA field shows library_strategy_mix: WGS:17 and library_source: METAGENOMIC, which aligns with abstract statement 'Shotgun metagenomics applied to archaeological feces' using 'host and microbial DNA.' || SUMMARY: This is a legitimate multi-host comparative gut microbiome study using shotgun WGS metagenomics to profile paleofecal samples from both human and canine (dog) sources, with explicit gut body-site designation and metagenomic sequencing methodology.	host_missing; gut_sample_issue; multihost_issue		paper names: human and canine (dog); deposit blank/generic	Biome labels include 'soil metagenome' alongside 'gut metagenome' and 'human gut metagenome'. The abstract mentions 'non-fecal sediments' as a control/comparison category that the pipeline distinguishes from fecal material.	Biome labels explicitly include both 'human gut metagenome' and (implied by 'canine paleofeces' in paper) separate host species. Samples with biome='gut metagenome' (generic) and 'human gut metagenome' suggest at least human origin, and paper states canine samples are also present and distinguishable, yet deposit lacks explicit canine host labeling.	The study has multiple reporting gaps: host species information is missing from the deposit despite being central to the paper's analysis (human and canine paleofeces distinguished), the deposit includes soil metagenome samples (non-gut controls), and the multihost nature is not clearly reflected in deposit host-species annotation.	2	0.0	Fals