PRJNA1127265	Rattus norvegicus	10116.0		New Zealand: Palmerston North	48	WGS	pubmed	40495477.0	40495477.0	Social Isolation Induces Sex-Specific Differences in Behavior and Gut Microbiota Composition in Stress-Sensitive Rats.	Hurst C	Dalziel JE	Mammalia	BACKGROUND: Social isolation (SI) is an established rat model of chronic stress. We applied this to the stress-sensitive Wistar Kyoto (WKY) strain to explore brain-to-gut interactions associated with mood. Whether SI stress-induced behavioral changes are sex-specific or if they affect the microbiome in WKY is unknown. We hypothesized individually housed (IH) animals would be more anxious than pair-housed (PH), with sex differences. Male and female rats were either IH or PH from 70 to 112 days old and behavior was assessed in modified open field (OFTmod), elevated plus maze (EPM), and novel object recognition (NOR) tests. Cecal content DNA was analyzed by shotgun metagenome sequencing. RESULTS: IH rats, particularly females, spent more time in the center of the OFTmod where the semi-novel feed was presented compared to PH group rats. There was a tendency for greater distance traveled, or potential hyperactivity, in IH female rats. Males stayed in the EPM closed arms more than females. No treatment difference occurred for recognition memory. SI altered cecal microbiome composition in females where housing was associated with seven differentially abundant taxa and 49 differentially abundant KEGG Level 3 ortholog/gene categories. Several relationships were noted between behavioral traits and relative abundance of microbiome taxa. There was a greater shift in female microbiome composition. CONCLUSIONS: In summary, behavioral responses to the housing treatment were minimal. IH animals, particularly females, spent more time in the center of an OFT that contained food; this may have been an indication of depression, as opposed to anxiety. Housing status had a differential impact on the microbiome for females compared to males. The associations between cecal microbiota and activity in the modified OFT suggest that dietary interventions that influence the relative abundance of Bifidobacteria, Alistipes, and Muribaculaceae should be explored.	2025.0	Brain and behavior	YES		METAGENOMIC	YES	Metazoa	Chordata	Rodentia	Muridae	Rattus	Rattus norvegicus	10.1002/brb3.70621				WGS:48	48	1	Rattus norvegicus		gut metagenome		Metagenome or environmental sample from gut metagenome	48.0	https://doi.org/10.1002/brb3.70621	True	True	Rattus norvegicus	True	rat	True	rat (Wistar Kyoto strain)	True	chronic stress	False	ENA + paper	[model of chronic stress] Cecal content DNA from individually housed and pair-housed male and female rats was analyzed by shotgun metagenome sequencing.	UNIFORM	48	METAGENOMIC:48	48	48	KEEP	yes	high	gut_explicit	single_clear	Rattus norvegicus	False	agree	GUT: yes (high) — Abstract explicitly states 'Cecal content DNA was analyzed by shotgun metagenome sequencing' and biome label is 'gut metagenome'. Cecal content is unambiguously gut material. Library strategy is WGS (shotgun), library source is METAGENOMIC (community DNA, not single organism). || BODY_SITE: gut_explicit — Paper title includes 'Gut Microbiota Composition'. Abstract specifies 'Cecal content DNA was analyzed'. Sample titles state 'from gut metagenome'. Biome label explicitly 'gut metagenome'. || HOST: Rattus norvegicus [single_clear] — Host field explicitly states 'Rattus norvegicus'. Host species in samples confirms '1 distinct' species: Rattus norvegicus. Abstract identifies study subjects as male and female rats of stress-sensitive Wistar Kyoto (WKY) strain, which is a strain of Rattus norvegicus. All 48 WGS samples are from this single host species. || METHOD: agree — Abstract states 'Cecal content DNA was analyzed by shotgun metagenome sequencing'. ENA fields confirm: library_strategy_mix = WGS (shotgun metagenome sequencing), library_source = METAGENOMIC (appropriate for community DNA from gut content), sample c