PRJNA1061804	Gallus gallus domesticus			USA: Arkansas	41	WGS	pubmed	39983259.0	39983259.0	Heat stress in chickens induces temporal changes in the cecal microbiome concomitant with host enteric serotonin responses.	Lyte JM	Lyte M	Aves	Heat stress is a potent modulator of the avian neuroendocrine system with concomitant impact on the gut microbiome. As an interkingdom signaling molecule, serotonin is largely derived from the gut and found in large concentrations in the avian gut lumen. Despite the role of serotonin in animal stress physiology and related host-microbe interactions, whether heat stress alters avian enteric concentrations of serotonin is unknown. As such, the present study sought to determine whether acute or chronic exposure to moderate heat stress alters both enteric serotonin concentrations and the microbiome in the chicken gut. Chickens were, or were not, subjected to an acute (1 day), repeated acute (2 days) or chronic (6 days) moderate ambient cyclic heat stress (12h per day, 31°C). Enteric concentrations of serotonin were significantly decreased in the acute heat stress group (P < 0.05), and rebounded to become elevated in the chronic heat stress group (P < 0.05). Shotgun metagenomic sequencing revealed heat stress caused both functional and taxonomic changes in the cecal microbiome. Abundances of bacterial taxa that are known to interact with the host via the serotonergic system, including Lactobacillus spp., and Bifidobacterium spp., were significantly (P < 0.05) altered by heat stress. As these findings demonstrate that heat stress can alter serotonin concentrations in the chicken intestinal tract, with distinct outcomes depending on duration of the stressor, serotonergic signaling may serve as potential leverageable point of intervention in host-microbe interactions including foodborne pathogen colonization in the chicken gut. In addition, this study provides novel insight into the impact of acute and chronic heat stress on the avian microbiome, and its relationship to stress-driven changes in the enteric serotonergic system.	2025.0	Poultry science	YES		METAGENOMIC	YES	Animalia	Chordata	Galliformes	Phasianidae	Gallus	gallus	10.1016/j.psj.2025.104886				WGS:41	41	0			chicken gut metagenome; synthetic metagenome	American Type Culture Collection (ATCC); Broiler chicken ceca luminal content	Metagenome or environmental sample from chicken gut metagenome; Metagenome or environmental sample from synthetic metagenome	41.0	https://doi.org/10.1016/j.psj.2025.104886	True	True	Gallus gallus domesticus	True	chicken	True	chicken	False		False	ENA + paper	Shotgun metagenomic sequencing was performed on cecal samples from chickens subjected to various heat stress conditions.	UNIFORM	40	METAGENOMIC:41	41	41	KEEP	yes	high	gut_explicit	single_clear	Gallus gallus domesticus	False	agree	GUT: yes (high) — Paper title explicitly states 'cecal microbiome' in chickens; abstract describes 'chicken gut' and 'avian gut lumen'; isolation source lists 'Broiler chicken ceca luminal content'; biome label includes 'chicken gut metagenome'; library_source is METAGENOMIC (community DNA, not single organism). || BODY_SITE: gut_explicit — Title specifies 'cecal microbiome'; abstract repeatedly references 'chicken gut', 'gut microbiome', 'gut lumen'; isolation source explicitly states 'ceca luminal content' (ceca are part of the avian gut); biome label is 'chicken gut metagenome'. || HOST: Gallus gallus domesticus [single_clear] — Host field clearly states 'Gallus gallus domesticus' (domestic chicken); paper title and abstract consistently reference 'chickens' as the experimental animal; isolation source specifies 'Broiler chicken ceca'; no mention of other host species. || METHOD: agree — ENA fields show library_strategy_mix WGS:41 (41 WGS samples), library_source METAGENOMIC (shotgun metagenomics of community DNA), which aligns with the paper's stated approach of shotgun metagenomics ('WGS metagenomics studies') analyzing cecal microbiome c