BACKGROUND: Bacteria use invertible genetic elements known as invertons to 
generate heterogeneity among a population and adapt to new and changing 
environments. In human gut bacteria, invertons are often found near genes 
associated with cell surface modifications, suggesting key roles in modulating 
dynamic processes such as surface adhesion and intestinal colonization. However, 
comprehensive testing of this hypothesis across complex bacterial communities 
like the human gut microbiome remains challenging. Metagenomic sequencing holds 
promise for detecting inversions without isolation and culturing, but ambiguity 
in read alignment limits the accuracy of the resulting inverton predictions.
RESULTS: Here, we developed a customized bioinformatic workflow-PhaseFinderDC-to 
identify and track invertons in metagenomic data. Applying this method to a 
defined yet complex gut community (hCom2) across different growth environments 
over time using both in vitro and in vivo metagenomic samples, we detected 
invertons in most hCom2 strains. These include invertons whose orientation 
probabilities change over time and are statistically associated with 
environmental conditions. We used motif enrichment to identify putative inverton 
promoters and predict genes regulated by inverton flipping during intestinal 
colonization and surface adhesion. Analysis of inverton-proximal genes also 
revealed candidate invertases that may regulate flipping of specific invertons.
CONCLUSIONS: Collectively, these findings suggest that surface adhesion and 
intestinal colonization in complex gut communities directly modulate inverton 
dynamics, offering new insights into the genetic mechanisms underlying these 
processes. Video Abstract.

