PRJNA1253095	Nyctalus noctula	51300.0		Russia: Rostov region	10	WGS	pubmed_postdoc	40649719.0	40649719.0	Metagenomic Investigation of Intestinal Microbiota of Insectivorous Synanthropic Bats: Densoviruses, Antibiotic Resistance Genes, and Functional Profiling of Gut Microbial Communities.	Popov IV	Popov IV	Mammalia	Bats serve as key ecological reservoirs of diverse microbial communities, including emerging viruses and antibiotic resistance genes. This study investigates the intestinal microbiota of two insectivorous bat species, Nyctalus noctula and Vespertilio murinus, at the Rostov Bat Rehabilitation Center in Southern Russia using whole metagenome shotgun sequencing. We analyzed taxonomic composition, functional pathways, antibiotic resistance genes, and virulence factors. Densoviruses, especially those closely related to Parus major densovirus, were the most dominant viral sequences identified. Metagenome-assembled densovirus genomes showed high sequence similarity with structural variations and clustered phylogenomically with viruses from mealworms and birds, reflecting both dietary origins and the potential for vertebrate infection. Functional profiling revealed microbial pathways associated with cell wall biosynthesis, energy metabolism, and biofilm formation. A total of 510 antibiotic resistance genes, representing 142 unique types, mainly efflux pumps and β-lactamases, were identified. Additionally, 870 virulence factor genes were detected, with a conserved set of iron acquisition systems and stress response regulators across all samples. These findings highlight the ecological complexity of bat-associated microbiota and viromes and suggest that synanthropic bats may contribute to the circulation of insect-associated viruses and antimicrobial resistance in urban settings.	2025.0	International journal of molecular sciences	YES		METAGENOMIC	YES	Metazoa	Chordata	Chiroptera	Vespertilionidae	Nyctalus	Nyctalus noctula	10.3390/ijms26135941				WGS:10	10	2	Nyctalus noctula; Vespertilio murinus		bat gut metagenome	feces	Metagenome or environmental sample from bat gut metagenome	10.0	https://doi.org/10.3390/ijms26135941	True	True	Nyctalus noctula	True	insectivorous bats	True	Nyctalus noctula; Vespertilio murinus	False		False	ENA + paper	Whole metagenome shotgun sequencing of intestinal samples from two insectivorous bat species at a rehabilitation center in Russia.	DIVERSE	10	METAGENOMIC:10	10	10	KEEP	yes	high	gut_explicit	multi_host	Nyctalus noctula; Vespertilio murinus	False	agree	GUT: yes (high) — Paper title explicitly states 'Intestinal Microbiota'; abstract confirms 'investigates the intestinal microbiota' of bat species; biome label states 'bat gut metagenome'; isolation source listed as 'feces' || BODY_SITE: gut_explicit — Title states 'Intestinal Microbiota'; abstract repeatedly refers to 'intestinal microbiota'; biome label: 'bat gut metagenome'; isolation source: 'feces'; sample titles reference 'bat gut metagenome' || HOST: Nyctalus noctula; Vespertilio murinus [multi_host] — Abstract explicitly states study 'investigates the intestinal microbiota of two insectivorous bat species, Nyctalus noctula and Vespertilio murinus'; Host species in samples field confirms '2 distinct' species; this is a genuine comparative study OF multiple animal hosts' guts, not an incidental survey || METHOD: agree — Paper describes 'whole metagenome shotgun sequencing'; library_strategy_mix confirms 'WGS:10' with 10 WGS samples; library_source confirms 'METAGENOMIC' (community DNA); method and ENA fields are consistent || SUMMARY: This is a rigorous multi-host comparative study of bat gut microbiomes using WGS, with two distinct insectivorous bat species sampled via fecal metagenomics. Title, abstract, host data, and ENA metadata are fully aligned.	none					No reporting errors detected; paper and deposit metadata are consistent for host species, sample type (gut/fecal), and study design.	1		False		True	True
PRJNA894343	Sus scrofa	9823.0		Ireland: County Cork	60	WGA	pubmed_postdoc	38384302.0	38384302.0	Fine-tuning of post-weaning pig microbiome structure and functionality by in-feed zinc oxide and antibiotics use.	Ortiz Sanjuán JM	Argüello H	Mammalia	INTRODUCTION: Post-weaning diarrhoea (PWD) is a multifactorial disease that affects piglets after weaning, contributing to productive and economic losses. Its control includes the use of in-feed prophylactic antibiotics and therapeutic zinc oxide (ZnO), treatments that, since 2022, are no longer permitted in the European Union due to spread of antimicrobial resistance genes and pollution of soil with heavy metals. A dysbiosis in the microbiota has been suggested as a potential risk factor of PWD onset. Understanding pig's microbiota development around weaning and its changes in response to ZnO and antibiotics is crucial to develop feasible alternatives to prophylactic and metaphylactic antimicrobial use. METHODS: This study used shotgun metagenomic sequencing to investigate the environmental and faecal microbiota on 10 farms using (Treated) or not using (ZnO-free) in-feed antibiotics and ZnO during the first 14 days post-weaning (dpw). Environmental samples from clean pens were collected at weaning day (0dpw), and faecal samples at 0, 7 and 14dpw. Diarrhoeic faecal samples were collected at 7dpw when available. RESULTS: The analysis of data revealed that the faecal microbiota composition and its functionality was impacted by the sampling time point (microbiota maturation after weaning) but not by the farm environment. Treatment with antibiotics and ZnO showed no effects on diversity indices while the analyses of microbiota taxonomic and functional profiles revealed increased abundance of taxa and metabolic functions associated with Phascolarctobacterium succinatutens or different species of Prevotella spp. on the Treated farms, and with Megasphaera elsdenii and Escherichia coli on the ZnO-free farms. The analysis of diarrhoea samples revealed that the treatment favoured the microbiota transition or maturation from 0dpw to 14dpw in Treated farms, resembling the composition of healthy animals, when compared to diarrhoea from ZnO-free farms, which were linked in composition to 0dpw samples. DISCUSSION: The results provide a comprehensive overview of the beneficial effects of ZnO and antibiotics in PWD in the microbiota transition after weaning, preventing the overgrowth of pathogens such as pathogenic E. coli and revealing the key aspects in microbiota maturation that antibiotics or ZnO alternatives should fulfil.	2024.0	Frontiers in cellular and infection microbiology	YES		METAGENOMIC	YES	Metazoa	Chordata	Artiodactyla	Suidae	Sus	Sus scrofa	10.3389/fcimb.2024.1354449				WGA:60	0	1	Sus scrofa		gut metagenome; metagenome	Diarrhoea; Farm_Feeder_and_Drinker; Farm_Wall_and_Floor; Negative Control library preparation; Negative Control of DNA extraction; Negative Control of Swab used for Environmental sampling; Positive Control (DNA mock community); feces	Metagenome or environmental sample from gut metagenome; Metagenome or environmental sample from metagenome	60.0	https://doi.org/10.3389/fcimb.2024.1354449	True	True	Sus scrofa	True	pig	True	pig	False		False	ENA + paper	Shotgun metagenomic sequencing was performed on faecal and environmental samples collected from piglets on 10 farms during the first 14 days post-weaning.	UNIFORM_WITH_GAPS	36	METAGENOMIC:60	60	0	REVIEW	yes	high	gut_explicit	single_clear	Sus scrofa (pig)	False	mismatch	GUT: yes (high) — Paper title explicitly states 'pig microbiome', abstract describes 'pig's microbiota development around weaning', biome labels include 'gut metagenome', and isolation sources include 'Diarrhoea' (post-weaning diarrhoea context from abstract). Sample titles state 'Metagenome or environmental sample from gut metagenome'. || BODY_SITE: gut_explicit — Biome labels explicitly list 'gut metagenome'. Sample titles state 'from gut metagenome'. Abstract focuses on 'pig's microbiota development' in post-weaning context and microbiota dysbiosis, which is clearly gut-focused. Isolation sources include 'Diarrhoea', a gut symptom. || HOST: Sus scrofa (pig) [single_clear] — Host field states 'Sus scrofa'. Host species in samples shows 1 distinct species: 'Sus scrofa'. Paper title specifies 'pig microbiome'. No evidence of multiple hosts or incidental sampling. || METHOD: mismatch — Paper abstract states 'shotgun metagenomic sequencing' as the method (WGS/shotgun), but ENA fields show WGS/shotgun sample count = 0 while library_strategy_mix shows 'WGA:60' (whole genome amplification, not shotgun). This is a contradiction: the paper describes shotgun metagenomics but the ENA metadata indicates WGA-based approach instead. || SUMMARY: This is a legitimate animal gut microbiome study of pigs (Sus scrofa) using metagenomic sequencing to characterize post-weaning microbiota. However, there is a notable discrepancy between the stated method (shotgun metagenomics) and the ENA library strategy metadata (WGA), suggesting a potential metadata annotation error or methodological inconsistency in the submission.	gut_sample_issue			Isolation sources include 'Farm_Feeder_and_Drinker', 'Farm_Wall_and_Floor', 'Negative Control library preparation', 'Negative Control of DNA extraction', 'Negative Control of Swab used for Environmental sampling', and 'Positive Control' — these are environmental and control samples, NOT gut samples.		Genuine gut_sample_issue detected: deposit includes environmental farm surfaces and controls, not purely gut samples as the paper's framing suggests.	1		False		True	False
PRJEB14312	Ovis aries			Italy	5	WGS	pubmed_postdoc	28165194.0	28165194.0	Diversity and functions of the sheep faecal microbiota: a multi-omic characterization.	Tanca A	Uzzau S	Mammalia	Little is currently known on the microbial populations colonizing the sheep large intestine, despite their expected key role in host metabolism, physiology and immunity. This study reports the first characterization of the sheep faecal microbiota composition and functions, obtained through the application of a multi-omic strategy. An optimized protocol was first devised for DNA extraction and amplification from sheep stool samples. Then, 16S rDNA sequencing, shotgun metagenomics and shotgun metaproteomics were applied to unravel taxonomy, genetic potential and actively expressed functions and pathways respectively. Under a taxonomic perspective, the sheep faecal microbiota appeared globally comparable to that of other ruminants, with Firmicutes being the main phylum. In functional terms, we detected 2097 gene and 441 protein families, finding that the sheep faecal microbiota was primarily involved in catabolism. We investigated carbohydrate transport and degradation activities and identified phylum-specific pathways, such as methanogenesis for Euryarchaeota and acetogenesis for Firmicutes. Furthermore, our approach enabled the identification of proteins expressed by the eukaryotic component of the microbiota. Taken together, these findings unveil structure and role of the distal gut microbiota in sheep, and open the way to further studies aimed at elucidating its connections with management and dietary variables in sheep farming.	2017.0	Microbial biotechnology	YES		METAGENOMIC	YES	Animalia	Chordata	Artiodactyla	Bovidae	Ovis	aries	10.1111/1751-7915.12462				WGS:5	5	0			Ovis aries		Ovine1; Ovine2; Ovine3; Ovine4; Ovine5	5.0	https://doi.org/10.1111/1751-7915.12462	True	True	Ovis aries	True	sheep	True	sheep	False		False	ENA + paper	Sequenced samples came from sheep faecal (stool) samples.	UNIFORM	5	METAGENOMIC:5	5	5	KEEP	yes	high	gut_explicit	single_clear	Ovis aries (sheep)	False	agree	GUT: yes (high) — Title explicitly states 'sheep faecal microbiota'; abstract confirms 'microbial populations colonizing the sheep large intestine' and describes 'shotgun metagenomics' applied to 'sheep stool samples'. This is a genuine animal gut microbiome study using WGS. || BODY_SITE: gut_explicit — Abstract states 'sheep faecal microbiota composition' and 'sheep stool samples'; title specifies 'faecal microbiota'. Samples are explicitly from gut/fecal source. || HOST: Ovis aries (sheep) [single_clear] — Host field explicitly lists 'Ovis aries'; sample titles (Ovine1–5) confirm five sheep samples from a single host species. No multi-host study—this is a characterization of sheep faecal microbiota across individuals of the same species. || METHOD: agree — ENA records show 'library_strategy_mix: WGS:5' and 'library_source: METAGENOMIC', consistent with abstract description of 'shotgun metagenomics' applied to community DNA from stool. || SUMMARY: This is a single-species, multi-individual comparative study of sheep gut microbiota composition and function using shotgun metagenomics (WGS). All four criteria are clearly satisfied: animal gut study (yes), gut source (explicit), single host species (Ovis aries, five samples), and methods align with ENA metadata.	none					Paper and deposit are aligned: both describe a sheep (Ovis aries) faecal microbiota study with single-host, gut-sample samples analysed by metagenomics.	1		False		True	False
PRJNA717669	Rattus norvegicus	10116.0		Saint Kitts and Nevis	12	WGS	pubmed_postdoc	37027361.0	37027361.0	The selection of software and database for metagenomics sequence analysis impacts the outcome of microbial profiling and pathogen detection.	Xu R	Salvador LCM	Mammalia	Shotgun metagenomic sequencing analysis is widely used for microbial profiling of biological specimens and pathogen detection. However, very little is known about the technical biases caused by the choice of analysis software and databases on the biological specimen. In this study, we evaluated different direct read shotgun metagenomics taxonomic profiling software to characterize the microbial compositions of simulated mice gut microbiome samples and of biological samples collected from wild rodents across multiple taxonomic levels. Using ten of the most widely used metagenomics software and four different databases, we demonstrated that obtaining an accurate species-level microbial profile using the current direct read metagenomics profiling software is still a challenging task. We also showed that the discrepancies in results when different databases and software were used could lead to significant variations in the distinct microbial taxa classified, in the characterizations of the microbial communities, and in the differentially abundant taxa identified. Differences in database contents and read profiling algorithms are the main contributors for these discrepancies. The inclusion of host genomes and of genomes of the interested taxa in the databases is important for increasing the accuracy of profiling. Our analysis also showed that software included in this study differed in their ability to detect the presence of Leptospira, a major zoonotic pathogen of one health importance, especially at the species level resolution. We concluded that using different databases and software combinations can result in confounding biological conclusions in microbial profiling. Our study warrants that software and database selection must be based on the purpose of the study.	2023.0	PloS one	YES		METAGENOMIC	YES	Metazoa	Chordata	Rodentia	Muridae	Rattus	Rattus norvegicus	10.1371/journal.pone.0284031				WGS:12	12	2	Rattus norvegicus; Rattus rattus		rat metagenome	Rat R22 kidney; Rat R22 lung; Rat R22 spleen; Rat R26 kidney; Rat R26 lung; Rat R26 spleen; Rat R27 kidney; Rat R27 lung; Rat R27 spleen; Rat R28 kidney; Rat R28 lung; Rat R28 spleen	Metagenome or environmental sample from rat metagenome	12.0	https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0284031	True	True	Rattus norvegicus	False		True	wild rodents	False		False	ENA + paper	Sequenced samples came from wild rodents; simulated and mouse model data were used for methodological comparison only.	DIVERSE	12	METAGENOMIC:12	12	12	EXCLUDE	no	high	non_gut	multi_host	Rattus norvegicus; Rattus rattus	False	agree	GUT: no (high) — Isolation sources explicitly list non-gut organs: 'Rat R22 kidney; Rat R22 lung; Rat R22 spleen; Rat R26 kidney; Rat R26 lung; Rat R26 spleen; Rat R27 kidney; Rat R27 lung; Rat R27 spleen; Rat R28 kidn...' No gut samples identified. Abstract mentions 'simulated mice gut microbiome samples' and 'wild rodents' but actual biological samples are from kidney, lung, spleen—not gut. || BODY_SITE: non_gut — Isolation sources document kidney, lung, and spleen tissues exclusively. No gut, fecal, or intestinal samples present in the metadata. || HOST: Rattus norvegicus; Rattus rattus [multi_host] — Two distinct rat species sampled: 'Host species in samples (2 distinct): Rattus norvegicus; Rattus rattus'. Study compares microbial composition across wild rodent species; this is a genuine comparative multi-host study, not incidental sampling. || METHOD: agree — ENA records 12 WGS (shotgun metagenomics) samples with METAGENOMIC library source, consistent with abstract statement: 'Shotgun metagenomic sequencing analysis' evaluating 'direct read shotgun metagenomics taxonomic profiling.' || SUMMARY: This is a methodological benchmarking study of metagenomics software/databases using wild rodent tissue samples (kidney, lung, spleen), not a gut microbiome study. The abstract's mention of 'simulated mice gut microbiome samples' refers to in silico controls, not the actual biological specimens analyzed, which are exclusively from non-gut organs.	gut_sample_issue; multihost_issue			Isolation sources include: kidney, lung, spleen from multiple individual rats (R22, R26, R27, R28). These are organ/tissue samples, NOT gut samples.	Deposit sample-level data explicitly lists two distinct host species across samples: Rattus norvegicus and Rattus rattus (seen in isolation source labels and host_species_list).	Two issues detected: (1) samples are from non-gut organs (kidney, lung, spleen) despite apparent gut-microbiome framing, and (2) deposit explicitly names two distinct rat species (Rattus norvegicus and Rattus rattus) while paper presents them as undifferentiated 'wild rodents.'	1		False		True	False
PRJEB73511	horse metagenome			Italy	47	WGS	pubmed_postdoc	39399231.0	39399231.0	A 15-day pilot biodiversity intervention with horses in a farm system leads to gut microbiome rewilding in 10 urban Italian children.	Scicchitano D	Candela M	Mammalia	"To provide some glimpses on the possibility of shaping the human gut microbiome (GM) through probiotic exchange with natural ecosystems, here we explored the impact of 15 days of daily interaction with horses on the GM of 10 urban-living Italian children. Specifically, the children were in close contact with the horses in an ""educational farm"", where they spent almost 10 h/day interacting with the animals. The children's GM was assessed before and after the horse interaction using metabarcoding sequencing and shotgun metagenomics, along with the horses' skin, oral and fecal microbiomes. Targeted metabolomic analysis for GM-produced beneficial metabolites (i.e., short-chain fatty acids) in the children's feces was also performed. Interaction with horses facilitated the acquisition of health-related traits in the children's GM, such as increased diversity, enhanced butyrate production and an increase in several health-promoting species considered to be next-generation probiotics. Among these, the butyrate producers Facecalibacterium prausnitzii and F. duncaniae and a species belonging to the order Christensenellales. Interaction with horses was also associated with increased proportions of Eggerthella lenta, Gordonibacter pamelae and G. urolithinfaciens, GM components known to play a role in the bioconversion of dietary plant polyphenols into beneficial metabolites. Notably, no increase in potentially harmful traits, including toxin genes, was observed. Overall, our pilot study provides some insights on the existence of possible health-promoting exchanges between children and horses microbiomes. It lays the groundwork for an implemented and more systematic enrollment effort to explore the full complexity of human GM rewilding through exchange with natural ecosystems, aligning with the One Health approach."	2024.0	One health (Amsterdam, Netherlands)	YES		METAGENOMIC	YES	Animalia	Chordata	Perissodactyla	Equidae	Equus	caballus	10.1016/j.onehlt.2024.100902				WGS:47	47	0			horse metagenome; human gut metagenome		Horse microbiome; Human gut microbiome	47.0	https://doi.org/10.1016/j.onehlt.2024.100902	True	False		True	horses; human (children)	True	human (children); horses	False		False	human-by-biome-label	Sequenced samples came from urban Italian children (before and after horse interaction) and from horses (skin, oral, and fecal microbiomes)	NO_HOST_DATA	0	METAGENOMIC:47	47	47	REVIEW	yes	high	gut_explicit	multi_host	Equus caballus (horse) and Homo sapiens (human child)	True	mismatch	GUT: yes (high) — Abstract explicitly states 'the children's GM was assessed' and 'the horses' skin, oral and fecal microbiomes' were sampled. Study title and abstract confirm gut microbiome investigation in both humans (children) and horses. Humans are animals; human gut microbiome studies are valid animal gut studies per instructions. || BODY_SITE: gut_explicit — Abstract states 'the children's feces was also performed' for metabolomic analysis. Title references 'gut microbiome rewilding in 10 urban Italian children.' Biome labels include 'human gut metagenome.' Sample titles specify 'Human gut microbiome.' For horses, abstract mentions 'fecal microbiomes' alongside skin and oral, confirming fecal (gut) sampling. || HOST: Equus caballus (horse) and Homo sapiens (human child) [multi_host] (HUMAN) — Study is a genuine comparative intervention examining microbiome changes in both horses and children through direct contact. Abstract: 'we explored the impact of 15 days of daily interaction with horses on the GM of 10 urban-living Italian children' and 'the horses' skin, oral and fecal microbiomes' were assessed. This is a multi-host comparative study, not incidental sampling. || METHOD: mismatch — Abstract states