PRJNA475948	Cortaritermes fulviceps	1544288.0		Argentina: Corrientes	2	WGS	pubmed_postdoc	32123275.0	32123275.0	Neotropical termite microbiomes as sources of novel plant cell wall degrading enzymes.	Romero Victorica M	Talia PM	Insecta	In this study, we used shotgun metagenomic sequencing to characterise the microbial metabolic potential for lignocellulose transformation in the gut of two colonies of Argentine higher termite species with different feeding habits, Cortaritermes fulviceps and Nasutitermes aquilinus. Our goal was to assess the microbial community compositions and metabolic capacity, and to identify genes involved in lignocellulose degradation. Individuals from both termite species contained the same five dominant bacterial phyla (Spirochaetes, Firmicutes, Proteobacteria, Fibrobacteres and Bacteroidetes) although with different relative abundances. However, detected functional capacity varied, with C. fulviceps (a grass-wood-feeder) gut microbiome samples containing more genes related to amino acid metabolism, whereas N. aquilinus (a wood-feeder) gut microbiome samples were enriched in genes involved in carbohydrate metabolism and cellulose degradation. The C. fulviceps gut microbiome was enriched specifically in genes coding for debranching- and oligosaccharide-degrading enzymes. These findings suggest an association between the primary food source and the predicted categories of the enzymes present in the gut microbiomes of each species. To further investigate the termite microbiomes as sources of biotechnologically relevant glycosyl hydrolases, a putative GH10 endo-β-1,4-xylanase, Xyl10E, was cloned and expressed in Escherichia coli. Functional analysis of the recombinant metagenome-derived enzyme showed high specificity towards beechwood xylan (288.1 IU/mg), with the optimum activity at 50 °C and a pH-activity range from 5 to 10. These characteristics suggest that Xy110E may be a promising candidate for further development in lignocellulose deconstruction applications.	2020.0	Scientific reports	YES		METAGENOMIC	YES	Metazoa	Arthropoda	Blattodea	Termitidae	Cortaritermes	Cortaritermes fulviceps	10.1038/s41598-020-60850-5				WGS:2	2	2	Cortaritermes fulviceps; Nasutitermes aquilinus		insect gut metagenome		Cortaritermes fulviceps gut metagenome; Nasutitermes aquilinus gut metagenome	2.0	https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-020-60850-5	True	True	Cortaritermes fulviceps	True	termite	True	Cortaritermes fulviceps; Nasutitermes aquilinus	False		False	ENA + paper	Metagenomic sequencing was performed on gut samples from two termite species.	DIVERSE	2	METAGENOMIC:2	2	2	KEEP	yes	high	gut_explicit	multi_host	Cortaritermes fulviceps; Nasutitermes aquilinus	False	agree	GUT: yes (high) — Abstract explicitly states 'shotgun metagenomic sequencing to characterise the microbial metabolic potential for lignocellulose transformation in the gut of two colonies of Argentine higher termite species'. Sample titles confirm 'gut metagenome'. Library source is METAGENOMIC (community DNA, not host genome). || BODY_SITE: gut_explicit — Title, abstract, and sample titles all explicitly reference 'gut'. Abstract: 'in the gut of two colonies'; sample titles: 'Cortaritermes fulviceps gut metagenome' and 'Nasutitermes aquilinus gut metagenome'. || HOST: Cortaritermes fulviceps; Nasutitermes aquilinus [multi_host] — Study explicitly compares two distinct termite species with different feeding habits. Abstract states 'two colonies of Argentine higher termite species... Cortaritermes fulviceps and Nasutitermes aquilinus'. Sample metadata confirms 2 distinct host species represented. This is a genuine comparative study OF multiple hosts' guts, not an incidental multi-host survey. || METHOD: agree — ENA library_strategy_mix indicates WGS:2 (two WGS samples); abstract describes 'shotgun metagenomic sequencing'. Library_source is METAGENOMIC, consistent with gut community DNA sampling. WGS sample count = 2 matches the number of host species sampled. || SUMMARY: This is a rigorous, primary animal gut microbiome study of two termite species using shotgun metagenomics. The study is a genuine comparative analysis of two hosts' guts, not an incidental survey, making it a valid multi-host gut microbiome study.	none					All metadata are consistent: the paper studies two termite species with distinct microbiomes, and the deposit correctly captures both species in separate samples with appropriate gut-sample annotations.	1		False		True	True
PRJNA609596	Rattus norvegicus	10116.0		Italy	74	WGS	pubmed_postdoc	33502259.0	33502259.0	Use of Shotgun Metagenomics and Metabolomics to Evaluate the Impact of Glyphosate or Roundup MON 52276 on the Gut Microbiota and Serum Metabolome of Sprague-Dawley Rats.	Mesnage R	Antoniou MN	Mammalia	BACKGROUND: There is intense debate on whether glyphosate can inhibit the shikimate pathway of gastrointestinal microorganisms, with potential health implications. OBJECTIVES: We tested whether glyphosate or its representative EU herbicide formulation Roundup MON 52276 affects the rat gut microbiome. METHODS: We combined cecal microbiome shotgun metagenomics with serum and cecum metabolomics to assess the effects of glyphosate [0.5, 50, 175mg/kg body weight (BW) per day] or MON 52276 at the same glyphosate-equivalent doses, in a 90-d toxicity test in rats. RESULTS: Glyphosate and MON 52276 treatment resulted in ceca accumulation of shikimic acid and 3-dehydroshikimic acid, suggesting inhibition of 5-enolpyruvylshikimate-3-phosphate synthase of the shikimate pathway in the gut microbiome. Cysteinylglycine, γ-glutamylglutamine, and valylglycine levels were elevated in the cecal microbiome following glyphosate and MON 52276 treatments. Altered cecum metabolites were not differentially expressed in serum, suggesting that the glyphosate and MON 52276 impact on gut microbial metabolism had limited consequences on physiological biochemistry. Serum metabolites differentially expressed with glyphosate treatment were associated with nicotinamide, branched-chain amino acid, methionine, cysteine, and taurine metabolism, indicative of a response to oxidative stress. MON 52276 had similar, but more pronounced, effects than glyphosate on the serum metabolome. Shotgun metagenomics of the cecum showed that treatment with glyphosate and MON 52276 resulted in higher levels of Eggerthella spp., Shinella zoogleoides, Acinetobacter johnsonii, and Akkermansia muciniphila. Shinella zoogleoides was higher only with MON 52276 exposure. In vitro culture assays with Lacticaseibacillus rhamnosus strains showed that Roundup GT plus inhibited growth at concentrations at which MON 52276 and glyphosate had no effect. DISCUSSION: Our study highlights the power of multi-omics approaches to investigate the toxic effects of pesticides. Multi-omics revealed that glyphosate and MON 52276 inhibited the shikimate pathway in the rat gut microbiome. Our findings could be used to develop biomarkers for epidemiological studies aimed at evaluating the effects of glyphosate herbicides on humans. https://doi.org/10.1289/EHP6990.	2021.0	Environmental health perspectives	YES		METAGENOMIC	YES	Metazoa	Chordata	Rodentia	Muridae	Rattus	Rattus norvegicus	10.1289/EHP6990				WGS:74	74	1	Rattus norvegicus		rat gut metagenome	Caecum sample; Negative; Zymobiotics	MIMS Environmental/Metagenome sample from rat gut metagenome	74.0	https://doi.org/10.1289/EHP6990	True	True	Rattus norvegicus	True	Sprague-Dawley rats	True	Sprague-Dawley rats	True	glyphosate toxicity	False	ENA + paper	[model of glyphosate toxicity] Cecal microbiome samples were collected from Sprague-Dawley rats exposed to glyphosate or Roundup MON 52276 and analyzed via shotgun metagenomics.	UNIFORM	74	METAGENOMIC:74	74	74	KEEP	yes	high	gut_explicit	single_clear	Rattus norvegicus	False	agree	GUT: yes (high) — Title explicitly states 'Gut Microbiota'; abstract describes 'cecal microbiome shotgun metagenomics'; isolation source confirmed as 'Caecum sample'; library_source is METAGENOMIC (community DNA); biome label states 'rat gut metagenome'; sample titles reference 'rat gut metagenome' || BODY_SITE: gut_explicit — Title states 'Gut Microbiota'; abstract specifies 'cecal microbiome' and '90-d toxicity test in rats'; isolation source explicitly lists 'Caecum sample'; biome label 'rat gut metagenome' || HOST: Rattus norvegicus [single_clear] — Host field: Rattus norvegicus; Host species in samples (1 distinct): Rattus norvegicus; study design is a controlled 90-day toxicity test on a single rat strain with no multi-host comparative design; title confirms Sprague-Dawley Rats (a common Rattus norvegicus laboratory strain) || METHOD: agree — Title and abstract describe 'Shotgun Metagenomics'; ENA records WGS:74 (shotgun = WGS); library_source METAGENOMIC aligns with cecal microbiome community DNA; 74 WGS samples matches metagenomics approach described || SUMMARY: This is a genuine, well-documented animal gut microbiome study using shotgun metagenomics on rat cecal samples. Single host (Rattus norvegicus), explicit gut sampling site, and complete methodological agreement with ENA metadata.	none					No data quality issues detected. Paper and deposit are in agreement on host (Sprague-Dawley rats = Rattus norvegicus) and sample type (gut/caecum samples).	1		False		True	False
PRJNA1010388	Mus musculus	10090.0			109	WGS	pubmed_postdoc	39079980.0	39079980.0	Short-chain fatty acids ameliorate imiquimod-induced skin thickening and IL-17 levels and alter gut microbiota in mice: a metagenomic association analysis.	Chen YJ	Wu CY	Mammalia	Short-chain fatty acids (SCFAs) have been proposed to have anti-inflammatory effects and improve immune homeostasis. We aimed to examine the effects of SCFAs on skin phenotype, systemic inflammation, and gut microbiota in mice with psoriasis-like inflammation. Imiquimod (IMQ)-treated C57BL/6 mice served as the study model. We conducted a metagenomic association study of IMQ-mice treated with SCFAs or anti-IL-17 antibody using whole-genome shotgun sequencing. The associations among SCFA supplements, skin thickness, circulating inflammatory profiles, and fecal microbiota profiles were investigated. The microbiome study was performed using pipelines for phylogenetic analysis, functional gene analysis, and pathway analysis. In IMQ-treated mice, there were increases in skin thickness and splenic weight, as well as unique fecal microbial profiles. SCFAs ameliorated IMQ-induced skin thickening, splenic weight gain, and serum IL-17F levels, with results that were comparable with those receiving anti-IL-17 treatment. IMQ-treated mice receiving SCFAs had greater microbial diversity than mice treated with IMQ alone. SCFAs and anti-IL17 treatment were associated with alteration of gut microbiota, with increased prevalences of Oscillospiraceae and Lachnopiraceae and decreased prevalences of Muribaculaceae and Bacteroides, which have been predicted to be associated with increased glycan degradation, phenylalanine metabolism, and xylene degradation. SCFAs may mitigate IMQ-induced skin thickening and IL-17F levels and alter fecal microbiota profiles in IMQ-treated mice.	2024.0	Scientific reports	YES		METAGENOMIC	YES	Metazoa	Chordata	Rodentia	Muridae	Mus	Mus musculus	10.1038/s41598-024-67325-x				WGS:109	109	1	Mus musculus		metagenome		MIMS Environmental/Metagenome sample from metagenome	109.0	https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-024-67325-x	True	True	Mus musculus	True	mice	True	mouse (model of psoriasis-like inflammation)	True	psoriasis-like inflammation	False	ENA + paper	[model of psoriasis-like inflammation] Fecal metagenomic sequencing was performed on imiquimod-treated C57BL/6 mice.	UNIFORM	109	METAGENOMIC:109	109	109	KEEP	yes	high	gut_explicit	single_clear	Mus musculus	False	agree	GUT: yes (high) — Abstract explicitly states 'metagenomic association study' of 'gut microbiota in mice' using 'whole-genome shotgun sequencing' on 'fecal microbiota profiles'. Title mentions 'alter gut microbiota in mice'. This is a genuine animal gut microbiome study. || BODY_SITE: gut_explicit — Abstract explicitly refers to 'fecal microbiota profiles' and 'gut microbiota'. Title states 'alter gut microbiota'. Samples are from fecal material, which is the standard proxy for gut microbiota. || HOST: Mus musculus [single_clear] — Host field explicitly states 'Mus musculus'. Host species in samples shows '1 distinct: Mus musculus'. Abstract confirms 'IMQ-treated C57BL/6 mice' (C57BL/6 is a mouse strain). Single host species across all 109 samples. || METHOD: agree — Abstract states 'whole-genome shotgun sequencing' and library_strategy_mix shows 'WGS:109' with 109 shotgun samples. library_source is 'METAGENOMIC' confirming community DNA sequencing. Methods align with ENA WGS metagenomic designation. || SUMMARY: This is a well-documented, single-host (mouse) gut microbiome study using WGS metagenomic sequencing on fecal samples. No host, body site, or method contradictions detected.	none					No reporting errors detected. Host is correctly identified as Mus musculus; this is an appropriate animal-model study of disease; sample origin (fecal/gut microbiota) aligns with paper narrative despite sparse deposit metadata.	1		False		True	False
PRJNA744378	Rhodnius prolixus	13249.0		Brazil: Belo Horizonte	30	WGS	pubmed_postdoc	35272716.0	35272716.0	Exposure to Trypanosoma parasites induces changes in the microbiome of the Chagas disease vector Rhodnius prolixus.	Eberhard FE	Tobias NJ	Insecta	BACKGROUND: The causative agent of Chagas disease, Trypanosoma cruzi, and its nonpathogenic relative, Trypanosoma rangeli, are transmitted by haematophagous triatomines and undergo a crucial ontogenetic phase in the insect's intestine. In the process, the parasites interfere with the host immune system as well as the microbiome present in the digestive tract potentially establishing an environment advantageous for development. However, the coherent interactions between host, pathogen and microbiota have not yet been elucidated in detail. We applied a metagenome shotgun sequencing approach to study the alterations in the microbiota of Rhodnius prolixus, a major vector of Chagas disease, after exposure to T. cruzi and T. rangeli focusing also on the functional capacities present in the intestinal microbiome of the insect. RESULTS: The intestinal microbiota of R. prolixus was dominated by the bacterial orders Enterobacterales, Corynebacteriales, Lactobacillales, Clostridiales and Chlamydiales, whereas the latter conceivably originated from the blood used for pathogen exposure. The anterior and posterior midgut samples of the exposed insects showed a reduced overall number of organisms compared to the control group. However, we also found enriched bacterial groups after exposure to T. cruzi as well as T rangeli. While the relative abundance of Enterobacterales and Corynebacteriales decreased considerably, the Lactobacillales, mainly composed of the genus Enterococcus, developed as the most abundant taxonomic group. This applies in particular to vectors challenged with T. rangeli and at early timepoints after exposure to vectors challenged with T. cruzi. Furthermore, we were able to reconstruct four metagenome-assembled genomes from the intestinal samples and elucidate their unique metabolic functionalities within the triatomine microbiome, including the genome of a recently described insect symbiont, Candidatus Symbiopectobacterium, and the secondary metabolites producing bacteria Kocuria spp. CONCLUSIONS: Our results facilitate a deeper understanding of the processes that take place in the intestinal tract of triatomine vectors during colonisation by trypanosomal parasites and highlight the influential aspects of pathogen-microbiota interactions. In particular, the mostly unexplored metabolic capacities of the insect vector's microbiome are clearer, underlining its role in the transmission of Chagas disease. Video Abstract.	2022.0	Microbiome	YES		METAGENOMIC	YES	Metazoa	Arthropoda	Hemiptera	Reduviidae	Rhodnius	Rhodnius prolixus	10.1186/s40168-022-01240-z				WGS:30	30	1	Rhodnius prolixus		insect metagenome		MIMS Environmental/Metagenome sample from insect metagenome	30.0	https://doi.org/10.1186/s40168-022-01240-z	True	True	Rhodnius prolixus	True	Rhodnius prolixus	True	Rhodnius prolixus	False		False	ENA + paper	Metagenome shotgun sequencing was performed on intestinal samples from Rhodnius prolixus insects exposed to Trypanosoma parasites.	UNIFORM	30	METAGENOMIC:30	30	30	KEEP	yes	high	gut_explicit	single_clear	Rhodnius prolixus	False	agree	GUT: yes (high) — Abstract explicitly states 'metagenome shotgun sequencing approach to study the alterations in the microbiota of Rhodnius prolixus' focusing on 'the intestine' and 'digestive tract'. This is a genuine animal (insect) gut microbiome study using WGS. || BODY_SITE: gut_explicit — Abstract explicitly specifies 'the insect's intestine' and 'digestive tract' as the focus. Title references 'microbiome of the Chagas disease vector Rhodnius prolixus' in context of parasite transmission through the intestinal environment. || HOST: Rhodnius prolixus [single_clear] — Host field states 'Rhodnius prolixus'; host species in samples field confirms