PRJNA1066198	Bombus impatiens	132113.0		Canada: Ottawa	49	WGS	pubmed_postdoc	39175129.0	39175129.0	Changes in bumblebee queen gut microbiotas during and after overwintering diapause.	Hotchkiss MZ	Poulain AJ	Insecta	Bumblebees are key pollinators with gut microbiotas that support host health. After bumblebee queens undergo winter diapause, which occurs before spring colony establishment, their gut microbiotas are disturbed, but little is known about community dynamics during diapause itself. Queen gut microbiotas also help seed worker microbiotas, so it is important that they recover post-diapause to a typical community structure, a process that may be impeded by pesticide exposure. We examined how bumblebee queen gut microbiota community structure and metabolic potential shift during and after winter diapause, and whether post-diapause recovery is affected by pesticide exposure. To do so, we placed commercial Bombus impatiens queens into diapause, euthanizing them at 0, 2 and 4 months of diapause. Additionally, we allowed some queens to recover from diapause for 1 week before euthanasia, exposing half to the common herbicide glyphosate. Using whole-community, shotgun metagenomic sequencing, we found that core bee gut phylotypes dominated queen gut microbiotas before, during and after diapause, but that two phylotypes, Schmidhempelia and Snodgrassella, ceased to be detected during late diapause and recovery. Despite fluctuations in taxonomic community structure, metabolic potential remained constant through diapause and recovery. Also, glyphosate exposure did not affect post-diapause microbiota recovery. However, metagenomic assembly quality and our ability to detect microbial taxa and metabolic pathways declined alongside microbial abundance, which was substantially reduced during diapause. Our study offers new insights into how bumblebee queen gut microbiotas change taxonomically and functionally during a key life stage and provides guidance for future microbiota studies in diapausing bumblebees.	2025.0	Insect molecular biology	YES		METAGENOMIC	YES	Metazoa	Arthropoda	Hymenoptera	Apidae	Bombus	Bombus impatiens	10.1111/imb.12957				WGS:49	49	1	Bombus impatiens		insect metagenome	gut	R15_R1; R15_filt; R18; R18_filt; R23	49.0	https://doi.org/10.1111/imb.12957	True	True	Bombus impatiens	True	bumblebee queen	True	Bombus impatiens queen	False		False	ENA + paper	Sequenced samples came from commercial Bombus impatiens queens during and after winter diapause.	UNIFORM	49	METAGENOMIC:49	49	49	KEEP	yes	high	gut_explicit	single_clear	Bombus impatiens	False	agree	GUT: yes (high) — Title states 'bumblebee queen gut microbiotas'; abstract confirms 'Queen gut microbiotas' and 'gut microbiota community structure'; isolation_source field explicitly lists 'gut'; library_source is METAGENOMIC (community DNA, not single organism genome). || BODY_SITE: gut_explicit — Isolation source explicitly states 'gut'; paper title and abstract repeatedly reference 'gut microbiota'; sample titles (e.g., R15_R1, R18) do not contradict this; no non-gut body sites mentioned. || HOST: Bombus impatiens [single_clear] — Host field and host species in samples both state 'Bombus impatiens' (bumble bee); 1 distinct host species; abstract specifies 'commercial Bombus impatiens queens' placed into controlled diapause; no multi-host survey structure—this is a controlled experiment on a single host species. || METHOD: agree — Paper describes WGS (shotgun metagenomics) of queen gut communities; ENA library_strategy_mix lists WGS:49 (49 WGS samples); library_source is METAGENOMIC; 49 WGS samples matches the metagenomics approach stated in abstract (euthanizing queens at multiple timepoints and analyzing gut microbiota). || SUMMARY: This is a straightforward, well-curated animal gut metagenomics study of bumblebee (Bombus impatiens) queen gut microbiota during diapause. Single host species, explicit gut sampling, metagenomic WGS approach, and metadata all align.	none					No reporting errors detected; paper and deposit are fully concordant on host identity (Bombus impatiens bumblebee queens) and sample type (gut microbiota).	1		False		True	False
PRJNA671703	manure metagenome			USA	229	WGS	pubmed_postdoc	33975936.0	33975936.0	Manure Microbial Communities and Resistance Profiles Reconfigure after Transition to Manure Pits and Differ from Those in Fertilized Field Soil.	Sukhum KV	Shukla SK	Mammalia	In agricultural settings, microbes and antimicrobial resistance genes (ARGs) have the potential to be transferred across diverse environments and ecosystems. The consequences of these microbial transfers are unclear and understudied. On dairy farms, the storage of cow manure in manure pits and subsequent application to field soil as a fertilizer may facilitate the spread of the mammalian gut microbiome and its associated ARGs to the environment. To determine the extent of both taxonomic and resistance similarity during these transitions, we collected fresh manure, manure from pits, and field soil across 15 different dairy farms for three consecutive seasons. We used a combination of shotgun metagenomic sequencing and functional metagenomics to quantitatively interrogate taxonomic and ARG compositional variation on farms. We found that as the microbiome transitions from fresh dairy cow manure to manure pits, microbial taxonomic compositions and resistance profiles experience distinct restructuring, including decreases in alpha diversity and shifts in specific ARG abundances that potentially correspond to fresh manure going from a gut-structured community to an environment-structured community. Further, we did not find evidence of shared microbial community or a transfer of ARGs between manure and field soil microbiomes. Our results suggest that fresh manure experiences a compositional change in manure pits during storage and that the storage of manure in manure pits does not result in a depletion of ARGs. We did not find evidence of taxonomic or ARG restructuring of soil microbiota with the application of manure to field soils, as soil communities remained resilient to manure-induced perturbation.IMPORTANCE The addition of dairy cow manure-stored in manure pits-to field soil has the potential to introduce not only organic nutrients but also mammalian microbial communities and antimicrobial resistance genes (ARGs) to soil communities. Using shotgun sequencing paired with functional metagenomics, we showed that microbial community composition changed between fresh manure and manure pit samples with a decrease in gut-associated pathobionts, while ARG abundance and diversity remained high. However, field soil communities were distinct from those in manure in both microbial taxonomic and ARG composition. These results broaden our understanding of the transfer of microbial communities in agricultural settings and suggest that field soil microbial communities are resilient against the deposition of ARGs or microbial communities from manure.	2021.0	mBio	YES		METAGENOMIC	YES	Animalia	Chordata	Artiodactyla	Bovidae	Bos	taurus	10.1128/mBio.00798-21				WGS:229	229	0			manure metagenome; soil metagenome		MIMS Environmental/Metagenome sample from manure metagenome; MIMS Environmental/Metagenome sample from soil metagenome	229.0	https://doi.org/10.1128/mBio.00798-21	True	False		False		True	dairy cow	False		False	paper only	Sequenced samples came from fresh dairy cow manure, manure pits, and field soil on dairy farms	NO_HOST_DATA	0	METAGENOMIC:229	229	229	EXCLUDE	no	high	non_gut	genus_only		False	agree	GUT: no (high) — Study explicitly sampled 'fresh manure, manure from pits, and field soil' – the biome labels are 'manure metagenome; soil metagenome', not gut microbiome. Host field is 'manure metagenome'. While the abstract mentions that 'storage of cow manure...may facilitate the spread of the mammalian gut microbiome', the actual samples analyzed are environmental (manure pits and soil), not gut tissue or fecal samples collected directly from the host. || BODY_SITE: non_gut — Sample titles indicate 'MIMS Environmental/Metagenome sample from manure metagenome' and 'soil metagenome'. Isolation sources are manure storage pits and field soil, not gastrointestinal tract samples. || HOST:  [genus_only] — The abstract states samples were collected 'across 15 different dairy farms' and references 'cow manure', inferring the source animal is Bos (cattle). However, no explicit host species field is populated (0 distinct host species listed), and the study focuses on the environmental reservoir rather than individual animals. The host is inferred from context but not formally declared in the metadata. || METHOD: agree — Abstract explicitly states 'We used a combination of shotgun metagenomic sequencing and functional metagenomics', matching the library_strategy_mix of WGS:229 and library_source METAGENOMIC. || SUMMARY: This is an environmental metagenomics study of manure and soil, not a gut microbiome study. Although it traces the fate of cow gut microbiota post-excretion, the actual samples are environmental reservoirs, placing this outside the scope of animal gut microbiome research.	gut_sample_issue			Sample titles and biome labels explicitly include 'soil metagenome' and 'manure metagenome'. The abstract describes sampling 'fresh manure, manure from pits, and field soil'. Field soil is not a gut sample.		Gut sample issue flagged: deposit explicitly contains soil metagenome samples, which are non-gut environmental samples, not gut microbiome samples.	1		False		True	False
PRJNA1184324	mouse			China: wuxi	20	WGS	pubmed_postdoc	40075266.0	40075266.0	Fecal microbiota transplantation mitigates postdieting weight regain in mice by modulating the gut-liver axis.	Cao H	Li D	Mammalia	BACKGROUND: Dysbiosis of the microbiome is strongly associated with weight rebound after dieting. However, the interactions between the host and microbiome and their relevance to the pathogenesis of post-diet weight rebound remain unclear. PURPOSE: This study aimed to evaluate the effects of fecal microbiota transplantation (FMT) on post-diet weight regain and to investigate the underlying mechanisms by which FMT inhibits weight regain. METHODS: FMT was administered once daily to mice for 5 weeks. Gas chromatography tandem mass spectrometry was employed to analyze short-chain fatty acid levels in serum, ultrahigh-performance liquid chromatography tandem mass spectrometry was utilized for analyzing hepatic lipid metabolites, and shotgun metagenomic sequencing was applied to examine the intestinal microbiome. RESULTS: FMT reduced weight regain and prevented lipid accumulation in both liver and adipose tissue while also improving glucose intolerance in mice. Furthermore, FMT increased the abundance of Enterorhabdus caecimuris and decreased the abundances of Burkholderiales, Sutterellaceae, Turicimonas muris, Bacteroides stercorirosoris, and Acetivibrio ethanolgignens within the gut microbiota. Additionally, elevated propionic acid levels and significant alterations in hepatic lipid metabolites were observed following FMT administration. CONCLUSIONS: Our findings demonstrate that FMT effectively mitigates post-diet weight regain and associated complications. These effects are mediated through interactions between the gut microbiota and the liver via the gut-propionic acid-liver axis. CLINICAL TRIAL NUMBER: Not applicable.	2025.0	BMC microbiology	YES		METAGENOMIC	YES	Animalia	Chordata	Rodentia	Muridae	Mus	musculus	10.1186/s12866-025-03853-4				WGS:20	20	0			feces met