PRJNA541820	C57BL/6			Japan:Tokyo	8	WGS	pubmed_postdoc	32208434.0	32208434.0	Association of colitis with gut-microbiota dysbiosis in clathrin adapter AP-1B knockout mice.	Jangid A	Prakash T	Mammalia	Inflammatory bowel disease results from alterations in the immune system and intestinal microbiota. The role of intestinal epithelial cells (IECs) in maintaining gut homeostasis is well known and its perturbation often causes gastrointestinal disorders including IBD. The epithelial specific adaptor protein (AP)-1B is involved in the establishment of the polarity of IECs. Deficiency of the AP-1B μ subunit (Ap1m2-/-) leads to the development of chronic colitis in mice. However, how this deficiency affects the gut microbes and its potential functions remains elusive. To gain insights into the gut microbiome of Ap1m2-/- mice having the colitis phenotype, we undertook shotgun metagenomic sequencing analysis of knockout mice. We found important links to the microbial features involved in altering various physiological pathways, including carbohydrate metabolism, nutrient transportation, oxidative stress, and bacterial pathogenesis (cell motility). In addition, an increased abundance of sulfur-reducing and lactate-producing bacteria has been observed which may aggravate the colitis condition.	2020.0	PloS one	YES		METAGENOMIC	YES	Animalia	Chordata	Rodentia	Muridae	Mus	musculus	10.1371/journal.pone.0228358				WGS:8	8	0			mouse gut metagenome	feces	Ap1BKO_pm_d; Dr_Fukuda_l1; Dr_Fukuda_l2; Dr_Fukuda_l3; Dr_Fukuda_l5	8.0	https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0228358	True	True	C57BL/6	True	mouse (model of colitis)	True	mouse (model of colitis)	True	colitis	False	ENA + paper	[model of colitis] Shotgun metagenomic sequencing was performed on gut samples from Ap1m2-/- knockout mice with chronic colitis.	UNIFORM	8	METAGENOMIC:8	8	8	KEEP	yes	high	gut_explicit	single_clear	Mus musculus (C57BL/6 mouse strain)	False	agree	GUT: yes (high) — Paper title states 'gut-microbiota dysbiosis in clathrin adapter AP-1B knockout mice'; abstract explicitly describes 'shotgun metagenomic sequencing analysis' of 'gut microbiome of Ap1m2-/- mice'; library_source is METAGENOMIC; isolation source is 'feces'. || BODY_SITE: gut_explicit — Abstract states 'gut microbiome' and 'gut microbes'; biome label is 'mouse gut metagenome'; isolation source is 'feces' (standard gut sampling method); no alternative body sites mentioned. || HOST: Mus musculus (C57BL/6 mouse strain) [single_clear] — Host field states 'C57BL/6' (a mouse strain); paper title and abstract both refer to 'mice' and specifically 'Ap1m2-/- mice' (knockout mice); sample titles include 'Ap1BKO_pm_d' confirming AP-1B knockout mouse line; single host organism studied. || METHOD: agree — Paper explicitly states 'shotgun metagenomic sequencing analysis' which matches library_strategy_mix WGS:8 and library_source METAGENOMIC; 8 WGS samples documented in both metadata and study design. || SUMMARY: This is a genuine animal gut metagenomics study of knockout mice with colitis, using fecal shotgun sequencing. The host is a single, clearly identified laboratory mouse strain (C57BL/6), and all metadata are concordant with the paper's stated methods.	none					No reporting errors detected. The study correctly describes shotgun metagenomic sequencing of gut microbiota from Ap1m2-/- mice on C57BL/6 background, and the deposit metadata accurately reflects this single-host, gut-sample study design.	1		False		True	False
PRJNA479518	vulture			missing	80	WGS	pubmed_postdoc	30309375.0	30309375.0	Protective role of the vulture facial skin and gut microbiomes aid adaptation to scavenging.	Zepeda Mendoza ML	Sicheritz-Pontén T	Aves	BACKGROUND: Vultures have adapted the remarkable ability to feed on carcasses that may contain microorganisms that would be pathogenic to most other animals. The holobiont concept suggests that the genetic basis of such adaptation may not only lie within their genomes, but additionally in their associated microbes. To explore this, we generated shotgun DNA sequencing datasets of the facial skin and large intestine microbiomes of the black vulture (Coragyps atratus) and the turkey vulture (Cathartes aura). We characterized the functional potential and taxonomic diversity of their microbiomes, the potential pathogenic challenges confronted by vultures, and the microbial taxa and genes that could play a protective role on the facial skin and in the gut. RESULTS: We found microbial taxa and genes involved in diseases, such as dermatitis and pneumonia (more abundant on the facial skin), and gas gangrene and food poisoning (more abundant in the gut). Interestingly, we found taxa and functions with potential for playing beneficial roles, such as antilisterial bacteria in the gut, and genes for the production of antiparasitics and insecticides on the facial skin. Based on the identified phages, we suggest that phages aid in the control and possibly elimination, as in phage therapy, of microbes reported as pathogenic to a variety of species. Interestingly, we identified Adineta vaga in the gut, an invertebrate that feeds on dead bacteria and protozoans, suggesting a defensive predatory mechanism. Finally, we suggest a colonization resistance role through biofilm formation played by Fusobacteria and Clostridia in the gut. CONCLUSIONS: Our results highlight the importance of complementing genomic analyses with metagenomics in order to obtain a clearer understanding of the host-microbial alliance and show the importance of microbiome-mediated health protection for adaptation to extreme diets, such as scavenging.	2018.0	Acta veterinaria Scandinavica	YES		METAGENOMIC	YES	Animalia	Chordata	Cathartiformes	Cathartidae	Coragyps	atratus	10.1186/s13028-018-0415-3				WGS:80	80	0			bird metagenome	vulture face; vulture gut	Metagenome or environmental sample from bird metagenome	80.0	https://doi.org/10.1186/s13028-018-0415-3	True	True	vulture	True	vulture	True	black vulture (Coragyps atratus); turkey vulture (Cathartes aura)	False		False	ENA + paper	Shotgun DNA sequencing was performed on facial skin and large intestine microbiome samples from black vultures and turkey vultures.	UNIFORM	80	METAGENOMIC:80	80	80	KEEP	yes	high	gut_explicit	multi_host	Coragyps atratus and Cathartes aura (black vulture and turkey vulture)	False	agree	GUT: yes (high) — Abstract explicitly states 'shotgun DNA sequencing datasets of the facial skin and large intestine microbiomes' and 'Isolation source(s): vulture gut' is present in metadata. Large intestine is part of the gut. || BODY_SITE: gut_explicit — Abstract explicitly mentions 'large intestine microbiomes' and isolation source lists 'vulture gut'. Title also states 'gut microbiomes'. Note: study also includes facial skin, but gut samples are explicitly present and named. || HOST: Coragyps atratus and Cathartes aura (black vulture and turkey vulture) [multi_host] — Abstract states 'we generated shotgun DNA sequencing datasets...of the black vulture (Coragyps atratus) and the turkey vulture (Cathartes aura).' This is a genuine comparative study of two distinct vulture species' microbiomes, not incidental sampling. || METHOD: agree — ENA metadata shows 'library_strategy_mix: WGS:80' (80 shotgun samples) and 'library_source: METAGENOMIC' (community DNA), matching the stated 'shotgun DNA sequencing datasets' in the abstract. || SUMMARY: This is a valid multi-host animal gut microbiome study using WGS metagenomics. Two vulture species' large intestine microbiomes are explicitly sampled and characterized alongside facial skin microbiomes. All evidence is concordant.	gut_sample_issue; multihost_issue			Isolation sources list 'vulture face' and 'vulture gut'. Facial skin is a non-gut surface sample.	Paper describes two distinct vulture species: Coragyps atratus (black vulture) and Cathartes aura (turkey vulture). Deposit host field shows only generic 'vulture' but isolation sources ('vulture face', 'vulture gut') do not explicitly disambiguate which species each sample came from at the sample level.	Study includes non-gut body sites (facial skin) alongside gut samples, and the deposit lacks sample-level species disambiguation between the two vulture species mentioned in the paper.	1		False		True	True
PRJNA1067415	Cotesia vestalis	217443.0		China	3	WGS	pubmed_postdoc	39248477.0	39248477.0	Characterization of larval gut microbiota of two endoparasitoid wasps associated with their common host, Plutella xylostella (Linnaeus) (Lepidoptera: Plutellidae).	Hu N-n	Chen X-x	Insecta	Insect gut microbes play important roles in digestion, metabolism, development, and environmental adaptation. Parasitoid wasps are one of the most important biological control agents in pest control, while the gut microbial species compositions and the associated functions have been poorly investigated. Two endoparasitoid wasps, Cotesia vestalis and Diadromus collaris, parasitize the larval stage and pupal stage of the diamondback moth, Plutella xylostella, respectively. Using whole-genome shotgun metagenomic sequencing, we characterized the gut microbial composition, diversity, and potential functional roles associated with the two parasitoid wasp larvae. The results reveal that Proteobacteria and Firmicutes are the dominant phyla in the gut of C. vestalis and D. collaris larvae, with Rhizobium and Enterococcus being the dominant genera. The putative microbial functions associated with the two parasitoid wasps might play a virtual role in assisting in consuming the host's nutritional composition. The enriched CAZymes family genes are primarily involved in the degradation and synthesis of chitin. Despite the richness of microbial species and communities, the microbes species and the microbial community structure exhibit significant similarity between the two parasitoid wasps and between the parasitoid wasp and the host P. xylostella. Notably, the prevalence of the genus Enterococcus shared among them suggests a possible link of gut microbes between the host and their associated parasitoids. Our study offers insights into the gut microbe-based interactions between the host and parasitoid wasps for the first time, potentially paving the way for the development of an ecologically friendly biocontrol strategy against the pest P. xylostella.IMPORTANCEEndoparasitoid wasps spend the majority of their lifespan within their host and heavily rely on the host's nutrition for survival. There is limited understanding regarding the composition and physiological impacts of gut microbial communities in parasitoid wasps, particularly during the larval stage, which is directly linked to the host. Based on a thorough characterization of the gut microbe and comprehensive comparative analysis, we found the microbial species of the larval parasitoid wasp Cotesia vestalis and the pupal parasitoid wasp Diadromus collaris were similar, sharing 159 genera and 277 species, as were the microbial community structure. Certain of the dominant microbial strains of the two parasitoid wasps were similar to that of their host Plutella xylostella larvae, revealing host insect may affect the microbial community of the parasitoid wasps. The putative microbial functions associated with the parasitoid wasp larvae play an important role in dietary consumption.	2024.0	Microbiology spectrum	YES		METAGENOMIC	YES	Metazoa	Arthropoda	Hymenoptera	Braconidae	Cotesia	Cotesia vestalis	10.1128/spectrum.01208-24				WGS:3	3	1	Cotesia vestalis		insect metagenome	insect	insect metagenome 1; insect metagenome 2; insect metagenome 3	3.0	https://doi.org/10.1128/spectrum.01208-24	True	True	Cotesia vestalis	True	Cotesia vestalis; Diadromus collaris	True	Cotesia vestalis; Diadromus collaris; Plutella xylostella	False		False	ENA + paper	Metagenomic sequencing of gut microbiota from larvae of two parasitoid wasp species and comparative analysis with their host P. xylostella.	UNIFORM	3	METAGENOMIC:3	3	3	KEEP	yes	high	gut_explicit	multi_host	Cotesia vestalis; Diadromus collaris	False	agree	GUT: yes (high) — Paper explicitly studies 'larval gut microbiota' of two parasitoid wasps using 'whole-genome shotgun metagenomic sequencing' to characterize 'gut microbial composition'. Abstract states 'The results reveal that Proteobacteria and Firmicutes are the dominant phyla in the gut of C. vestalis and D. collaris larvae.' || BODY_SITE: gut_explicit — Title explicitly names 'larval gut microbiota'. Abstract repeatedly references 'gut' of the parasitoid wasp larvae. Isolation source listed as 'insect' with biome label 'insect metagenome' and no body_site field contradiction. || HOST: Cotesia vestalis; Diadromus collaris [multi_host] — Paper title and abstract describe a comparative study 'of two endoparasitoid wasps': 'Cotesia vestalis and Diadromus collaris'. Host field lists only 'Cotesia vestalis', but this is a multi-host comparative study by design. Host species in samples shows 1 distinct value, likely reflecting the primary submission focus on C. vestalis (WGS samples: 3), but the study explicitly characterizes both wasps' gut microbiota. || METHOD: agree — Paper states 'whole-genome shotgun metagenomic sequencing'. ENA library_strategy_mix shows 'WGS:3' and library_source is 'METAGENOMIC', consistent with stated shotgun metagenomic approach on community DNA. || SUMMARY: This is a genuine multi-host animal gut microbiome study using WGS metagenomic sequencing to characterize parasitoid wasp larval microbiota. The submission focuses on C. vestalis (3 samples), but the paper explicitly compares two wasp species' guts; both are valid animal hosts.	none					No genuine reporting errors detected. Deposit host field captures C. vestalis; D. collaris underrepresented in metadata but not a direct contradiction of deposit values.	2	0.0	False		True	True
PRJNA1086397	Mus musculus	10090.0		USA: Maryland, Bethesda	184	WGS	pubmed_postdoc	38807988.0	38807988.0	Effects of combined ciprofloxacin and Neulasta therapy on intestinal pathology and gut microbiota after high-dose irradiation in mice.	Horseman TS	Burmeister DM	Mammalia	INTRODUCTION: Treatments that currently exist in the strategic national stockpile for acute radiation syndrome (ARS) focus on the hematopoietic subsyndrome, with no treatments on gastrointestinal (GI)-ARS. While the gut microbiota helps maintain host homeostasis by mediating GI epithelial and mucosal integrity, radiation exposure can alter gut commensal microbiota which may leave the host susceptible to opportunistic pathogens and serious sequelae such as sepsis. To mitigate the effects of hematopoietic ARS irradiation, currently approved treatments exist in the form of colony stimulating factors and antibiotics: however, there are few studies examining how these therapeutics affect GI-ARS and the gut microbiota. The aim of our study was to examine the longitudinal effects of Neulasta and/or ciprofloxacin treatment on the gut microbiota after exposure to 9.5 Gy 60Co gamma-radiation in mice. METHODS: The gut microbiota of vehicle and drug-treated mice exposed to sham or gamma-radiation was characterized by shotgun sequencing with alpha diversity, beta diversity, and taxonomy analyzed on days 2, 4, 9, and 15 post-irradiation. RESULTS: No significant alpha diversity differences were observed following radiation, while beta diversity shifts and taxonomic profiles revealed significant alterations in Akkermansia, Bacteroides, and Lactobacillus. Ciprofloxacin generally led to lower Shannon diversity and Bacteroides prevalence with increases in Akkermansia and Lactobacillus compared to vehicle treated and irradiated mice. While Neulasta increased Shannon diversity and by day 9 had more similar taxonomic profiles to sham than ciprofloxacin-or vehicle-treated irradiated animals. Combined therapy of Neulasta and ciprofloxacin induced a decrease in Shannon diversity and resulted in unique taxonomic profiles early post-irradiation, returning closer to vehicle-treated levels over time, but persistent increases in Akkermansia and Bacteroides compared to Neulasta alone. DISCUSSION: This study provides a framework for the identification of microbial elements that may influence radiosensitivity, biodosimetry and the efficacy of potential therapeutics. Moreover, increased survival from H-ARS using these therapeutics may affect the symptoms and appearance of what may have been subclinical GI-ARS.	2024.0	Frontiers in public health	YES		METAGENOMIC	YES	Metazoa	Chordata	Rodentia	Muridae	Mus	Mus musculus	10.3389/fpubh.2024.1365161				WGS:184	184	1	Mus musculus		gut metagenome	Fecal pellet	Metagenome or environmental sample from gut metagenome	184.0	https://doi.org/10.3389/fpubh.2024.1365161	True	True	Mus musculus	True	mouse (model of acute radiation syndrome)	True	mouse (model of acute radiation syndrome)	True	acute radiation syndrome	False	ENA + paper	[model of acute radiation syndrome] Gut microbiota was directly sequenced from mice exposed to gamma-radiation and treated with ciprofloxacin and/or Neulasta.	UNIFORM	184	METAGENOMIC:184	184	184	KEEP	yes	high	gut_explicit	single_clear	Mus musculus	False	agree	GUT: yes (high) — Paper title explicitly states 'gut microbiota after high-dose irradiation in mice'. Abstract focuses on 'gut microbiota' and 'GI epithelial and mucosal integrity'. Biome label is 'gut metagenome'. Isolation source is 'Fecal pellet', the standard sample type for gut microbiota studies. || BODY_SITE: gut_explicit — Biome label explicitly states 'gut metagenome'. Isolation source explicitly states 'Fecal pellet'. Sample titles reference 'gut metagenome'. Paper title and abstract both focus on intestinal pathology and gut microbiota effects. || HOST: Mus musculus [single_clear] — Host field explicitly states 'Mus musculus'. Host species in samples lists one distinc