PRJNA932553	Homo sapiens	9606.0		Hong Kong	48	WGS	pubmed_postdoc	41163128.0	41163128.0	Comprehensive metagenomic analysis of the giant panda's oral microbiome reveals distinct taxonomic and functional characteristics.	Xiao LF	Shao NY	Mammalia	UNLABELLED: Host-associated microbiomes play a crucial role in various biological processes, including host physiology, health, and evolution. However, despite its importance, the oral microbiome of wild animals such as the giant panda (Ailuropoda melanoleuca), remains poorly studied. Here we presented the initial comprehensive metagenomic analysis of the giant panda’s oral microbiome using shotgun sequencing, generating the valuable data for this endangered species. Our study included 118 samples, comprising 28 supragingival samples (11 giant pandas and 3 Asiatic black bears (Ursus thibetanus), also known as a moon bear, collected by us, along with 14 human (Homo sapiens) samples originating from public datasets.) as well as 90 fecal samples from public databases. Our findings indicated that the oral and gut microbiomes of Ursidae were different from those of humans, both taxonomically and functionally. These results suggest that phylogenetic factors, rather than ecology, play an important role in shaping the oral and gut microbiomes (phylosymbiosis). Notably, periodontal opportunistic pathogens in humans coexisted with giant pandas, other bears and gorillas without dental issues. This suggests immune tolerance mechanisms may increase with long-term coevolution in host-microbiome, enabling pathogen coexistence without inflammatory harm. Lastly, giant pandas exhibited a high abundance of nitrate-reducing oral taxa, which may contribute to their adaptation to the environment and promoting oral health. This initial study not only enhances our understanding of host-microbiome interactions but also demonstrates the importance of metagenomic approaches in conservation biology, which could contribute to the long-term conservation of giant pandas. SUPPLEMENTARY INFORMATION: The online version contains supplementary material available at 10.1186/s42523-025-00475-z.	2025.0	Animal microbiome	YES		METAGENOMIC	YES	Metazoa	Chordata	Primates	Hominidae	Homo	Homo sapiens	10.1186/s42523-025-00475-z				WGS:48	48	1	Homo sapiens		human oral metagenome		MIMARKS Survey related sample from human oral metagenome	48.0	https://doi.org/10.1186/s42523-025-00475-z	True	True	Homo sapiens	True	giant panda	True	giant panda; Asiatic black bear; human	False		False	ENA + paper	Oral (supragingival) and fecal samples from wild giant pandas, Asiatic black bears, and humans	UNIFORM	48	METAGENOMIC:48	48	48	REVIEW						False		GUT:  () —  || BODY_SITE:  —  || HOST:  [] —  || METHOD:  —  || SUMMARY: PARSE_ERROR	host_mismatch; gut_sample_issue	paper: Ailuropoda melanoleuca (giant panda), Ursus thibetanus (Asiatic black bear), Homo sapiens (human), and gorillas vs deposit: Homo sapiens		Sample titles reference 'human oral metagenome' and biome labels show 'human oral metagenome'. The paper states 118 samples total: 28 supragingival (oral) samples and 90 fecal samples. The deposit metadata explicitly identifies samples as oral, not gut/fecal.		The deposit (Homo sapiens oral samples only) does not represent the full multi-host scope described in the paper (giant pandas, bears, humans, gorillas), and sample-level metadata indicates oral rather than fecal samples, creating both host and sample-type discrepancies.	1		False		True	True
PRJDB20286	Locusta migratoria	7004.0		Japan	14	WGS	pubmed_postdoc	41696020.0	41696020.0	Behavioural phase transitions in the migratory locust, Locusta migratoria, are related to changes in the gut bacterial composition.	Kim J	Mori H	Insecta	Locusta migratoria is a grasshopper species that can change its behaviour from solitary to gregarious. Previous studies have implicated metabolites such as serotonin and dopamine in the regulation of behavioural transition in this species. While many studies using cultured microbes have demonstrated that some microbes harbor the neuroactive metabolic potential of these neurotransmitters, the association between microbial community composition and phase transition remains poorly understood. Here, we employed 16S rRNA gene amplicon sequencing and shotgun metagenomic sequencing analyses to compare the composition of gut microbial communities of L. migratoria in different behavioural phases. We found that Serratia ureilytica was enriched in the gut of gregarious individuals in contrast to the decreased presence of Klebsiella aerogenes, one of the most abundant taxa in wild individuals. The gut microbiome of gregarious individuals was functionally characterised by enriched kynurenine and tryptophan synthesis pathways, and by reduced representation of GABA, indole, and dopamine metabolism pathways compared with that of solitary individuals. These compositional changes were consistent with the enrichment of S. ureilytica and depletion of K. aerogenes, which possess the corresponding genes. In particular, the genes for kynurenine synthesis encoded by S. ureilytica specific to the gregarious phase, are known to be involved in the tryptophan production and are associated with reduced serotonin synthesis. These results highlight a distinct shift in both the taxonomic and functional composition of the gut microbiome across behavioural phases and suggest a potential microbial contribution to the behavioural changes of L. migratoria.	2026.0	ISME communications	YES		METAGENOMIC	YES	Metazoa	Arthropoda	Orthoptera	Acrididae	Locusta	Locusta migratoria	10.1093/ismeco/ycag009				WGS:14	14	0			insect gut metagenome		hindgut metagenome sample of the grasshopper Lab-23-Gre-Female-2; hindgut metagenome sample of the grasshopper Lab-23-Gre-Female-4; hindgut metagenome sample of the grasshopper Lab-23-Gre-Male-3; hindgut metagenome sample of the grasshopper Lab-23-Gre-Male-4; hindgut metagenome sample of the grasshopper Lab-23-Sol-Female-1	14.0	https://doi.org/10.1093/ismeco/ycag009	True	True	Locusta migratoria	True	Locusta migratoria	True	Locusta migratoria	False		False	ENA + paper	Gut microbial samples were sequenced directly from Locusta migratoria individuals in different behavioural phases using 16S rRNA and shotgun metagenomic sequencing.	UNIFORM	14	METAGENOMIC:14	14	14	KEEP	yes	high	gut_explicit	single_clear	Locusta migratoria	False	agree	GUT: yes (high) — Abstract explicitly states 'shotgun metagenomic sequencing analyses to compare the composition of gut microbial communities of L. migratoria'. Library_source is METAGENOMIC (community DNA). Biome label: 'insect gut metagenome'. Sample titles reference 'hindgut metagenome sample'. || BODY_SITE: gut_explicit — Sample titles explicitly state 'hindgut metagenome sample'. Abstract mentions 'gut microbial communities' and 'gut of gregarious individuals'. No ambiguity about body site. || HOST: Locusta migratoria [single_clear] — Host field states 'Locusta migratoria'. Paper title and abstract both confirm this locust species as the single host organism. Sample titles reference 'the grasshopper' (L. migratoria). Study compares behavioural phases within this single species, not a multi-host survey. || METHOD: agree — Abstract states 'shotgun metagenomic sequencing analyses'. ENA field library_strategy_mix shows 'WGS:14' (14 whole-genome shotgun samples). Library_source is METAGENOMIC. No mismatch between stated method and ENA metadata. || SUMMARY: This is a genuine single-host animal gut microbiome study using shotgun metagenomics to investigate how behavioural phase transitions in locusts correlate with changes in hindgut bacterial composition. The evidence is consistent and complete.	none					No reporting errors detected. Host, sample type, and all metadata are consistent between paper and deposit.	2	0.0	False		True	False
PRJNA953147	Acutalibacter muris			China: Beijing	20	WGS	pubmed_postdoc	37752615.0	37752615.0	The gut metabolite 3-hydroxyphenylacetic acid rejuvenates spermatogenic dysfunction in aged mice through GPX4-mediated ferroptosis.	Jin Z	Zhang Z	Mammalia	BACKGROUND: Aging-related fertility decline is a prevalent concern globally. Male reproductive system aging is mainly characterized by a decrease in sperm quality and fertility. While it is known that intestinal physiology changes with age and that microbiota is shaped by physiology, the underlying mechanism of how the microbiota affects male reproductive aging is still largely unexplored. RESULTS: Here, we utilized fecal microbiota transplantation (FMT) to exchange the fecal microbiota between young and old mice. Cecal shotgun metagenomics and metabolomics were used to identify differences in gut microbiota composition and metabolic regulation during aging. Our results demonstrated that FMT from young to old mice alleviated aging-associated spermatogenic dysfunction through an unexpected mechanism mediated by a gut bacteria-derived metabolite, 3-hydroxyphenylacetic acid (3-HPAA). 3-HPAA treatment resulted in an improvement of spermatogenesis in old mice. RNA sequencing analysis, qRT-PCR and Western blot revealed that 3-HPAA induced an upregulation of GPX4, thereby restraining ferroptosis and restoring spermatogenesis. These findings were further confirmed by in vitro induction of ferroptosis and inhibition of GPX4 expression. CONCLUSIONS: Our results demonstrate that the microbiome-derived metabolite, 3-HPAA, facilitates spermatogenesis of old mice through a ferroptosis-mediated mechanism. Overall, these findings provide a novel mechanism of dysregulated spermatogenesis of old mice, and suggest that 3-HPAA could be a potential therapy for fertility decline of aging males in clinical practice. Video Abstract.	2023.0	Microbiome	YES		METAGENOMIC	YES	Animalia	Chordata	Rodentia	Muridae	Mus	musculus	10.1186/s40168-023-01659-y				WGS:20	20	0			Acutalibacter muris; Helicobacter typhlonius	Gut	Microbe sample from Acutalibacter muris; Microbe sample from Helicobacter typhlonius	20.0	https://doi.org/10.1186/s40168-023-01659-y	True	True	Acutalibacter muris	True	aged mice	True	mouse (model of spermatogenic dysfunction)	True	spermatogenic dysfunction	False	ENA + paper	[model of spermatogenic dysfunction] Cecal shotgun metagenomics was performed on young and old mice to identify microbiota and metabolite differences.	DIVERSE	20	METAGENOMIC:20	20	20	KEEP	yes	high	gut_explicit	unclear	mice (Mus musculus) - inferred	False	agree	GUT: yes (high) — Paper title and abstract explicitly describe a gut microbiome study in mice using fecal microbiota transplantation (FMT) and cecal shotgun metagenomics to investigate microbiota composition and metabolic regulation during aging. || BODY_SITE: gut_explicit — Isolation source field states 'Gut'; abstract explicitly mentions 'cecal shotgun metagenomics'; sample titles reference microbiota samples (not host tissue); FMT involves fecal/cecal material. || HOST: mice (Mus musculus) - inferred [unclear] — Paper title and abstract unambiguously state the study organism is 'aged mice' and 'young and old mice'. However, the ENA Host field is populated with 'Acutalibacter muris' (a bacterium, not a host organism) and biome labels list bacterial taxa (Acutalibacter muris, Helicobacter typhlonius). This is a metadata error: the host should be 'Mus musculus' or 'mouse', not bacterial species names. The actual host is inferred from the paper text as mice, but the structured ENA fields are incorrect/corrupted. || METHOD: agree — Paper describes 'Cecal shotgun metagenomics' (WGS); ENA records show library_strategy_mix WGS:20 with 20 WGS shotgun samples and library_source METAGENOMIC, which correctly represents community DNA sequencing from gut samples. || SUMMARY: This is a genuine animal (mouse) gut microbiome study using shotgun metagenomics on cecal/fecal material as part of an FMT aging experiment. The ENA metadata contains a significant error: bacterial taxa names appear in the Host field instead of the actual mouse host organism, but the isolation source, library strategy, and paper evidence all confirm this is a valid gut metagenomics study.	none					No genuine reporting errors detected. The deposit has a metadata annotation error (bacteria listed as host instead of animal host), but the underlying sample provenance (mouse gut microbiota) is correct and consistent with the paper describing an aged-mice FMT study with cecal metagenomics.	1		False		True	False
PRJEB81709	Acrocephalus sechellensis			Seychelles	173	WGS	pubmed_postdoc	41814421.0	41814421.0	Host immunogenetic variation and gut microbiome functionality in a wild vertebrate population.	Lee CZ	Richardson DS	Aves	BACKGROUND: The gut microbiome (GM) -important for host health and survival- is partially shaped by host immunogenetics. However, to date, no study has investigated the influence of host Major Histocompatibility Complex (MHC) genes on gut microbiome functionality in a wild population. Here we use a natural population of the Seychelles warbler (Acrocephalus sechellensis) to assess the effects of MHC genes on GM taxonomy and functionality using shotgun metagenomics. RESULTS: Our results show that taxonomic GM composition was associated with MHC-II diversity and the presence of one specific MHC-I allele (Ase-ua 7). Specifically, MHC-II diversity was associated with decreased Lactococcus lactis and increased Staphylococcus lloydii abundance, while Ase-ua 7 was linked to reduced Enterococcus casselifavus and Gordonia sp OPL2 but increased Escherichia coli and Vulcaniibacterium thermophilum. These taxonomic changes may reflect differences in MHC-mediated microbial recognition. In contrast, functional GM composition was significantly associated with increasing individual MHC-I diversity but not MHC-II diversity. In particular, increasing MHC-I diversity was associated with an increased prevalence of microbial defence genes but a reduced prevalence of microbial metabolism genes. Analysis also revealed that functional GM networks were more fragmented in high compared to low MHC-I diversity hosts. CONCLUSION: These results suggest that MHC variation (particularly at MHC-I) plays an important role in shaping both the taxonomy and function of the GM in wild vertebrates. In the Seychelles warbler, this results in trade-offs whereby there is an increase in microbial defence and a reduction in GM metabolic potential in individuals with higher MHC-I diversity. Thus, this work sheds light on the possible costs and benefits of maintaining a healthy microbiome, which is essential for understanding how the GM and immune system co-evolve. Video Abstract.	2026.0	Microbiome	YES		METAGENOMIC	YES	Animalia	Chordata	Passeriformes	Acrocephalidae	Acrocephalus	sechellensis	10.1186/s40168-026-02341-9				WGS:173	173	0			Acrocephalus sechellensis		Seychelles warbler gut microbiome sample	173.0	https://doi.org/10.1186/s40168-026-02341-9	True	True	Acrocephalus sechellensis	False		True	Seychelles warbler (Acrocephalus sechellensis)	False		False	ENA + paper	Shotgun metagenomic sequencing was performed on gut microbiome samples from a wild population of Seychelles warblers.	UNIFORM	173	METAGENOMIC:173	173	173	KEEP	yes	high	gut_explicit	single_clear	Acrocephalus sechellensis	False	agree	GUT: yes (high) — Paper title explicitly states 'gut microbiome' study. Abstract confirms 'gut microbiome (GM)' investigation using 'shotgun metagenomics' in wild Seychelles warbler population. Sample titles reference 'gut microbiome sample'. Library source is METAGENOMIC (community DNA), consistent with microbiome study. || BODY_SITE: gut_explicit — Title contains 'gut microbiome'. Abstract repeatedly references 'gut microbiome' and 'GM'. Sample titles explicitly state 'Seychelles warbler gut microbiome sample'. No conflicting body site mentioned. || HOST: Acrocephalus sechellensis [single_clear] — Host field clearly states 'Acrocephalus sechellensis' (Seychelles warbler). Paper title and abstract consistently refer to 'a wild population' of this single species. Biome label confirms 'Acrocephalus sechellensis'. This is a comparative study OF one species' gut across individuals with varying MHC genotypes, not an incidental sample in a multi-host survey. || METHOD: agree — ENA library_strategy_mix reports 'WGS:173' with 173 shotgun metagenomics samples. Abstract states 'shotgun metagenomics' methodology. Library_source is METAGENOMIC. Methods align with stated approach. || SUMMARY: This is a rigorous single-host (Seychelles warbler) gut microbiome study using shotgun metagenomics to investigate immunogenetic influences on microbiome composition and function. All metadata consistently point to genuine gut community sampling in a wild bird population.	none					No reporting errors detected; host identity matches perfectly, and all metadata are consistent with a single-host wild bird gut microbiome study.	1		False		True	False
PRJNA753312	Bos taurus	10090.0		Bangladesh: Dhaka	24	WGS	pubmed_postdoc	35794639.0	35794639.0	Induction of mastitis by cow-to-mouse fecal and milk microbiota transplantation causes microbiome dysbiosis and genomic functional perturbation in mice.	Hoque MN	Hossain MA	Mammalia	BACKGROUND: Mastitis pathogenesis involves a wide range of opportunistic and apparently resident microorganims including bacteria, viruses and archaea. In dairy animals, microbes reside in the host, interact with environment and evade the host immune system, providing a potential for host-tropism to favor mastitis pathogenesis. To understand the host-tropism phenomena of bovine-tropic mastitis microbiomes, we developed a cow-to-mouse mastitis model. METHODS: A cow-to-mouse mastitis model was established by fecal microbiota transplantation (FMT) and milk microbiota transplantation (MMT) to pregnant mice to assess microbiome dysbiosis and genomic functional perturbations through shotgun whole metagenome sequencing (WMS) along with histopathological changes in mice mammary gland and colon tissues. RESULTS: The cow-to-mouse FMT and MMT from clinical mastitis (CM) cows induced mastitis syndromes in mice as evidenced by histopathological changes in mammary gland and colon tissues. The WMS of 24 samples including six milk (CM = 3, healthy; H = 3), six fecal (CM = 4, H = 2) samples from cows, and six fecal (CM = 4, H = 2) and six mammary tissue (CM = 3, H = 3) samples from mice generating 517.14 million reads (average: 21.55 million reads/sample) mapped to 2191 bacterial, 94 viral and 54 archaeal genomes. The Kruskal-Wallis test revealed significant differences (p = 0.009) in diversity, composition, and relative abundances in microbiomes between CM- and H-metagenomes. These differences in microbiome composition were mostly represented by Pseudomonas aeruginosa, Lactobacillus crispatus, Klebsiella oxytoca, Enterococcus faecalis, Pantoea dispersa in CM-cows (feces and milk), and Muribaculum spp., Duncaniella spp., Muribaculum intestinale, Bifidobacterium animalis, Escherichia coli, Staphylococcus aureus, Massilia oculi, Ralstonia pickettii in CM-mice (feces and mammary tissues). Different species of Clostridia, Bacteroida, Actinobacteria, Flavobacteriia and Betaproteobacteria had a strong co-occurrence and positive correlation as the indicator species of murine mastitis. However, both CM cows and mice shared few mastitis-associated microbial taxa (1.14%) and functional pathways regardless of conservation of mastitis syndromes, indicating the higher discrepancy in mastitis-associated microbiomes among lactating mammals. CONCLUSIONS: We successfully induced mastitis by FMT and MMT that resulted in microbiome dysbiosis and genomic functional perturbations in mice. This study induced mastitis in a mouse model through FMT and MMT, which might be useful for further studies- focused on pathogen(s) involved in mastitis, their cross-talk among themselves and the host.	2022.0	Animal microbiome	YES		METAGENOMIC	YES	Metazoa	Chordata	Rodentia	Muridae	Mus	Mus musculus	10.1186/s42523-022-00193-w				WGS:24	24	2	Bos taurus; Mus musculus		gut metagenome; milk metagenome; mouse metagenome	Mammary tissue; feces; milk	CCMF1; CCMF2; CCMF3; CCMF4; CCMM1	24.0	https://doi.org/10.1186/s42523-022-00193-w	True	True	Bos taurus	True	cow; mouse (model of mastitis)	True	cow; mouse (model of mastitis)	True	mastitis	False	ENA + paper	[model of mastitis] Sequenced samples came from both dairy cows (fecal and milk) and mice that received cow microbiota transplants.	DIVERSE	24	METAGENOMIC:24	24	24	KEEP	yes	high	gut_explicit	multi_host	Bos taurus (cow) and Mus musculus (mouse)	False	agree	GUT: yes (high) — Study includes fecal microbiota transplantation (FMT) and explicitly states 'gut metagenome' in biome labels. Feces is a gut-derived sample. Shotgun WGS metagenomic sequencing of community DNA confirms microbiome study design. || BODY_SITE: gut_explicit — Biome labels explicitly list 'gut metagenome'; isolation sources include 'feces' which is gut-derived material. Title and methods emphasize fecal microbiota transplantation (FMT) as primary intervention. || HOST: Bos taurus (cow) and Mus musculus (mouse) [multi_host] — Host field states 'Bos taurus'; host species in samples lists both 'Bos taurus' and 'Mus musculus' (2 distinct species). This is a genuine comparative/transplantation study OF multiple animal hosts' gut microbiomes, not an incidental sample. Study design transfers cow microbiota to mice to model mastitis. || METHOD: agree — Abstract states 'shotgun whole metagenome sequencing (WMS)' corresponding to WGS in ENA. Library source 'METAGENOMIC' confirms community DNA sequencing. Sample count of 24 WGS samples matches library_strategy_mix: WGS:24. || SUMMARY: This is a valid animal gut microbiome study employing WGS metagenomics on fecal samples from two animal species (cow and mouse). It is a multi-host comparative study where cow fecal microbiota are deliberately transplanted to mice, making both hosts genuine subjects of investigation rather than incidental.	none					No reporting errors detected; this is a correctly documented animal-model study with dual-host design (bovine donors and murine recipients) where deposit metadata appropriately reflects the experimental structure.	1		False		True	False
PRJEB88012				United Kingdom	24	WGS	pubmed_postdoc	40817325.0	40817325.0	Investigating microbial population structure and function in the chicken caeca and large intestine over time using metagenomics.	Star-Shirko B	Gundogdu O	Aves	OBJECTIVES: Although taxonomic variations in chicken gut microbiota have been previously documented, their functional capacity remain poorly understood. To gain a better understanding, we incorporated whole genome shotgun metagenomics to analyse microbial communities of two different organs: the caeca and the large intestine. RESULTS: Using 24 samples obtained from the caeca and the large intestine of commercial chickens, we assembled Metagenome-Assembled Genomes (MAGs) and characterise their functional profiles. Afterwards, using 8 samples, we integrated this sequencing data with chicken performance metadata body weight (BW), weight gain, feed intake (FI), feed conversion ratio (FCR) and age. MAGs belonging to specific families were found to be positively associated with changes in performance parameters. Functional analyses suggest changes in nutrient geochemical cycles including hydrogen generation within the carbon-cycle. Furthermore, 108 CAZymes were identified for MAGs belonging to two major families - glycoside hydrolase (GH) and polysaccharide lyase (PL), which are important for breakdown of dietary carbohydrates and fibres. A total of 13 polysaccharide lyases were identified functioning on day 20 with enzymes were specific to organs. Overall, our results provide a deeper understanding of microbial-mediated metabolism concerning key performance parameters in chicken production.	2025.0	BMC research notes	YES		METAGENOMIC	YES	Animalia	Chordata	Galliformes	Phasianidae	Gallus	Gallus gallus	10.1186/s13104-025-07441-7				WGS:24	24	0			metagenome		S1; S10; S11; S116; S12	24.0	https://doi.org/10.1186/s13104-025-07441-7	True	False		True	chicken	True	chicken	False		False	paper only	Metagenomics samples were sequenced from the caeca and large intestine of commercial chickens.	NO_HOST_DATA	0	METAGENOMIC:24	24	24	KEEP	yes	high	gut_explicit	single_clear	Gallus gallus (chicken)	False	agree	GUT: yes (high) — Title states 'chicken caeca and large intestine'; abstract explicitly describes 'microbial communities of two different organs: the caeca and the large intestine' from 'commercial chickens' using 'whole genome shotgun metagenomics.' Caeca and large intestine are recognized gut compartments. || BODY_SITE: gut_explicit — Title explicitly names 'chicken caeca and large intestine.' Abstract reiterates 'two different organs: the caeca and the large intestine.' Both are anatomically defined intestinal structures. || HOST: Gallus gallus (chicken) [single_clear] — Abstract states samples obtained 'from the caeca and the large intestine of commercial chickens.' All 24 WGS samples derive from chicken; no multi-host design or mixed sampling indicated. || METHOD: agree — ENA records library_strategy_mix as WGS:24 and library_source as METAGENOMIC. Abstract confirms 'whole genome shotgun metagenomics' applied to 24 samples from chicken gut organs. No contradiction. || SUMMARY: A straightforward, single-host (chicken) gut microbiome study using WGS metagenomics on caeca and large intestine samples. Title, abstract, and metadata are concordant; method (WGS) matches ENA classification.	host_missing		paper names: chicken (commercial chickens); deposit blank/generic			Host is missing from the deposit ENA metadata despite the paper explicitly sampling from commercial chickens.	1		False		True	False
PRJNA1234694	Diaprepres abbreviatus			Puerto Rico	9	WGS	pubmed_postdoc	41230492.0	41230492.0	Gut microbial community structure of the adult citrus root weevil Diaprepes abbreviatus.	Rodriguez-Fernandez IA	Toranzos GA	Insecta	Diaprepes abbreviatus is an agricultural pest known to affect around 270 plant species across the Caribbean and the United States, posing significant challenges to pest management. Chemical control dominates management, but environmental and health concerns motivate microbiome-informed alternatives. However, limited information exists on the gut anatomy, physicochemical environment, and microbial composition of D. abbreviatus. In this study, we provide the first comprehensive characterization of the gut morphology, pH, and microbiota of adult D. abbreviatus in both females and males collected in Puerto Rico. Using dye-based gut tracing, we identified foregut, midgut, and hindgut or posterior gut compartments, and confirmed the presence of a muscular, sclerotized gizzard. Colorimetric analysis revealed a mildly acidic gut environment (approximately pH 4-5, based on qualitative ranges), consistent across sexes and regions. Shotgun metagenomic sequencing of dissected guts from males and females revealed microbial communities distinct from the leaf samples microbiota. While alpha and beta diversity did not differ significantly between sexes, co-occurrence analyses identified sex-specific correlation patterns among bacterial taxa. Notably, Enterobacter cloacae, Pantoea vagans, Lactococcus lactis, and Pseudomonas monteilii were repeatedly detected across individuals and generated metagenomic datasets, and some were localized to the hindgut, suggesting possible niche specialization. The presence of taxa, such as Enterobacter cloacae, previously reported as symbionts in other phytophagous insects further supports the hypothesis that certain bacteria may contribute to host digestion or adaptation. These findings establish a framework for understanding the gut environment and microbial community of D. abbreviatus, and highlight candidate taxa for future functional studies. More broadly, this work supports further research into the potential roles of gut microbiota in the ecology and management of this pest.	2025.0	Frontiers in insect science	YES		METAGENOMIC	YES	Animalia	Arthropoda	Coleoptera	Curculionidae	Diaprepes	abbreviatus	10.3389/finsc.2025.1676003				WGS:9	9	0			metagenome		F311G1FMe_CKDN240004717-1A_HCHFJDSXC_L3_2.fq; F311G4MMe_CKDN240004713-1A_HCHFJDSXC_L2_2.fq; F311G5MMe_CKDN240004716-1A_HCHFJDSXC_L3_2.fq; F311M1FMe_CKDN240004714-1A_HCHJJDSXC_L3_2.fq; F311M2MMe_CKDN240004715-1A_HCHFJDSXC_L3_2.fq	9.0	https://doi.org/10.3389/finsc.2025.1676003	True	True	Diaprepres abbreviatus	True	Diaprepes abbreviatus	True	Diaprepes abbreviatus	False		False	ENA + paper	Shotgun metagenomic sequencing was performed on dissected guts from adult male and female D. abbreviatus collected in Puerto Rico.	UNIFORM	9	METAGENOMIC:9	9	9	KEEP	yes	high	gut_explicit	single_clear	Diaprepes abbreviatus	False	agree	GUT: yes (high) — Title explicitly states 'Gut microbial community structure of the adult citrus root weevil Diaprepes abbreviatus'. Abstract describes 'comprehensive characterization of the gut morphology, pH, and microbiota of adult D. abbreviatus' with identification of 'foregut, midgut, and hindgut or posterior gut compartments'. This is a genuine animal gut microbiome study. || BODY_SITE: gut_explicit — Title and abstract explicitly focus on gut: 'Gut microbial community structure', 'gut anatomy, physicochemical environment, and microbial composition', 'gut morphology', 'foregut, midgut, and hindgut'. No ambiguity; sampling site is unambiguously the digestive tract. || HOST: Diaprepes abbreviatus [single_clear] — Host field states 'Diaprepres abbreviatus' (minor spelling variant of title 'Diaprepes abbreviatus'). Abstract confirms sampling of 'adult D. abbreviatus in both females and males collected in Puerto Rico'. This is a single insect species; the sex differentiation (female/male) in sample titles does not constitute multiple hosts, but rather intra-host biological replication. || METHOD: agree — ENA fields show library_strategy_mix = WGS:9 (9 whole-genome shotgun samples), library_source = METAGENOMIC (community DNA), and biome label = metagenome. This aligns with a shotgun metagenomic study of gut microbial communities as described in title and abstract. || SUMMARY: This is a rigorous, single-host animal gut metagenomics study of the citrus root weevil. The gut microbiota is explicitly characterized via shotgun sequencing of 9 metagenomic samples (females and males), with clear anatomical documentation. No concerns regarding host identity, sampling site, or method misalignment.	host_mismatch	paper: Diaprepes abbreviatus vs deposit: Diaprepres abbreviatus				Spelling error in deposit host field ('Diaprepres' instead of 'Diaprepes') is the only issue, representing a metadata transcription error rather than a genuine biological mismatch.	2	0.0	False		True	False
PRJEB52043				France	97	WGS	pubmed_postdoc	37141097.0	37141097.0	MetaPhlAn 4 profiling of unknown species-level genome bins improves the characterization of diet-associated microbiome changes in mice.	Manghi P	Segata N	Mammalia	Mouse models are key tools for investigating host-microbiome interactions. However, shotgun metagenomics can only profile a limited fraction of the mouse gut microbiome. Here, we employ a metagenomic profiling method, MetaPhlAn 4, which exploits a large catalog of metagenome-assembled genomes (including 22,718 metagenome-assembled genomes from mice) to improve the profiling of the mouse gut microbiome. We combine 622 samples from eight public datasets and an additional cohort of 97 mouse microbiomes, and we assess the potential of MetaPhlAn 4 to better identify diet-related changes in the host microbiome using a meta-analysis approach. We find multiple, strong, and reproducible diet-related microbial biomarkers, largely increasing those identifiable by other available methods relying only on reference information. The strongest drivers of the diet-induced changes are uncharacterized and previously undetected taxa, confirming the importance of adopting metagenomic methods integrating metagenomic assemblies for comprehensive profiling.	2023.0	Cell reports	YES		METAGENOMIC	YES	Animalia	Chordata	Rodentia	Muridae	Mus	musculus	10.1016/j.celrep.2023.112464				WGS:97	97	0			mouse gut metagenome		ABX_COLI_16_CA; ABX_COLI_16_SI; ABX_COLI_17_CA; ABX_COLI_17_SI; ABX_COLI_20_CA	97.0	https://doi.org/10.1016/j.celrep.2023.112464	True	False		True	mice	True	mice	False		False	paper only	Sequenced samples came from 622 public mouse microbiome samples and 97 additional mouse microbiomes used in a meta-analysis of diet-associated changes.	NO_HOST_DATA	0	METAGENOMIC:97	97	97	KEEP	yes	high	gut_explicit	single_clear	Mus musculus (mouse)	False	agree	GUT: yes (high) — Paper title states 'diet-associated microbiome changes in mice'. Abstract explicitly states 'mouse gut microbiome' and 'we combine 622 samples from eight public datasets and an additional cohort of 97 mouse microbiomes'. Library_source is METAGENOMIC (community DNA). Biome label: 'mouse gut metagenome'. || BODY_SITE: gut_explicit — Paper title includes 'microbiome changes in mice'. Abstract states 'mouse gut microbiome' (stated twice). Biome label explicitly: 'mouse gut metagenome'. Sample isolation source inferred from context as gut. || HOST: Mus musculus (mouse) [single_clear] — Paper title: 'in mice'. Abstract: 'Mouse models are key tools' and 'mouse gut microbiome' and 'cohort of 97 mouse microbiomes'. All 97 WGS samples are from mice, no multi-host survey indicated. || METHOD: agree — ENA fields show library_strategy_mix WGS:97 and library_source METAGENOMIC. Paper describes shotgun metagenomics (WGS) of mouse gut community DNA using MetaPhlAn 4 profiling. Methods align with metadata. || SUMMARY: This is a genuine animal gut microbiome study of mice using shotgun metagenomics. A single host species (Mus musculus) is studied across 97 samples from an original cohort, combined with public datasets for meta-analysis of diet-related microbial changes.	none					This is a mouse model study with appropriately blank host metadata complemented by explicit 'mouse gut metagenome' biome labels; no reporting errors detected.	1		False		True	False
PRJNA1006316	Apis mellifera	7460.0		Canada: University of Guelph, Ontario	45	WGS	pubmed_postdoc	41390863.0	41390863.0	Neonicotinoid-induced signature dysbiosis identified via metagenomic sequencing of the honey bee gut microbiome.	Tran L	Guarna MM	Insecta	UNLABELLED: The Western honey bee (Apis mellifera) plays an essential role in agriculture around the world. In Canada, honey bees contribute up to $7 billion in economic value annually by pollinating crops and producing honey. However, since 2006–2007 North American beekeepers have lost more than a quarter of their colonies each winter. In recent years, the losses have been up to 50% in some regions. The causes of losses are complex, including the interacting effects of nutrition, pathogens, and pesticides. Although the bee gut microbiome plays a crucial role in colony health and disease, studies on the effects of agricultural pesticides on the bee microbial community are sparse. We report the use of shotgun metagenomic sequencing to investigate bee gut microbiota changes, or dysbiosis, in response to two neonicotinoid insecticides, clothianidin and thiamethoxam. Common dysbiosis signatures included an increase in Bifidobacterium spp. after chronic sublethal exposure and an increase in Apibacter adventoris after short-term acute exposure. Other dysbiosis signatures were unique to each compound, such as an increase in Snodgrassella alvi for clothianidin and a decrease in Lactobacillus spp. for thiamethoxam. These findings enhance our understanding of how the honey bee gut microbiome responds to stressors and highlight identifiable microbial profile signatures which underscores the potential utility of gut microbiome profiling as a bee health diagnostic tool. Access to timely and accurate bee health diagnosis will inform regulatory actions to decrease and mitigate exposure to stressors and will facilitate managing and improving bee health. SUPPLEMENTARY INFORMATION: The online version contains supplementary material available at 10.1038/s41598-025-30907-4.	2025.0	Scientific reports	YES		METAGENOMIC	YES	Metazoa	Arthropoda	Hymenoptera	Apidae	Apis	Apis mellifera	10.1038/s41598-025-30907-4				WGS:45	350	1	Apis mellifera		bee metagenome	gut microbiome	Metagenome or environmental sample from bee metagenome	45.0	https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-30907-4	True	True	Apis mellifera	True	honey bee	True	Apis mellifera	False		False	ENA + paper	Metagenomic sequencing was performed on gut samples collected from Western honey bees (Apis mellifera) exposed to neonicotinoid insecticides.	UNIFORM	350	METAGENOMIC:350	350	45	KEEP	yes	high	gut_explicit	single_clear	Apis mellifera	False	agree	GUT: yes (high) — Title states 'honey bee gut microbiome'; abstract explicitly describes 'shotgun metagenomic sequencing to investigate bee gut microbiota'; isolation_source field confirms 'gut microbiome'; library_source is METAGENOMIC (community DNA, not single organism genome). || BODY_SITE: gut_explicit — Isolation source explicitly states 'gut microbiome'; sample titles reference 'bee metagenome'; abstract focuses on 'bee gut microbiota changes, or dysbiosis'; no other body sites mentioned. || HOST: Apis mellifera [single_clear] — Host field and host species in samples both list single taxon 'Apis mellifera' (Western honey bee); paper title specifies same species; no multi-host comparative design evident. || METHOD: agree — Paper title and abstract state 'shotgun metagenomic sequencing'; ENA library_strategy_mix shows 'WGS:45' (whole genome shotgun); library_source is METAGENOMIC; 45 WGS samples match the shotgun metagenomic method described. || SUMMARY: This is a straightforward, single-host (Apis mellifera) gut microbiome study using shotgun WGS metagenomic sequencing to examine pesticide-induced dysbiosis. All four criteria are clearly supported by the evidence with high confidence.	none					No reporting errors detected; paper and deposit are fully concordant for host identity and sample type.	1		False		