PRJNA1161616				USA: Georgia	1	WGS	pubmed_postdoc	40326765.0	40326765.0	SourceApp: A Novel Metagenomic Source Tracking Tool that can Distinguish between Fecal Microbiomes Using Genome-To-Source Associations Benchmarked Against Mixed Input Spike-In Mesocosms.	Lindner BG	Konstantinidis KT		Methodologies utilizing metagenomics are attractive to fecal source tracking (FST) aims for assessing the presence and proportions of various fecal inputs simultaneously. Yet, compared to established culture- or PCR-based techniques, metagenomic approaches for these purposes are rarely benchmarked or contextualized for practice. We performed shotgun sequencing experiments (n = 35) of mesocosms constructed from the water of a well-studied recreational and drinking water reservoir spiked with various fecal (n = 6 animal sources, 3 wastewater sources, and 1 septage source) and synthetic microbiome spike-ins (n = 1) introduced at predetermined cell concentrations to simulate fecal pollution events of known composition. We built source-associated genome databases using publicly available reference genomes and metagenome assembled genomes (MAGs) recovered from short- and long-read sequencing of the fecal spike-ins, and then created an associated bioinformatic tool, called SourceApp, for inferring source attribution and apportionment by mapping the metagenomic data to these genome databases. SourceApp's performance varied substantially by source, with cows being underestimated due to under sampling of cow fecal microbiomes. Parameter tuning revealed sensitivity and specificity near 0.90 overall, which exceeded all alternative tools. SourceApp can assist researchers with analyzing and interpreting shotgun sequencing data and developing standard operating procedures on the frontiers of metagenomic FST.	2025.0	Environmental science & technology	NO	True	METAGENOMIC	YES							10.1021/acs.est.5c03603				WGS:1	1	0			freshwater metagenome			1.0	https://doi.org/10.1021/acs.est.5c03603	True	False		False		False		False		False	unknown	Sequenced samples came from mesocosms (water + fecal spike-ins), not from a living host organism.	NO_HOST_DATA	0	METAGENOMIC:1	1	1	EXCLUDE	no	high	non_gut	incidental	multiple animal fecal sources (6 animal sources, 3 wastewater, 1 septage)	True	agree	GUT: no (high) — This is a water metagenomics study. Samples are mesocosms constructed from 'water of a well-studied recreational and drinking water reservoir spiked with various fecal...inputs.' The library_source is METAGENOMIC, biome is 'freshwater metagenome', and there are no body_sites or isolation_sources listed. Fecal material was added as spike-ins to water to simulate pollution events; the study does not sample animal gut microbiomes directly. No host species are recorded. || BODY_SITE: non_gut — Samples are freshwater mesocosms (water-based systems), not gut tissue or fecal samples from living animals. Although fecal material was spiked in as a laboratory control, the study sampled the resulting water community, not an animal's gut microbiome. || HOST: multiple animal fecal sources (6 animal sources, 3 wastewater, 1 septage) [incidental] (HUMAN) — Fecal material from 6 animal sources and 3 wastewater sources (which may contain human feces) were used as spike-in controls in a water pollution-tracking study. No specific animals were sampled; fecal inputs were laboratory reagents to benchmark a source-tracking tool. The primary focus is the tool development and water metagenomics, not characterization of the animal or human fecal microbiomes themselves. || METHOD: agree — Paper explicitly states 'shotgun sequencing experiments (n = 35)' and ENA records library_strategy_mix as WGS:1 with library_source METAGENOMIC, consistent with whole-genome shotgun sequencing of a community DNA sample. || SUMMARY: This is a freshwater metagenomics methodology paper that used fecal spike-ins as positive controls to benchmark a source-tracking tool. It is not an animal gut microbiome study because no animal guts were sampled; fecal material served only as calibration material for a water pollution tracking benchmark. The study belongs in a water/environmental category, not animal gut.	none					No reporting errors detected. This is a freshwater mesocosm study benchmarking metagenomics for fecal source tracking; no live host sampling occurred, and deposit metadata correctly reflects this as a non-host environmental study.	1		False		True	False
PRJNA924548	Bovine			China:Shangqiu	15	WGS	pubmed_postdoc	37525231.0	37525231.0	Metagenomic analysis reveals the relationship between intestinal protozoan parasites and the intestinal microecological balance in calves.	Fu Y	Zhang L	Mammalia	BACKGROUND: A close connection between a protozoan parasite and the balance of the other gut microbes of the host has been demonstrated. The calves may be naturally co-infected with many parasites, and the co-effects of parasites on other intestinal microbes of calves remain unclear. This study aims to preliminarily reveal the relationship between intestinal parasites and other intestinal microbes in calves. METHODS: Fecal samples were collected from four calves with bloody diarrhea, four calves with watery diarrhea, and seven normal calves, and the microbial flora of the samples were analyzed by whole-genome sequencing. Protozoal parasites were detected in the metagenome sequences and identified using polymerase chain reaction (PCR). RESULTS: Cryptosporidium, Eimeria, Giardia, Blastocystis, and Entamoeba were detected by metagenomic analysis, and the identified species were Giardia duodenalis assemblage E, Cryptosporidium bovis, Cryptosporidium ryanae, Eimeria bovis, Eimeria subspherica, Entamoeba bovis, and Blastocystis ST2 and ST10. Metagenomic analysis showed that the intestinal microbes of calves with diarrhea were disordered, especially in calves with bloody diarrhea. Furthermore, different parasites show distinct relationships with the intestinal microecology. Cryptosporidium, Eimeria, and Giardia were negatively correlated with various intestinal bacteria but positively correlated with some fungi. However, Blastocystis and Entamoeba were positively associated with other gut microbes. Twenty-seven biomarkers not only were significantly enriched in bloody diarrhea, watery diarrhea, and normal calves but were also associated with Eimeria, Cryptosporidium, and Giardia. Only Eimeria showed a distinct relationship with seven genera of bacteria, which were significantly enriched in the healthy calves. All 18 genera of fungi were positively correlated with Cryptosporidium, Eimeria, and Giardia, which were also significantly enriched in calves with bloody diarrhea. Functional genes related to parasites and diseases were found mainly in fungi. CONCLUSIONS: This study revealed the relationship between intestinal protozoan parasites and the other calf gut microbiome. Different intestinal protozoan parasites have diametrically opposite effects on other gut microecology, which not only affects bacteria in the gut, but also is significantly related to fungi and archaea.	2023.0	Parasites & vectors	NO	True	METAGENOMIC	YES	Animalia	Chordata	Artiodactyla	Bovidae	Bos	taurus	10.1186/s13071-023-05877-z				WGS:15	15	0			gut metagenome	Stools of beef calves	Metagenome or environmental sample from gut metagenome	15.0	https://doi.org/10.1186/s13071-023-05877-z	True	True	Bovine	True	calf	True	calf	False		False	ENA + paper	Fecal samples were collected from calves with bloody diarrhea, watery diarrhea, and normal calves for metagenomic sequencing.	UNIFORM	15	METAGENOMIC:15	15	15	KEEP	yes	high	gut_explicit	single_clear	Bovine (Bos taurus, beef calf)	False	agree	GUT: yes (high) — Paper title states 'intestinal protozoan parasites and the intestinal microecological balance in calves'; abstract explicitly describes 'gut microbes of the host' and 'intestinal microbes in calves'; biome label is 'gut metagenome'; isolation source is 'Stools of beef calves'; library_source is METAGENOMIC (community DNA, not host genome). || BODY_SITE: gut_explicit — Paper title contains 'intestinal'; abstract repeatedly uses 'intestinal microbes', 'intestinal parasites', 'gut microbes'; biome label explicitly states 'gut metagenome'; isolation source specifies 'Stools'; sample titles reference 'gut metagenome'. || HOST: Bovine (Bos taurus, beef calf) [single_clear] — Host field states 'Bovine'; isolation source specifies 'beef calves'; abstract refers to 'calves' throughout; study compares calves with different diarrhea phenotypes (four with bloody diarrhea, four with watery diarrhea, seven normal) but all belong to the same host species (bovine)—this is a phenotypic survey within a single host species, not a multi-host comparative study. || METHOD: agree — Paper describes 'whole-genome sequencing' (WGS) in METHODS; ENA shows library_strategy_mix WGS:15 with 15 WGS/shotgun samples; library_source is METAGENOMIC; fecal metagenomic sequencing matches stated methodology. || SUMMARY: This is a genuine single-host-species animal gut microbiome study using WGS metagenomics on fecal samples from beef calves. The study investigates relationships between intestinal parasites and microbiota composition across phenotypic variants (diarrhea status) within calves, not across different host species.	none					No reporting errors detected; paper describes fecal samples from calves, deposit correctly identifies host as Bovine with gut samples.	1		False		True	False
PRJNA944265	Mouse			USA	11	WGS	pubmed_postdoc	38203712.0	38203712.0	Red Cabbage Juice-Mediated Gut Microbiota Modulation Improves Intestinal Epithelial Homeostasis and Ameliorates Colitis.	Jean Wilson E	Rachagani S	Mammalia	Gut microbiota plays a crucial role in inflammatory bowel diseases (IBD) and can potentially prevent IBD through microbial-derived metabolites, making it a promising therapeutic avenue. Recent evidence suggests that despite an unclear underlying mechanism, red cabbage juice (RCJ) alleviates Dextran Sodium Sulfate (DSS)-induced colitis in mice. Thus, the study aims to unravel the molecular mechanism by which RCJ modulates the gut microbiota to alleviate DSS-induced colitis in mice. Using C57BL/6J mice, we evaluated RCJ's protective role in DSS-induced colitis through two cycles of 3% DSS. Mice were daily gavaged with PBS or RCJ until the endpoint, and gut microbiota composition was analyzed via shotgun metagenomics. RCJ treatment significantly improved body weight (p ≤ 0.001), survival in mice (p < 0.001) and reduced disease activity index (DAI) scores. Further, RCJ improved colonic barrier integrity by enhancing the expression of protective colonic mucins (p < 0.001) and tight junction proteins (p ≤ 0.01) in RCJ + DSS-treated mice compared to the DSS group. Shotgun metagenomic analysis revealed an enrichment of short-chain fatty acids (SCFAs)-producing bacteria (p < 0.05), leading to increased Peroxisome Proliferator-Activated Receptor Gamma (PPAR-γ) activation (p ≤ 0.001). This, in turn, resulted in repression of the nuclear factor κB (NFκB) signaling pathway, causing decreased production of inflammatory cytokines and chemokines. Our study demonstrates colitis remission in a DSS-induced mouse model, showcasing RCJ as a potential modulator for gut microbiota and metabolites, with promising implications for IBD prevention and treatment.	2023.0	International journal of molecular sciences	YES		METAGENOMIC	YES	Animalia	Chordata	Rodentia	Muridae	Mus	musculus	10.3390/ijms25010539				WGS:11	11	0			mouse gut metagenome	fecal samples	301; 305; 313; 318; 319	11.0	https://doi.org/10.3390/ijms25010539	True	True	Mouse	False		True	mouse (model of colitis)	True	colitis	False	ENA + paper	[model of colitis] Shotgun metagenomic sequencing was performed on gut microbiota samples from C57BL/6J mice treated with DSS-induced colitis and red cabbage juice.	UNIFORM	11	METAGENOMIC:11	11	11	KEEP	yes	high	gut_explicit	single_clear	Mus musculus (C57BL/6J)	False	agree	GUT: yes (high) — Abstract explicitly states 'gut microbiota composition was analyzed via shotgun metagenomics' in mice; biome label 'mouse gut metagenome'; isolation source 'fecal samples' (gut-derived) || BODY_SITE: gut_explicit — Title mentions 'Gut Microbiota'; abstract states 'gut microbiota composition was analyzed'; isolation source explicitly 'fecal samples'; biome label 'mouse gut metagenome' || HOST: Mus musculus (C57BL/6J) [single_clear] — Abstract states 'Using C57BL/6J mice' as the experimental model; host field confirms 'Mouse'; study is a controlled intervention trial with a single inbred mouse strain, not a multi-host survey || METHOD: agree — Abstract describes 'shotgun metagenomics' for gut microbiota analysis; ENA metadata confirms library_strategy_mix WGS:11, library_source METAGENOMIC, and 11 WGS samples; no contradictions detected || SUMMARY: This is a genuine single-host animal gut microbiome study using shotgun sequencing (WGS) of fecal samples from C57BL/6J mice to investigate red cabbage juice effects on DSS-induced colitis. All four criteria are clearly supported by the evidence provided.	none					No reporting errors detected. Mouse host in deposit correctly matches the paper's mouse-model study design; all samples are gut-derived (fecal).	1		False		True	False
PRJNA1297587	Diaprepes abbreviatus	13040.0		Puerto Rico: Florida	16	WGS	pubmed_postdoc	41230492.0	41230492.0	Gut microbial community structure of the adult citrus root weevil Diaprepes abbreviatus.	Rodriguez-Fernandez IA	Toranzos GA	Insecta	Diaprepes abbreviatus is an agricultural pest known to affect around 270 plant species across the Caribbean and the United States, posing significant challenges to pest management. Chemical control dominates management, but environmental and health concerns motivate microbiome-informed alternatives. However, limited information exists on the gut anatomy, physicochemical environment, and microbial composition of D. abbreviatus. In this study, we provide the first comprehensive characterization of the gut morphology, pH, and microbiota of adult D. abbreviatus in both females and males collected in Puerto Rico. Using dye-based gut tracing, we identified foregut, midgut, and hindgut or posterior gut compartments, and confirmed the presence of a muscular, sclerotized gizzard. Colorimetric analysis revealed a mildly acidic gut environment (approximately pH 4-5, based on qualitative ranges), consistent across sexes and regions. Shotgun metagenomic sequencing of dissected guts from males and females revealed microbial communities distinct from the leaf samples microbiota. While alpha and beta diversity did not differ significantly between sexes, co-occurrence analyses identified sex-specific correlation patterns among bacterial taxa. Notably, Enterobacter cloacae, Pantoea vagans, Lactococcus lactis, and Pseudomonas monteilii were repeatedly detected across individuals and generated metagenomic datasets, and some were localized to the hindgut, suggesting possible niche specialization. The presence of taxa, such as Enterobacter cloacae, previously reported as symbionts in other phytophagous insects further supports the hypothesis that certain bacteria may contribute to host digestion or adaptation. These findings establish a framework for understanding the gut environment and microbial community of D. abbreviatus, and highlight candidate taxa for future functional studies. More broadly, this work supports further research into the potential roles of gut microbiota in the ecology and management of this pest.	2025.0	Frontiers in insect science	YES		METAGENOMIC	YES	Metazoa	Arthropoda	Coleoptera	Curculionidae	Diaprepes	Diaprepes abbreviatus	10.3389/finsc.2025.1676003				WGS:16	16	2	Citrus x aurantiifolia; Diaprepes abbreviatus		insect gut metagenome; leaf metagenome	gut; leaves	Female Gut ID57; Female Gut ID58; Female Gut ID59; Female Gut ID63; Female Gut ID70	16.0	https://doi.org/10.3389/finsc.2025.1676003	True	True	Diaprepes abbreviatus	True	Diaprepes abbreviatus	True	Diaprepes abbreviatus	False		False	ENA + paper	Shotgun metagenomic sequencing was performed on dissected guts from adult citrus root weevils collected in Puerto Rico.	DIVERSE	16	METAGENOMIC:16	16	16	KEEP	yes	high	gut_explicit	single_clear	Diaprepes abbreviatus	False	agree	GUT: yes (high) — Paper title explicitly states 'Gut microbial community structure of the adult citrus root weevil Diaprepes abbreviatus'. Abstract confirms 'comprehensive characterization of the gut morphology, pH, and microbiota of adult D. abbreviatus' with identification of 'foregut, midgut, and hindgut or posterior gut compartments'. Isolation source lists 'gut' and library_source is 'METAGENOMIC' (community DNA). || BODY_SITE: gut_explicit — Isolation source field explicitly lists 'gut'. Sample titles reference 'Female Gut ID57', 'Female Gut ID58', etc. Abstract and methods describe gut compartments (foregut, midgut, hindgut). Biome label includes 'insect gut metagenome'. || HOST: Diaprepes abbreviatus [single_clear] — Host field lists 'Diaprepes abbreviatus'. Paper title and abstract focus exclusively on this weevil species as the subject of study. While 'Citrus x aurantiifolia' appears in host species list (likely the plant food source, not a sampled host), all 1