PRJNA297579	Gymnogyps californianus	33616.0		USA: Idaho	31	WGS	pubmed_postdoc	31825977.0	31825977.0	California condor microbiomes: Bacterial variety and functional properties in captive-bred individuals.	Jacobs L	Fair JM	Aves	Around the world, scavenging birds such as vultures and condors have been experiencing drastic population declines. Scavenging birds have a distinct digestive process to deal with higher amounts of bacteria in their primary diet of carcasses in varying levels of decay. These observations motivate us to present an analysis of captive and healthy California condor (Gymnogyps californianus) microbiomes to characterize a population raised together under similar conditions. Shotgun metagenomic DNA sequences were analyzed from fecal and cloacal samples of captive birds. Classification of shotgun DNA sequence data with peptide signatures using the Sequedex package provided both phylogenetic and functional profiles, as well as individually annotated reads for targeted confirmatory analysis. We observed bacterial species previously associated with birds and gut microbiomes, including both virulent and opportunistic pathogens such as Clostridium perfringens, Propionibacterium acnes, Shigella flexneri, and Fusobacterium mortiferum, common flora such as Lactobacillus johnsonii, Lactobacillus ruminus, and Bacteroides vulgatus, and mucosal microbes such as Delftia acidovorans, Stenotrophomonas maltophilia, and Corynebacterium falsnii. Classification using shotgun metagenomic reads from phylogenetic marker genes was consistent with, and more specific than, analysis based on 16S rDNA data. Classification of samples based on either phylogenetic or functional profiles of genomic fragments differentiated three types of samples: fecal, mature cloacal and immature cloacal, with immature birds having approximately 40% higher diversity of microbes.	2019.0	PloS one	YES		METAGENOMIC	YES	Metazoa	Chordata	Accipitriformes	Cathartidae	Gymnogyps	Gymnogyps californianus	10.1371/journal.pone.0225858				AMPLICON:19; WGS:12	13	1	Gymnogyps californianus		gut metagenome	World Center for Birds of Prey	CCP_1; CCP_10; CCP_11; CCP_12; CCP_13	31.0	https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0225858	True	True	Gymnogyps californianus	True	California condor	True	California condor (Gymnogyps californianus)	False		False	ENA + paper	Shotgun metagenomic DNA sequences were analyzed from fecal and cloacal samples of captive California condors.	UNIFORM	31	METAGENOMIC:31	31	12	KEEP	yes	high	gut_explicit	single_clear	Gymnogyps californianus (California condor)	False	agree	GUT: yes (high) — Abstract explicitly states 'analysis of captive and healthy California condor (Gymnogyps californianus) microbiomes' and 'Shotgun metagenomic DNA sequences were analyzed from fecal and cloacal samples'. Biome label confirms 'gut mebiome'. This is a genuine animal (bird) gut microbiome study using shotgun sequencing. || BODY_SITE: gut_explicit — Abstract states samples were 'fecal and cloacal samples' from captive condors. Cloacal is the gut exit in birds. Biome label explicitly lists 'gut metagenome'. || HOST: Gymnogyps californianus (California condor) [single_clear] — Host field specifies 'Gymnogyps californianus'; host species in samples lists only 1 distinct species (Gymnogyps californianus); sample titles (CCP_1, CCP_10, etc.) are from a single captive population at World Center for Birds of Prey. No multi-host sampling detected. || METHOD: agree — ENA library_strategy_mix shows WGS:12 samples (shotgun). Abstract confirms 'Shotgun metagenomic DNA sequences were analyzed'. library_source 'METAGENOMIC' aligns with community DNA from fecal/cloacal samples. The 19 AMPLICON samples are supplementary data not mentioned in the abstract excerpt but do not contradict the stated WGS focus. || SUMMARY: This is a straightforward, well-documented single-host (California condor) gut microbiome study using shotgun metagenomics on fecal and cloacal samples from captive-bred individuals. All four criteria align clearly with the evidence provided.	none					No reporting errors detected; paper and deposit are fully concordant on California condor as the single host species with appropriate gut samples.	1		False		True	False
PRJNA509211	Cryptotermes secundus	105785.0		Australia: Darwin, Northern Territory	28	WGS	pubmed_postdoc	33499940.0	33499940.0	Ecological specificity of the metagenome in a set of lower termite species supports contribution of the microbiome to adaptation of the host.	Waidele L	Staubach F	Insecta	BACKGROUND: Elucidating the interplay between hosts and their microbiomes in ecological adaptation has become a central theme in evolutionary biology. A textbook example of microbiome-mediated adaptation is the adaptation of lower termites to a wood-based diet, as they depend on their gut microbiome to digest wood. Lower termites have further adapted to different life types. Termites of the wood-dwelling life type never leave their nests and feed on a uniform diet. Termites of the foraging life type forage for food outside the nest and have access to other nutrients. Here we sought to investigate whether the microbiome that is involved in food substrate breakdown and nutrient acquisition might contribute to adaptation to these dietary differences. We reasoned that this should leave ecological imprints on the microbiome. RESULTS: We investigated the protist and bacterial microbiomes of a total of 29 replicate colonies from five termite species, covering both life types, using metagenomic shotgun sequencing. The microbiome of wood-dwelling species with a uniform wood diet was enriched for genes involved in lignocellulose degradation. Furthermore, metagenomic patterns suggest that the microbiome of wood-dwelling species relied primarily on direct fixation of atmospheric nitrogen, while the microbiome of foraging species entailed the necessary pathways to utilize nitrogen in the form of nitrate for example from soil. CONCLUSION: Our findings are consistent with the notion that the microbiome of wood-dwelling species bears an imprint of its specialization on degrading a uniform wood diet, while the microbiome of the foraging species might reflect its adaption to access growth limiting nutrients from more diverse sources. This supports the idea that specific subsets of functions encoded by the microbiome can contribute to host adaptation.	2019.0	Animal microbiome	YES		METAGENOMIC	YES	Metazoa	Arthropoda	Blattodea	Kalotermitidae	Cryptotermes	Cryptotermes secundus	10.1186/s42523-019-0014-2				WGS:28	28	5	Cryptotermes domesticus; Cryptotermes secundus; Prorhinotermes simplex; Reticulitermes flavipes; Reticulitermes grassei		termite gut metagenome	gut homogenate	Cryptotermes domesticus gut community colony replicate_2; Cryptotermes domesticus gut community colony replicate_3; Cryptotermes domesticus gut community colony replicate_4; Cryptotermes domesticus gut community colony replicate_5; Cryptotermes domesticus gut community colony replicate_6	28.0	https://doi.org/10.1186/s42523-019-0014-2	True	True	Cryptotermes secundus	True	lower termite species	True	termite species	False		False	ENA + paper	Metagenomic shotgun sequencing was performed on samples from 29 replicate colonies of five termite species.	DIVERSE	28	METAGENOMIC:28	28	28	KEEP	yes	high	gut_explicit	multi_host	five termite species (Cryptotermes domesticus, Cryptotermes secundus, Prorhinotermes simplex, Reticulitermes flavipes, Reticulitermes grassei)	False	agree	GUT: yes (high) — Paper title explicitly states 'gut microbiome' study; abstract describes 'gut microbiome to digest wood' in lower termites; library_source is METAGENOMIC (community DNA); isolation_source is 'gut homogenate'; biome label is 'termite gut metagenome' || BODY_SITE: gut_explicit — Isolation source stated as 'gut homogenate'; sample titles consistently reference 'gut community' (e.g., 'Cryptotermes domesticus gut community colony replicate_2'); biome label: 'termite gut metagenome' || HOST: five termite species (Cryptotermes domesticus, Cryptotermes secundus, Prorhinotermes simplex, Reticulitermes flavipes, Reticulitermes grassei) [multi_host] — Host field lists 'Cryptotermes secundus' as primary; however, 'Host species in samples' explicitly enumerates 5 distinct termite species sampled across the study. Abstract describes comparative investigation of microbiome differences across 'a set of lower termite species' adapted to different life types, indicating this is a genuine multi-species comparative gut study, not an incidental sampling scenario. || METHOD: agree — ENA fields show library_strategy_mix: WGS:28 with 28 shotgun samples; library_source is METAGENOMIC (community metagenomics); isolation source 'gut homogenate' matches WGS shotgun metagenomic sequencing of microbial communities from termite guts || SUMMARY: This is a rigorous comparative metagenomics study of gut microbial communities across five termite species, explicitly designed to investigate how microbiome composition relates to host ecological adaptation. All four criteria are clearly satisfied: genuine animal gut microbiome study, explicit gut sampling, multi-host comparative design, and consistent WGS metagenomic methodology.	multihost_issue				Deposit sample-level data (sample titles and host_species_list) explicitly names FIVE DISTINCT termite species: Cryptotermes domesticus, Cryptotermes secundus, Prorhinotermes simplex, Reticulitermes flavipes, Reticulitermes grassei. Paper title and abstract refer to 'a set of lower termite species' and 'five termite species', treating them as a comparative study, not as a single-host study.	No genuine reporting errors detected. The study properly compares microbiomes across five lower termite species; deposit and paper are consistent.	1		False		True	False
PRJNA271123	hot springs metagenome			Greece: Nea Apollonia, Thessaloniki	1	WGS	pubmed_postdoc	25973144.0	25973144.0	Under-detection of endospore-forming Firmicutes in metagenomic data.	Filippidou S	Junier P		Microbial diversity studies based on metagenomic sequencing have greatly enhanced our knowledge of the microbial world. However, one caveat is the fact that not all microorganisms are equally well detected, questioning the universality of this approach. Firmicutes are known to be a dominant bacterial group. Several Firmicutes species are endospore formers and this property makes them hardy in potentially harsh conditions, and thus likely to be present in a wide variety of environments, even as residents and not functional players. While metagenomic libraries can be expected to contain endospore formers, endospores are known to be resilient to many traditional methods of DNA isolation and thus potentially undetectable. In this study we evaluated the representation of endospore-forming Firmicutes in 73 published metagenomic datasets using two molecular markers unique to this bacterial group (spo0A and gpr). Both markers were notably absent in well-known habitats of Firmicutes such as soil, with spo0A found only in three mammalian gut microbiomes. A tailored DNA extraction method resulted in the detection of a large diversity of endospore-formers in amplicon sequencing of the 16S rRNA and spo0A genes. However, shotgun classification was still poor with only a minor fraction of the community assigned to Firmicutes. Thus, removing a specific bias in a molecular workflow improves detection in amplicon sequencing, but it was insufficient to overcome the limitations for detecting endospore-forming Firmicutes in whole-genome metagenomics. In conclusion, this study highlights the importance of understanding the specific methodological biases that can contribute to improve the universality of metagenomic approaches.	2015.0	Computational and structural biotechnology journal	YES		METAGENOMIC	YES							10.1016/j.csbj.2015.04.002				WGS:1	1	0			hot springs metagenome	thermal spring	Metagenome from Nea Apollonia natural hot spring	1.0	https://doi.org/10.1016/j.csbj.2015.04.002	True	False		False		False		False		False	unknown	This is a meta-analysis of 73 published metagenomic datasets from multiple environments; no new samples were sequenced from a single host organism.	NO_HOST_DATA	0	METAGENOMIC:1	1	1	EXCLUDE	no	high	non_gut	unclear	none (environmental)	False	agree	GUT: no (high) — Host field states 'hot springs metagenome'; isolation source is 'thermal spring'; sample title is 'Metagenome from Nea Apollonia natural hot spring'. This is an environmental (non-animal) metagenome study. No animal host is present. || BODY_SITE: non_gut — Samples sourced from natural hot springs thermal environment. No body site field populated. The study concerns environmental microbial communities, not organismal gut samples. || HOST: none (environmental) [unclear] — Host species field shows 0 distinct species with none listed. This is an environmental study of hot springs with no animal host. The 'host field' label 'hot springs metagenome' refers to the environment sampled, not a biological host organism. || METHOD: agree — ENA records WGS (shotgun) library_strategy with metagenomic source. Abstract describes 'metagenomic sequencing' methodology. Method and ENA classification are consistent. || SUMMARY: PRJNA271123 is an environmental metagenomics study of hot spring microbial communities, not an animal gut microbiome study. It should not be included in an animal gut microbiome catalog.	none					No reporting errors detected. This is a meta-analysis paper evaluating endospore-forming Firmicutes across 73 published datasets; the deposit represents one environmental sample (hot spring) appropriately labeled in the deposit metadata.	1		False		True	False
PRJNA750644	Pardosa agraria	2865751.0		China:Chongqing	6	WGS	pubmed_postdoc	34975785.0	34975785.0	Diversity and Function of Wolf Spider Gut Microbiota Revealed by Shotgun Metagenomics.	Wu R	Zhang Z	Arachnida	Wolf spiders (Lycosidae) are crucial component of integrated pest management programs and the characteristics of their gut microbiota are known to play important roles in improving fitness and survival of the host. However, there are only few studies of the gut microbiota among closely related species of wolf spider. Whether wolf spiders gut microbiota vary with habitats remains unknown. Here, we used shotgun metagenomic sequencing to compare the gut microbiota of two wolf spider species, Pardosa agraria and P. laura from farmland and woodland ecosystems, respectively. The results show that the gut microbiota of Pardosa spiders is similar in richness and abundance. Approximately 27.3% of the gut microbiota of P. agraria comprises Proteobacteria, and approximately 34.5% of the gut microbiota of P. laura comprises Firmicutes. We assembled microbial genomes and found that the gut microbiota of P. laura are enriched in genes for carbohydrate metabolism. In contrast, those of P. agraria showed a higher proportion of genes encoding acetyltransferase, an enzyme involved in resistance to antibiotics. We reconstructed three high-quality and species-level microbial genomes: Vulcaniibacterium thermophilum, Anoxybacillus flavithermus and an unknown bacterium belonging to the family Simkaniaceae. Our results contribute to an understanding of the diversity and function of gut microbiota in closely related spiders.	2021.0	Frontiers in microbiology	YES		METAGENOMIC	YES	Metazoa	Arthropoda	Araneae	Lycosidae	Pardosa	Pardosa agraria	10.3389/fmicb.2021.758794				WGS:6	6	2	Pardosa agraria; Pardosa laura		spider metagenome	farmland ecosystems; woodland ecosystems	Metagenome or environmental sample from spider metagenome	6.0	https://doi.org/10.3389/fmicb.2021.758794	True	True	Pardosa agraria	True	wolf spider	True	Pardosa agraria; Pardosa laura	False		False	ENA + paper	Metagenomic sequences were obtained from the gut microbiota of two Pardosa wolf spider species from different ecosystems.	DIVERSE	6	METAGENOMIC:6	6	6	KEEP	yes	high	gut_explicit	multi_host	Pardosa agraria; Pardosa laura	False	agree	GUT: yes (high) — Title states 'Wolf Spider Gut Microbiota'; abstract explicitly describes 'gut microbiota of Pardosa spiders' and 'gut microbiota of P. agraria' and 'P. laura'; shotgun metagenomic sequencing of community DNA from spider gut. || BODY_SITE: gut_explicit — Title: 'Wolf Spider Gut Microbiota'; abstract repeatedly uses 'gut microbiota' (appears 4+ times); isolation source describes ecosystems (farmland/woodland) where spiders were collected, not gut tissue type explicitly stated but gut is the singular focus throughout. || HOST: Pardosa agraria; Pardosa laura [multi_host] — Abstract states 'to compare the gut microbiota of two wolf spider species, Pardosa agraria and P. laura'; Host field lists 'Pardosa agraria'; Host species in samples field lists both 'Pardosa agraria' and 'Pardosa laura' (2 distinct species); this is a genuine comparative study of two different spider species' guts, not incidental sampling. || METHOD: agree — Abstract states 'shotgun metagenomic sequencing'; ENA fields confirm library_strategy_mix = WGS:6 with 6 WGS/shotgun samples; library_source = METAGENOMIC (appropriate for community DNA from gut microbiota). Methods and metadata align. || SUMMARY: This is a straightforward multi-species comparative gut microbiome study of wolf spiders using shotgun metagenomics. Two Pardosa species sampled from different ecosystems with explicit focus on gut microbial composition and diversity.	multihost_issue				Deposit host_species_list shows 2 distinct species: Pardosa agraria and Pardosa laura. Paper compares gut microbiota of both species.	Multihost issue: deposit sample-level data explicitly identify two distinct Pardosa species 