PRJNA641765	Homo sapiens	9606.0		Germany: Tuebingen	1	WGS	pubmed_postdoc	33335515.0	33335515.0	The Neuromodulator-Encoding sadA Gene Is Widely Distributed in the Human Skin Microbiome.	Luqman A	Götz F	Mammalia	Trace amines (TA) are endogenously produced in mammals, have a low concentration in the central nervous system (CNS), but trigger a variety of neurological effects and intervene in host cell communication. It emerged that neurotransmitters and TA are produced also by the microbiota. As it has been shown that TA contribute to wound healing, we examined the skin microbiome of probands using shotgun metagenomics. The phyla Actinobacteria, Proteobacteria, Firmicutes, and Bacteroidetes were predominant. Since SadA is a highly promiscuous TA-producing decarboxylase in Firmicutes, the skin microbiome was specifically examined for the presence of sadA-homologous genes. By mapping the reads of certain genes, we found that, although there were less reads mapping to sadA than to ubiquitous housekeeping genes (arcC and mutS), normalized reads counts were still >1000 times higher than those of rare control genes (icaA, icaB, and epiA). At protein sequence level SadA homologs were found in at least 7 phyla: Firmicutes, Actinobacteria, Proteobacteria, Bacteroidetes, Acidobacteria, Chloroflexi, and Cyanobacteria, and in 23 genera of the phylum Firmicutes. A high proportion of the genera that have a SadA homolog belong to the classical skin and intestinal microbiota. The distribution of sadA in so many different phyla illustrates the importance of horizontal gene transfer (HGT). We show that the sadA gene is widely distributed in the human skin microbiome. When comparing the sadA read counts in the probands, there was no correlation between age and gender, but an enormous difference in the sadA read counts in the microbiome of the individuals. Since sadA is involved in TA synthesis, it is likely that the TA content of the skin is correlated with the amount of TA producing bacteria in the microbiome. In this way, the microbiome-generated TA could influence signal transmission in the epithelial and nervous system.	2020.0	Frontiers in microbiology	YES		METAGENOMIC	YES	Metazoa	Chordata	Primates	Hominidae	Homo	Homo sapiens	10.3389/fmicb.2020.573679				WGS:1	1	1	Homo sapiens		human skin metagenome		MIMS Environmental/Metagenome sample from human skin metagenome	1.0	https://doi.org/10.3389/fmicb.2020.573679	True	True	Homo sapiens	True	human	True	human	False		False	ENA + paper	Shotgun metagenomic sequencing was performed on skin microbiome samples from human probands.	UNIFORM	1	METAGENOMIC:1	1	1	EXCLUDE	no	high	non_gut	single_clear	Homo sapiens	True	agree	GUT: no (high) — Paper title explicitly states 'human skin microbiome'. Abstract states 'we examined the skin microbiome of probands using shotgun metagenomics' and 'Sample titles: MIMS Environmental/Metagenome sample from human skin metagenome'. Body site is skin, not gut. || BODY_SITE: non_gut — Title: 'The Neuromodulator-Encoding sadA Gene Is Widely Distributed in the Human Skin Microbiome'. Abstract: 'we examined the skin microbiome'. Biome label: 'human skin metagenome'. Sample titles explicitly reference 'human skin metagenome'. || HOST: Homo sapiens [single_clear] (HUMAN) — Host field: Homo sapiens. Host species in samples (1 distinct): Homo sapiens. Paper focuses on human probands' skin microbiome with no multi-host comparative design. || METHOD: agree — ENA field library_strategy_mix indicates WGS:1 (shotgun metagenomics). Abstract states 'we examined the skin microbiome of probands using shotgun metagenomics'. library_source is METAGENOMIC (community DNA), consistent with metagenomics. No mismatch detected. || SUMMARY: This is a human skin microbiome study, not a gut microbiome study. Although humans are animals and the study uses valid WGS metagenomics methodology, the body site is explicitly skin rather than gut, making it ineligible for an animal gut microbiome catalog.	gut_sample_