PRJNA730805	Mus musculus	10090.0		USA: Auburn, Alabama	30	WGS	pubmed_postdoc	34414128.0	34414128.0	Integrative Longitudinal Analysis of Metabolic Phenotype and Microbiota Changes During the Development of Obesity.	Higgins KV	Schwartz EH	Mammalia	Obesity has increased at an alarming rate over the past two decades in the United States. In addition to increased body mass, obesity is often accompanied by comorbidities such as Type II Diabetes Mellitus and metabolic dysfunction-associated fatty liver disease, with serious impacts on public health. Our understanding of the role the intestinal microbiota in obesity has rapidly advanced in recent years, especially with respect to the bacterial constituents. However, we know little of when changes in these microbial populations occur as obesity develops. Further, we know little about how other domains of the microbiota, namely bacteriophage populations, are affected during the progression of obesity. Our goal in this study was to monitor changes in the intestinal microbiome and metabolic phenotype following western diet feeding. We accomplished this by collecting metabolic data and fecal samples for shotgun metagenomic sequencing in a mouse model of diet-induced obesity. We found that after two weeks of consuming a western diet (WD), the animals weighed significantly more and were less metabolically stable than their chow fed counterparts. The western diet induced rapid changes in the intestinal microbiome with the most pronounced dissimilarity at 12 weeks. Our study highlights the dynamic nature of microbiota composition following WD feeding and puts these events in the context of the metabolic status of the mammalian host.	2021.0	Frontiers in cellular and infection microbiology	YES		METAGENOMIC	YES	Metazoa	Chordata	Rodentia	Muridae	Mus	Mus musculus	10.3389/fcimb.2021.671926				WGS:30	30	1	Mus musculus		gut metagenome		Metagenome or environmental sample from gut metagenome	30.0	https://doi.org/10.3389/fcimb.2021.671926	True	True	Mus musculus	False		True	mouse (model of diet-induced obesity)	True	diet-induced obesity	False	ENA + paper	[model of diet-induced obesity] Fecal samples for shotgun metagenomic sequencing were collected from mice fed a western diet or chow diet.	UNIFORM	30	METAGENOMIC:30	30	30	KEEP	yes	high	gut_explicit	single_clear	Mus musculus	False	agree	GUT: yes (high) — Abstract explicitly states 'our goal in this study was to monitor changes in the intestinal microbiome' and 'biome label(s): gut metagenome'; sample titles confirm 'gut metagenome' || BODY_SITE: gut_explicit — Biome label explicitly 'gut metagenome'; abstract focuses on 'intestinal microbiota' and 'intestinal microbiome'; sample titles state 'gut metagenome' || HOST: Mus musculus [single_clear] — Host field lists 'Mus musculus'; host species in samples shows '1 distinct: Mus musculus'; paper title and abstract focus on obesity development in a single model organism context || METHOD: agree — Library strategy is WGS (shotgun metagenomic sequencing); library source is METAGENOMIC (community DNA, not single organism); 30 WGS samples align with stated longitudinal monitoring of microbiota changes || SUMMARY: This is a genuine longitudinal animal gut microbiome study using shotgun metagenomic sequencing of Mus musculus (mouse) intestinal samples to track microbiota changes during obesity development. All four criteria align clearly with the evidence.	none					No data-quality issues detected; mouse host appropriately matches the animal model obesity study design.	1		False		True	False
PRJNA738608	Canis lupus familiaris	9615.0		India	107	WGS	pubmed_postdoc	35506233.0	35506233.0	Geographically diverse canid sampling provides novel insights into pre-industrial microbiomes.	Yarlagadda K	Malhi RS	Mammalia	Canine microbiome studies are often limited in the geographic and temporal scope of samples studied. This results in a paucity of data on the canine microbiome around the world, especially in contexts where dogs may not be pets or human associated. Here, we present the shotgun sequences of fecal microbiomes of pet dogs from South Africa, shelter and stray dogs from India, and stray village dogs in Laos. We additionally performed a dietary experiment with dogs housed in a veterinary medical school, attempting to replicate the diet of the sampled dogs from Laos. We analyse the taxonomic diversity in these populations and identify the underlying functional redundancy of these microbiomes. Our results show that diet alone is not sufficient to recapitulate the higher diversity seen in the microbiome of dogs from Laos. Comparisons to previous studies and ancient dog fecal microbiomes highlight the need for greater population diversity in studies of canine microbiomes, as modern analogues can provide better comparisons to ancient microbiomes. We identify trends in microbial diversity and industrialization in dogs that mirror results of human studies, suggesting future research can make use of these companion animals as substitutes for humans in studying the effects of industrialization on the microbiome.	2022.0	Proceedings. Biological sciences	YES		METAGENOMIC	YES	Metazoa	Chordata	Carnivora	Canidae	Canis	Canis lupus	10.1098/rspb.2022.0052				WGS:107	107	1	Canis lupus familiaris		gut metagenome	feces	Metagenome or environmental sample from gut metagenome	107.0	https://doi.org/10.1098/rspb.2022.0052	True	True	Canis lupus familiaris	True	canid	True	dog	False		False	ENA + paper	Fecal microbiome sequences from pet dogs (South Africa), shelter/stray dogs (India), village dogs (Laos), and dogs in a veterinary medical school dietary experiment.	UNIFORM	107	METAGENOMIC:107	107	107	KEEP	yes	high	gut_explicit	single_clear	Canis lupus familiaris	False	agree	GUT: yes (high) — Paper explicitly states 'shotgun sequences of fecal microbiomes' and biome label is 'gut metagenome'; isolation source is 'feces'; 107 WGS samples; library_source is METAGENOMIC (community DNA, not single organism genome). || BODY_SITE: gut_explicit — Biome label explicitly 'gut metagenome'; isolation source explicitly 'feces'; sample titles reference 'gut metagenome'; abstract discusses 'fecal microbiomes'. || HOST: Canis lupus familiaris [single_clear] — Host field: Canis lupus familiaris (domestic dog); host species in samples shows 1 distinct species; title confirms 'canid sampling'; abstract describes three geographic populations of dogs (pet dogs from South Africa, shelter/stray dogs from India, stray village dogs in Laos) plus experimental dogs. All samples are from the same species despite geographic/lifestyle diversity. || METHOD: agree — Paper describes 'shotgun sequences' (WGS); ENA library_strategy_mix confirms WGS:107 samples; library_source METAGENOMIC aligns with fecal microbiome community sampling; no contradictions detected. || SUMMARY: This is a genuine, well-documented animal gut microbiome study of domestic dogs from multiple geographic and social contexts. The sampling method, host identity, and body site (feces/gut) are all explicitly and consistently stated across metadata and narrative evidence.	none					No reporting errors detected. Paper and deposit are consistent: all samples are from domestic dogs (Canis lupus familiaris / Canis familiaris), with fecal/gut material as isolation source.	1		False		True	False
PRJNA758898	Felis catus	9685.0		USA: Auburn	16	WGS	pubmed_postdoc	35467389.0	35467389.0	Whole-Genome Shotgun Metagenomic Sequencing Reveals Distinct Gut Microbiome Signatures of Obese Cats.	Ma X	Wang X	Mammalia	Overweight and obesity are growing health problems in domestic cats, increasing the risks of insulin resistance, lipid dyscrasias, neoplasia, cardiovascular disease, and decreasing longevity. The signature of obesity in the feline gut microbiota has not been studied at the whole-genome metagenomic level. We performed whole-genome shotgun metagenomic sequencing in the fecal samples of eight overweight/obese and eight normal cats housed in the same research environment. We obtained 271 Gbp of sequences and generated a 961-Mbp de novo reference contig assembly, with 1.14 million annotated microbial genes. In the obese cat microbiome, we discovered a significant reduction in microbial diversity (P < 0.01) and Firmicutes abundance (P = 0.005), as well as decreased Firmicutes/Bacteroidetes ratios (P = 0.02), which is the inverse of obese human/mouse microbiota. Linear discriminant analysis and quantitative PCR (qPCR) validation revealed significant increases of Bifidobacterium sp., Olsenella provencensis, Dialister sp.CAG:486, and Campylobacter upsaliensis as the hallmark of obese microbiota among 400 enriched species, whereas 1,525 bacterial species have decreased abundance in the obese microbiome. Phascolarctobacterium succinatutens and an uncharacterized Erysipelotrichaceae bacterium are highly abundant (>0.05%) in the normal gut with over 400-fold depletion in the obese microbiome. Fatty acid synthesis-related pathways are significantly overrepresented in the obese compared with the normal cat microbiome. In conclusion, we discovered dramatically decreased microbial diversity in obese cat gut microbiota, suggesting potential dysbiosis. A panel of seven significantly altered, highly abundant species can serve as a microbiome indicator of obesity. Our findings in the obese cat microbiome composition, abundance, and functional capacities provide new insights into feline obesity. IMPORTANCE Obesity affects around 45% of domestic cats, and licensed drugs for treating feline obesity are lacking. Physical exercise and calorie restrictions are commonly used for weight loss but with limited efficacy. Through comprehensive analyses of normal and obese cat gut bacteria flora, we identified dramatic shifts in the obese gut microbiome, including four bacterial species significantly enriched and two species depleted in the obese cats. The key bacterial community and functional capacity alterations discovered from this study will inform new weight management strategies for obese cats, such as evaluations of specific diet formulas that alter the microbiome composition, and the development of prebiotics and probiotics that promote the increase of beneficial species and the depletion of obesity-associated species. Interestingly, these bacteria identified in our study were also reported to affect the weight loss success in human patients, suggesting translational potential in human obesity.	2022.0	Microbiology spectrum	YES		METAGENOMIC	YES	Metazoa	Chordata	Carnivora	Felidae	Felis	Felis catus	10.1128/spectrum.00837-22				WGS:16	16	1	Felis catus		gut metagenome	rectum fecal sample from normal cat 111030; rectum fecal sample from normal cat 111041; rectum fecal sample from normal cat 111468; rectum fecal sample from normal cat 111473; rectum fecal sample from normal cat 81392; rectum fecal sample from normal cat 9Z110; rectum fecal sample from normal cat 9Z113; rectum fecal sample from normal cat 9Z116; rectum fecal sample from obese cat D001; rectum fecal sample from obese cat F001; rectum fecal sample from obese cat G001; rectum fecal sample from obese cat H001; rectum fecal sample from obese cat I001; rectum fecal sample from obese cat J001; rectum fecal sample from obese cat K001; rectum fecal sample from obese cat L001	Metagenome or environmental sample from gut metagenome	16.0	https://doi.org/10.1128/spectrum.00837-22	True	True	Felis catus	True	cat	True	cat	False		False	ENA + paper	Whole-genome shotgun metagenomic sequencing was performed on fecal samples from eight overweight/obese and eight normal domestic cats.	UNIFORM	16	METAGENOMIC:16	16	16	KEEP	yes	high	gut_explicit	single_clear	Felis catus	False	agree	GUT: yes (high) — Paper title explicitly states 'Gut Microbiome' study; abstract describes 'whole-genome shotgun metagenomic sequencing in the fecal samples' of cats; library_source is METAGENOMIC (community DNA); Biome label is 'gut metagenome'; isolation source specifies 'rectum fecal sample' || BODY_SITE: gut_explicit — Biome label explicitly states 'gut metagenome'; isolation source explicitly documents 'rectum fecal sample'; abstract specifies fecal sampling from cats; sample titles reference 'gut metagenome' || HOST: Felis catus [single_clear] — Host field clearly states 'Felis catus' (domestic cat); Host species in samples shows 1 distinct species; abstract describes comparative study of 'eight overweight/obese and eight normal cats' (16 cats total, not incidental); all samples are from the same host species || METHOD: agree — Abstract explicitly states 'whole-genome shotgun metagenomic sequencing'; ENA library_strategy_mix shows 'WGS:16' (16 WGS samples); library_source is METAGENOMIC; 16 total WGS samples match the 16 fecal samples (8 obese + 8 normal) described in abstract || SUMMARY: This is a genuine, well-documented animal (cat) gut microbiome WGS study with explicit fecal/rectal sampling, consistent metagenomic library strategy, and clear single-host comparative design between obese and normal animals.	none					No reporting errors detected. Paper and deposit metadata are consistent: domestic cat (Felis catus) gut microbiome study with fecal samples.	1		False		True	False
PRJNA1098026	Mus musculus	10090.0		USA: NY, Valhalla	21	WGS	pubmed_postdoc	40693139.0	40693139.0	Early life microbiome disbalance impacts neuroendocrine outcomes in pre-pubertal mice in a sexually dimorphic manner.	Nankova BB	LaGamma EF	Mammalia	INTRODUCTION: Adverse exposures during perinatal development disrupt the emerging gut microbial ecology that in turn negatively influences long term health. How gut dysbiosis affects complex neurobehavioral functions or even simple reflex arcs (e.g. the amplitude of sympathoadrenal adaptive responses to hypoglycemia) in the extrauterine environment is not well understood. METHODS: The C57Bl6 dams were given broad-spectrum antibiotics in the drinking water at parturition until weaning of their litter to perturb the normal seeding and maturation of the postnatal microbiome, control animals received sterile water. To evaluate the impact of altered postnatal flora the offspring were subjected to behavioral tests or sacrificed after exposure to insulin-induced hypoglycemia. Fecal samples were collected for microbial whole genome shotgun taxonomic profiling and predictive functionality. As an index of host sympathoadrenal capacity, individual adrenal medulla samples from each group were subjected to RNA sequencing to identify differentially expressed genes between the groups and gain insights into molecular pathways contributing to the observed outcomes. Given that several neurodevelopmental disorders in humans are biased by sex we also included it as variable in this report. RESULTS: The offspring of control dams displayed sex-specific differences in microbiome composition, exploratory behavior, adrenal transcriptome profiles and basal urinary epinephrine levels. Maternal antibiotics during nursing caused: (1) microbial dysbiosis in the offspring as evident by markedly enlarged ceca, no detectable by-products of bacterial fermentation (sp. SCFA) and dramatic changes in microbial composition, diversity (reduced - alpha Chao1and beta Bray-Curtis, as compared to their respective controls) and predictive metabolic activity; (2) alteration in the transcriptional signature of the adrenal medullae and attenuated peripheral stress responses in male offspring, associated with gap junction signaling pathways; (3) increased anxiety-like testing metrics, and decreased locomotor activity; all in a sexually dimorphic manner. DISCUSSION: We speculate that the observed sex differences in the gut microbiome may contribute to neurodevelopmental disorders known to have sex-related disparities and in the capacity for successful adaptation to stress. A better understanding of how microbial communities and their hosts interact during critical portions of postnatal neurobehavioral development may help personalize nutritional and therapeutic strategies to promote long term health.	2025.0	Frontiers in microbiology	NO	True	METAGENOMIC	YES	Metazoa	Chordata	Rodentia	Muridae	Mus	Mus musculus	10.3389/fmicb.2025.1504513				WGS:21	21	1	Mus musculus		mouse gut metagenome	stool	Metagenome or environmental sample from mouse gut metagenome	21.0	https://doi.org/10.3389/fmicb.2025.1504513	True	True	Mus musculus	True	mouse	True	mouse (model of microbiome dysbiosis)	True	microbiome dysbiosis	False	ENA + paper	[model of microbiome dysbiosis] Fecal samples were collected from C57Bl6 mouse offspring for metagenomic whole genome shotgun sequencing to assess microbial composition and diversity.	UNIFORM	21	METAGENOMIC:21	21	21	KEEP	yes	high	gut_explicit	single_clear	Mus musculus	False	agree	GUT: yes (high) — Abstract explicitly describes gut microbial ecology disruption and neuroendocrine outcomes in mice. Fecal samples collected from mouse offspring to evaluate postnatal microbiome composition and function. || BODY_SITE: gut_explicit — Isolation source stated as 'stool'; biome label explicitly 'mouse gut metagenome'; sample titles reference 'mouse gut metagenome'; abstract discusses 'gut microbial ecology' and 'postnatal microbiome' from fecal sampling. || HOST: Mus musculus [single_clear] — Host field and host species in samples both clearly identify 'Mus musculus' (laboratory mouse). Study design compares C57Bl6 dams and their offspring as a single host species in a developmental intervention study, not a multi-host survey. || METHOD: agree — Paper describes shotgun metagenomic sequencing of fecal samples (gut microbiome). ENA fields confirm WGS (21 samples), library_source METAGENOMIC, isolation source 'stool', biome 'mouse gut metagenome'. Methods and metadata are consistent. || SUMMARY: This is a genuine, single-host (Mus musculus) gut microbiome study using shotgun WGS of fecal samples to investigate how perinatal antibiotic-induced dysbiosis affects neuroendocrine development in mice. All four criteria are clearly satisfied with high confidence.	none					No reporting errors detected. Paper and deposit are consistent: mouse (C57Bl6, Mus musculus) gut microbiome study with appropriate sample metadata.	1		False		True	False
PRJNA751718	Vespa mandarinia	7446.0		China: Tengchong, Yunnan	2	WGS	pubmed_postdoc	38529182.0	38529182.0	Shotgun metagenomics reveals the gut microbial diversity and functions in Vespa mandarinia (Hymenoptera: Vespidae) at multiple life stages.	Yin PK	Yang YH	Insecta	Wasps play important roles as predators and pollinators in the ecosystem. The Jingpo minority residing in Yunnan Province, China, has a traditional practice of using wine infused with mature wasps as a customary remedy for managing rheumatoid arthritis. The larva of the wasp is also a tasteful folk dish that has created a tremendous market. There is a paucity of survival knowledge, which has greatly restricted their potential applications in food and healthcare. Recent research has highlighted the importance of gut microbiota in insect growth. Nevertheless, there is still a lack of understanding regarding the composition, changes, and functions of the gut microbiota in Vespa mandarinia during development. In this research, the gut microbiota were investigated across three growth stages of Vespa mandarinia using a metagenomic technology. The result revealed that there are significant variations in the proportion of main gut microbes during the metamorphosis of Vespa mandarinia. Tenericutes were found to dominate during the larval stage, while Proteobacteria emerged as the dominant group post-pupation. Through a comprehensive analysis of the gut microbiota metagenome, this study revealed functional differences in the wasp gut microbiota at various growth stages. During the larval stage, the gut microbiota plays a central role in promoting metabolism. Following pupation, the gut microbiota exhibited diversified functions, likely due to the complex environments and diverse food sources encountered after metamorphosis. These functions included amino acid metabolism, compound degradation, and defense mechanisms. This research provides an extensive dataset on the gut microbiota during the metamorphosis of Vespa mandarinia, contributing to a deeper understanding of the influence of gut microbiota on wasp growth. Furthermore, this study uncovers a unique microbial treasure within insect guts, which is important for advancing the application of wasps in the fields of food and medicine.	2024.0	Frontiers in microbiology	YES		METAGENOMIC	YES	Metazoa	Arthropoda	Hymenoptera	Vespidae	Vespa	Vespa mandarinia	10.3389/fmicb.2024.1288051				WGS:2	2	1	Vespa mandarinia		insect gut metagenome	whole gut	the metagenomic of the adult gut of Vespa mandarinia; the metagenomic of the larva gut of Vespa mandarinia	2.0	https://doi.org/10.3389/fmicb.2024.1288051	True	True	Vespa mandarinia	True	Vespa mandarinia	True	Vespa mandarinia	False		False	ENA + paper	Gut microbiota samples were collected from Vespa mandarinia wasps at three different developmental stages (larval, pupal, and adult).	UNIFORM	2	METAGENOMIC:2	2	2	KEEP	yes	high	gut_explicit	single_clear	Vespa mandarinia	False	agree	GUT: yes (high) — Paper title explicitly states 'gut microbial diversity' in Vespa mandarinia; abstract confirms 'gut microbiota were investigated'; isolation_source field states 'whole gut'; biome label is 'insect gut metagenome'; library_source is METAGENOMIC (community DNA, not host genome) || BODY_SITE: gut_explicit — Isolation source explicitly recorded as 'whole gut'; sample titles specify 'adult gut' and 'larva gut'; abstract and title both focus on gut microbiota composition and function || HOST: Vespa mandarinia [single_clear] — Host field states 'Vespa mandarinia'; host_species_in_samples shows 1 distinct species (Vespa mandarinia); samples represent different life stages (adult and larva) of the same species, not different hosts; paper title explicitly names Vespa mandarinia (Asian giant hornet) || METHOD: agree — ENA records WGS (shotgun metagenomics) with 2 metagenomic samples; paper title states 'Shotgun metagenomics'; library_strategy_mix shows WGS:2 and library_source is METAGENOMIC; methodology is consistent || SUMMARY: This is a well-documented, straightforward animal (insect) gut microbiome study using shotgun metagenomics on a single wasp species sampled at two developmental stages. No contradictions detected between metadata fields and paper content.	none					No reporting errors detected; paper and deposit metadata are fully aligned on host identity, sample type, and study design.	1		False		True	False
PRJNA1067380	Cotesia vestalis	217443.0		China	3	WGS	pubmed_postdoc	39248477.0	39248477.0	Characterization of larval gut microbiota of two endoparasitoid wasps associated with their common host, Plutella xylostella (Linnaeus) (Lepidoptera: Plutellidae).	Hu N-n	Chen X-x	Insecta	Insect gut microbes play important roles in digestion, metabolism, development, and environmental adaptation. Parasitoid wasps are one of the most important biological control agents in pest control, while the gut microbial species compositions and the associated functions have been poorly investigated. Two endoparasitoid wasps, Cotesia vestalis and Diadromus collaris, parasitize the larval stage and pupal stage of the diamondback moth, Plutella xylostella, respectively. Using whole-genome shotgun metagenomic sequencing, we characterized the gut microbial composition, diversity, and potential functional roles associated with the two parasitoid wasp larvae. The results reveal that Proteobacteria and Firmicutes are the dominant phyla in the gut of C. vestalis and D. collaris larvae, with Rhizobium and Enterococcus being the dominant genera. The putative microbial functions associated with the two parasitoid wasps might play a virtual role in assisting in consuming the host's nutritional composition. The enriched CAZymes family genes are primarily involved in the degradation and synthesis of chitin. Despite the richness of microbial species and communities, the microbes species and the microbial community structure exhibit significant similarity between the two parasitoid wasps and between the parasitoid wasp and the host P. xylostella. Notably, the prevalence of the genus Enterococcus shared among them suggests a possible link of gut microbes between the host and their associated parasitoids. Our study offers insights into the gut microbe-based interactions between the host and parasitoid wasps for the first time, potentially paving the way for the development of an ecologically friendly biocontrol strategy against the pest P. xylostella.IMPORTANCEEndoparasitoid wasps spend the majority of their lifespan within their host and heavily rely on the host's nutrition for survival. There is limited understanding regarding the composition and physiological impacts of gut microbial communities in parasitoid wasps, particularly during the larval stage, which is directly linked to the host. Based on a thorough characterization of the gut microbe and comprehensive comparative analysis, we found the microbial species of the larval parasitoid wasp Cotesia vestalis and the pupal parasitoid wasp Diadromus collaris were similar, sharing 159 genera and 277 species, as were the microbial community structure. Certain of the dominant microbial strains of the two parasitoid wasps were similar to that of their host Plutella xylostella larvae, revealing host insect may affect the microbial community of the parasitoid wasps. The putative microbial functions associated with the parasitoid wasp larvae play an important role in dietary consumption.	2024.0	Microbiology spectrum	YES		METAGENOMIC	YES	Metazoa	Arthropoda	Hymenoptera	Braconidae	Cotesia	Cotesia vestalis	10.1128/spectrum.01208-24				WGS:3	3	1	Cotesia vestalis		insect metagenome	insect	insect metagenome 1; insect metagenome 2; insect metagenome 3	3.0	https://doi.org/10.1128/spectrum.01208-24	True	True	Cotesia vestalis	True	Cotesia vestalis; Diadromus collaris	True	Cotesia vestalis; Diadromus collaris	False		False	ENA + paper	Metagenomic sequencing was performed on gut samples from larval stages of two parasitoid wasps.	UNIFORM	3	METAGENOMIC:3	3	3	KEEP	yes	high	gut_explicit	single_clear	Cotesia vestalis	False	agree	GUT: yes (high) — Title states 'larval gut microbiota' and abstract explicitly describes 'the gut microbial composition' of parasitoid wasp larvae using 'whole-genome shotgun metagenomic sequencing'. Isolation source is 'insect' and biome label is 'insect metagenome', consistent with gut sampling. || BODY_SITE: gut_