loimmune responses following antibiotic pretreatment. The host is a single, non-human animal (Mus musculus), and all evidence aligns consistently.	none					No reporting errors detected. Paper describes a mouse model study of gut microbiota effects on allograft rejection, and deposit correctly identifies Mus musculus gut samples with appropriate metagenomic sequencing.	1		False		True	False
PRJEB40767	Rattus norvegicus			United Kingdom	157	WGS	pubmed_postdoc	35392807.0	35392807.0	Pharmacologically induced weight loss is associated with distinct gut microbiome changes in obese rats.	Raineri S	Mellor J	Mammalia	BACKGROUND: Obesity, metabolic disease and some psychiatric conditions are associated with changes to relative abundance of bacterial species and specific genes in the faecal microbiome. Little is known about the impact of pharmacologically induced weight loss on distinct microbiome species and their respective gene programs in obese individuals. METHODOLOGY: Using shotgun metagenomics, the composition of the microbiome was obtained for two cohorts of obese female Wistar rats (n = 10-12, total of 82) maintained on a high fat diet before and after a 42-day treatment with a panel of four investigatory or approved anti-obesity drugs (tacrolimus/FK506, bupropion, naltrexone and sibutramine), alone or in combination. RESULTS: Only sibutramine treatment induced consistent weight loss and improved glycaemic control in the obese rats. Weight loss was associated with reduced food intake and changes to the faecal microbiome in multiple microbial taxa, genes, and pathways. These include increased β-diversity, increased relative abundance of multiple Bacteroides species, increased Bacteroides/Firmicutes ratio and changes to abundance of genes and species associated with obesity-induced inflammation, particularly those encoding components of the flagellum and its assembly. CONCLUSIONS: Sibutramine-induced weight loss in obese rats is associated with improved metabolic health, and changes to the faecal microbiome consistent with a reduction in obesity-induced bacterially-driven inflammation.	2022.0	BMC microbiology	YES		METAGENOMIC	YES	Animalia	Chordata	Rodentia	Muridae	Rattus	norvegicus	10.1186/s12866-022-02494-1				WGS:157	157	0			Rattus norvegicus		Gut microbiome profiling of obese rats treated with panel of weight-loss drugs	157.0	https://doi.org/10.1186/s12866-022-02494-1	True	True	Rattus norvegicus	True	rat	True	rat	True	obesity	False	ENA + paper	[model of obesity] Shotgun metagenomic sequencing was performed on faecal samples from obese female Wistar rats before and after anti-obesity drug treatment.	UNIFORM	157	METAGENOMIC:157	157	157	KEEP	yes	high	gut_explicit	single_clear	Rattus norvegicus	False	agree	GUT: yes (high) — Paper title states 'gut microbiome changes in obese rats'; abstract explicitly mentions 'faecal microbiome' and describes shotgun metagenomics analysis of microbiome composition; sample titles reference 'Gut microbiome profiling'. || BODY_SITE: gut_explicit — Abstract specifies 'faecal microbiome'; sample titles state 'Gut microbiome profiling'; body site metadata field empty but multiple explicit textual references to gut/faecal origin confirm this is intestinal material. || HOST: Rattus norvegicus [single_clear] — Host field clearly states 'Rattus norvegicus'; paper title and abstract specify 'obese female Wistar rats' (Wistar is a laboratory strain of Rattus norvegicus); study design uses defined cohorts (n=10-12, total 82 rats); no evidence of multi-host survey. || METHOD: agree — Abstract states 'Using shotgun metagenomics' for microbiome composition analysis; ENA library_strategy_mix shows WGS:157 (157 WGS samples); library_source marked METAGENOMIC (community DNA); 157 total samples match the stated methodology for a defined cohort study. || SUMMARY: This is a genuine animal gut microbiome study using shotgun metagenomics on fecal samples from laboratory rats. Single host species (Rattus norvegicus) studied across multiple individuals in a pharmacological intervention trial; all criteria align.	none					No errors detected. Paper and deposit metadata are consistent: study involved Wistar rats (Rattus norvegicus) gut microbiome samples.	1		False		True	False
PRJEB21599	Arion ater				1	WGS	pubmed_postdoc	29167663.0	29167663.0	Metagenomic Analysis of the Gut Microbiome of the Common Black Slug Arion ater in Search of Novel Lignocellulose Degrading Enzymes.	Joynson R	Ferry N	Gastropoda	Some eukaryotes are able to gain access to well-protected carbon sources in plant biomass by exploiting microorganisms in the environment or harbored in their digestive system. One is the land pulmonate Arion ater, which takes advantage of a gut microbial consortium that can break down the widely available, but difficult to digest, carbohydrate polymers in lignocellulose, enabling them to digest a broad range of fresh and partially degraded plant material efficiently. This ability is considered one of the major factors that have enabled A. ater to become one of the most widespread plant pest species in Western Europe and North America. Using metagenomic techniques we have characterized the bacterial diversity and functional capability of the gut microbiome of this notorious agricultural pest. Analysis of gut metagenomic community sequences identified abundant populations of known lignocellulose-degrading bacteria, along with well-characterized bacterial plant pathogens. This also revealed a repertoire of more than 3,383 carbohydrate active enzymes (CAZymes) including multiple enzymes associated with lignin degradation, demonstrating a microbial consortium capable of degradation of all components of lignocellulose. This would allow A. ater to make extensive use of plant biomass as a source of nutrients through exploitation of the enzymatic capabilities of the gut microbial consortia. From this metagenome assembly we also demonstrate the successful amplification of multiple predicted gene sequences from metagenomic DNA subjected to whole genome amplification and expression of functional proteins, facilitating the low cost acquisition and biochemical testing of the many thousands of novel genes identified in metagenomics studies. These findings demonstrate the importance of studying Gastropod microbial communities. Firstly, with respect to understanding links between feeding and evolutionary success and, secondly, as sources of novel enzymes with biotechnological potential, such as, CAZYmes that could be used in the production of biofuel.	2017.0	Frontiers in microbiology	NO	True	METAGENOMIC	YES	Animalia	Mollusca	Stylommatophora	Arionidae	Arion	ater	10.3389/fmicb.2017.02181				WGS:1	1	0			Arion ater		Metagenomic analysis of the gut microbiome of the common black slug Arion ater	1.0	https://doi.org/10.3389/fmicb.2017.02181	True	True	Arion ater	True	Arion ater	True	Arion ater	False		False	ENA + paper	Metagenomic samples were sequenced from the gut microbiome of the common black slug Arion ater.	UNIFORM	1	METAGENOMIC:1	1	1	KEEP	yes	high	gut_explicit	single_clear	Arion ater	False	agree	GUT: yes (high) — Paper title explicitly states 'Gut Microbiome of the Common Black Slug Arion ater'; abstract confirms 'gut microbial consortium' and 'digestive system'; library_source is METAGENOMIC (community DNA); sample title specifies 'gut microbiome of the common black slug Arion ater' || BODY_SITE: gut_explicit — Title and abstract repeatedly state 'gut microbiome'; sample title specifies 'gut microbiome'; abstract discusses 'digestive system' and 'break down carbohydrate polymers in lignocellulose' in the context of gut function || HOST: Arion ater [single_clear] — Host field clearly lists 'Arion ater' (common black slug); paper title and abstract consistently name this species; biome label confirms 'Arion ater'; WGS sample count is 1, indicating single host organism sampled || METHOD: agree — ENA records library_strategy_mix as WGS:1 and library_source as METAGENOMIC; paper title and abstract describe metagenomic techniques applied to gut microbiome; these align perfectly with shotgun WGS of community DNA from the slug gut || SUMMARY: This is a clear, unambiguous animal gut metagenomics study of a single slug (Arion ater) using WGS shotgun sequencing of community DNA from the digestive system. No contradictions or ambiguities detected.	none					Study reports gut microbiome of Arion ater; deposit metadata is consistent with paper across host identity and sample type.	1		False		True	False
PRJNA1026073	Plutella xylostella	51655.0		United Kingdom	1	WGS	pubmed_postdoc	39037312.0	39037312.0	Whole-genome sequence of Enterobacter hormaechei, isolate jjbc recovered from the gut of Plutella xylostella feeding on cabbage.	Mugo-Kamiri L	Raymond B	Insecta	We present the whole-genome sequence of Enterobacter hormaechei (previously Enterobacter cloacae) obtained from long and short reads. It is a dominant gut symbiont of the notorious crop pest Plutella xylostella, highly prevalent in lepidopteran midguts and a useful model for the evolution of resistance to antimicrobials.	2024.0	Microbiology resource announcements	NO	True	METAGENOMIC	YES	Metazoa	Arthropoda	Lepidoptera	Plutellidae	Plutella	Plutella xylostella	10.1128/mra.00330-24				WGS:1	1	1	Plutella xylostella		Enterobacter sp. JJBC	Gut	Microbe sample from Enterobacter sp. JJBC	1.0	https://doi.org/10.1128/mra.00330-24	True	True	Plutella xylostella	True	Plutella xylostella	True	Plutella xylostella	False		False	ENA + paper	The sequenced samples of Enterobacter hormaechei were isolated from the gut of Plutella xylostella (diamondback moth).	UNIFORM	1	METAGENOMIC:1	1	1	REVIEW	yes	high	gut_explicit	single_clear	Plutella xylostella	False	mismatch	GUT: yes (high) — Title states 'recovered from the gut of Plutella xylostella'; abstract confirms 'dominant gut symbiont of...Plutella xylostella' and 'lepidopteran midguts'; isolation source explicitly listed as 'Gut'. || BODY_SITE: gut_explicit — Title: 'from the gut of Plutella xylostella'; isolation source field: 'Gut'; abstract: 'gut symbiont' and 'lepidopteran midguts'. || HOST: Plutella xylostella [single_clear] — Host field and host species both state 'Plutella xylostella' (diamond-back moth, a lepidopteran insect); single distinct species; title and abstract consistently refer to this host. || METHOD: mismatch — Paper describes isolation and whole-genome sequencing of a single bacterial isolate (Enterobacter hormaechei strain jjbc); ENA library_source is marked 'METAGENOMIC' (implying community DNA) and WGS count is 1. However, the study appears to be a single-isolate genome, not a shotgun metagenomics survey. The isolate was recovered FROM the gut but the sequenced material is the cultured isolate, not a metagenomic community sample. || SUMMARY: This is a valid animal gut study (insect host, gut isolation confirmed), but it is fundamentally a bacterial isolate genome study rather than a true shotgun metagenomics survey of the gut community. The ENA metadata marking this as 'METAGENOMIC' appears to reflect the isolation source rather than the actual sequencing strategy applied.	none					No reporting errors detected; paper and deposit are fully aligned on host (Plutella xylostella), isolation source (gut), and study scope.	1		False		True	False
PRJNA865544	Mus musculus				1	WGS	pubmed_postdoc	37450589.0	37450589.0	Targeting gut microbiota-derived kynurenine to predict and protect the remodeling of the pressure-overloaded young heart.	Shi B	He X	Mammalia	Pressure-overloaded left ventricular remodeling in young population is progressive and readily degenerate into heart failure. The aims of this study were to identify a plasma metabolite that predicts and is mechanistically linked to the disease. Untargeted metabolomics determined elevated plasma kynurenine (Kyn) in both the patient cohorts and the mice model, which was correlated with remodeling parameters. In vitro and in vivo evidence, combined with single-nucleus RNA sequencing (snRNA-seq), demonstrated that Kyn affected both cardiomyocytes and cardiac fibroblasts by activating aryl hydrocarbon receptors (AHR) to up-regulate hypertrophy- and fibrosis-related genes. Shotgun metagenomics and fecal microbiota transplantation revealed the existence of the altered gut microbiota-Kyn relationship. Supplementation of selected microbes reconstructed the gut microbiota, reduced plasma Kyn, and alleviated ventricular remodeling. Our data collectively discovered a gut microbiota-derived metabolite to activate AHR and its gene targets in remodeling young heart, a process that could be prevented by specific gut microbiota modulation.	2023.0	Science advances	NO	True	METAGENOMIC	YES	Animalia	Chordata	Rodentia	Muridae	Mus	musculus	10.1126/sciadv.adg7417				WGS:1	1	0			Mus musculus		Gut microbiota from Mus musculus	1.0	https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.adg7417	True	True	Mus musculus	False		True	human; mouse (model of pressure-overloaded left ventricular remodeling)	True	pressure-overloaded left ventricular remodeling	False	ENA + paper	[model of pressure-overloaded left ventricular remodeling] Shotgun metagenomics performed on fecal samples from both patient cohorts and pressure-overloaded mouse models.	UNIFORM	1	METAGENOMIC:1	1	1	KEEP	yes	high	gut_explicit	single_clear	Mus musculus	False	agree	GUT: yes (high) — Abstract explicitly states 'Shotgun metagenomics and fecal microbiota transplantation revealed the existence of the altered gut microbiota-Kyn relation'. Sample title: 'Gut microbiota from Mus musculus'. Library source is METAGENOMIC (community DNA), not genomic. || BODY_SITE: gut_explicit — Sample title explicitly states 'Gut microbiota from Mus musculus'. Abstract mentions 'fecal microbiota transplantation' and 'gut microbiota'. Body site field absent but isolation source and sample naming unambiguously identify gut. || HOST: Mus musculus [single_clear] — Host field explicitly lists 'Mus musculus'. Sample title confirms 'Gut microbiota from Mus musculus'. Single host organism, no multi-species survey indicated. || METHOD: agree — Abstract describes 'Shotgun metagenomics' as method. ENA WGS/shotgun sample count: 1, library_strategy_mix: WGS:1, library_source: METAGENOMIC. All fields consistent with stated shotgun metagenomics approach on community (fecal) DNA. || SUMMARY: This is a legitimate single-host (Mus musculus) animal gut microbiome study using shotgun WGS on fecal samples. The study investigates gut microbiota composition in relation to cardiac remodeling, with explicit sampling and sequencing methods matching the ENA metadata.	none					No reporting errors detected. The mouse host is appropriate for this animal-model study of human cardiac disease, and all samples are correctly labeled as gut microbiota.	1		False		True	False
PRJNA255439	Homo sapiens	9606.0		USA: Maryland	32	WGS	pubmed_postdoc	26336637.0	26336637.0	Composition, taxonomy and functional diversity of the oropharynx microbiome in individuals with schizophrenia and controls.	Castro-Nallar E	Crandall KA	Mammalia	The role of the human microbiome in schizophrenia remains largely unexplored. The microbiome has been shown to alter brain development and modulate behavior and cognition in animals through gut-brain connections, and research in humans suggests that it may be a modulating factor in many disorders. This study reports findings from a shotgun metagenomic analysis of the oropharyngeal microbiome in 16 individuals with schizophrenia and 16 controls. High-level differences were evident at both the phylum and genus levels, with Proteobacteria, Firmicutes, Bacteroidetes, and Actinobacteria dominating both schizophrenia patients and controls, and Ascomycota being more abundant in schizophrenia patients than controls. Controls were richer in species but less even in their distributions, i.e., dominated by fewer species, as opposed to schizophrenia patients. Lactic acid bacteria were relatively more abundant in schizophrenia, including species of Lactobacilli and Bifidobacterium, which have been shown to modulate chronic inflammation. We also found Eubacterium halii, a lactate-utilizing species. Functionally, the microbiome of schizophrenia patients was characterized by an increased number of metabolic pathways related to metabolite transport systems including siderophores, glutamate, and vitamin B12. In contrast, carbohydrate and lipid pathways and energy metabolism were abundant in controls. These findings suggest that the oropharyngeal microbiome in individuals with schizophrenia is significantly different compared to controls, and that particular microbial species and metabolic pathways differentiate both groups. Confirmation of these findings in larger and more diverse samples, e.g., gut microbiome, will contribute to elucidating potential links between schizophrenia and the human microbiota.	2015.0	PeerJ	YES		METAGENOMIC	YES	Metazoa	Chordata	Primates	Hominidae	Homo	Homo sapiens	10.7717/peerj.1140				WGS:32	32	1	Homo sapiens		human metagenome		ES_001; ES_007; ES_010; ES_014; ES_017	32.0	https://doi.org/10.7717/peerj.1140	True	True	Homo sapiens	True	human	True	human	False		False	ENA + paper	Shotgun metagenomic sequencing was performed on oropharyngeal microbiome samples from 16 individuals with schizophrenia and 16 controls.	UNIFORM	32	METAGENOMIC:32	32	32	EXCLUDE	no	high	non_gut	single_clear	Homo sapiens	True	agree	GUT: no (high) — Paper title explicitly states 'oropharynx microbiome', and abstract confirms 'shotgun metagenomic analysis of the oropharyngeal microbiome'. The oropharynx is the throat region, not the gut. While the abstract mentions gut-brain connections and cites animal studies, the actual samples analyzed are from the oropharynx (oral/throat), not gut. || BODY_SITE: non_gut — Title and abstract both explicitly identify the sampling site as 'oropharynx' and 'oropharyngeal microbiome'. The oropharynx is an oral/throat anatomical site, not the gastrointestinal tract. || HOST: Homo sapiens [single_clear] (HUMAN) — Host field explicitly lists 'Homo sapiens'; host species in samples shows '1 distinct' entry of 'Homo sapiens'; study compares 16 individuals with schizophrenia and 16 controls (n=32 humans total). || METHOD: agree — Paper states 'shotgun metagenomic analysis'; ENA library_strategy_mix shows 'WGS:32' (whole-genome shotgun); library_source marked 'METAGENOMIC' (community DNA); sample count aligns at 32 samples across both groups. || SUMMARY: This is a valid human microbiome study but sampled from the oropharynx (oral/throat), not the gut. It therefore does not qualify as an animal gut microbiome study and should be excluded from a gut-focused catalog.	gut_sample_issue			Paper title and abstract explicitly state 'oropharyngeal microbiome' and 'oropharynx'. Deposit body-sites field is empty (no metadata confirming oral/oropharyngeal site). Sample titles (ES_001, ES_007, etc.) provide no indication of body site.		Valid reporting issue: paper describes oropharyngeal microbiome samples but deposit body-sites field is empty, creating a metadata gap for a non-gut oral site study.	1		False		True	False
PRJNA645015	Apis mellifera	7460.0		China: Jilin	125	WGS	pubmed_postdoc	34784973.0	34784973.0	Honey bee genetics shape the strain-level structure of gut microbiota in social transmission.	Wu J	Zheng H	Insecta	"BACKGROUND: Honey bee gut microbiota transmitted via social interactions are beneficial to the host health. Although the microbial community is relatively stable, individual variations and high strain-level diversity have been detected across honey bees. Although the bee gut microbiota structure is influenced by environmental factors, the heritability of the gut members and the contribution of the host genetics remains elusive. Considering bees within a colony are not readily genetically identical due to the polyandry of the queen, we hypothesize that the microbiota structure can be shaped by host genetics. RESULTS: We used shotgun metagenomics to simultaneously profile the microbiota and host genotypes of bees from hives of four different subspecies. Gut composition is more distant between genetically different bees at both phylotype- and ""sequence-discrete population"" levels. We then performed a successive passaging experiment within colonies of hybrid bees generated by artificial insemination, which revealed that the microbial composition dramatically shifts across batches of bees during the social transmission. Specifically, different strains from the phylotype of Snodgrassella alvi are preferentially selected by genetically varied hosts, and strains from different hosts show a remarkably biased distribution of single-nucleotide polymorphism in the Type IV pili loci. Genome-wide association analysis identified that the relative abundance of a cluster of Bifidobacterium strains is associated with the host glutamate receptor gene specifically expressed in the bee brain. Finally, mono-colonization of Bifidobacterium with a specific polysaccharide utilization locus impacts the alternative splicing of the gluR-B gene, which is associated with an increased GABA level in the brain. CONCLUSIONS: Our results indicated that host genetics influence the bee gut composition and suggest a gut-brain connection implicated in the gut bacterial strain preference. Honey bees have been used extensively as a model organism for social behaviors, genetics, and the gut microbiome. Further identification of host genetic function as a shaping force of microbial structure will advance our understanding of the host-microbe interactions. Video abstract."	2021.0	Microbiome	YES		METAGENOMIC	YES	Metazoa	Arthropoda	Hymenoptera	Apidae	Apis	Apis mellifera	10.1186/s40168-021-01174-y				WGS:125	125	1	Apis mellifera		insect gut metagenome		MIMS Environmental/Metagenome sample from insect gut metagenome	125.0	https://doi.org/10.1186/s40168-021-01174-y	True	True	Apis mellifera	True	honey bee	True	honey bee	False		False	ENA + paper	Sequenced samples came from gut microbiota of honey bees from different subspecies and hybrid colonies via shotgun metagenomics.	UNIFORM	125	METAGENOMIC:125	125	125	KEEP	yes	high	gut_explicit	single_clear	Apis mellifera	False	agree	GUT: yes (high) — Paper title explicitly states 'Honey bee genetics shape the strain-level structure of gut microbiota'; abstract confirms 'Honey bee gut microbiota' and 'gut composition'; biome label states 'insect gut metagenome'; sample titles state 'insect gut metagenome'. This is unambiguously a gut microbiome study. || BODY_SITE: gut_explicit — Multiple explicit mentions: paper title 'gut microbiota', abstract 'bee gut microbiota', 'Gut composition', biome label 'insect gut metagenome', sample titles 'insect gut metagenome'. No ambiguity regarding body site. || HOST: Apis mellifera [single_clear] — Host field explicitly lists 'Apis mellifera' (honey bee); host species in samples shows 1 distinct species: 'Apis mellifera'. Paper compares strains across multiple subspecies of the same species but all samples are from a single host species. Stated clearly and unambiguously throughout. || METHOD: agree — Abstract states 'We used shotgun metagenomics to simultaneously profile the microbiota'; ENA library_strategy_mix confirms 'WGS:125' (shotgun metagenomics); library_source correctly marked 'METAGENOMIC'; sample count of 125 WGS samples matches the study scope. Methods and metadata are congruent. || SUMMARY: This is a straightforward, high-confidence animal gut metagenomics study of honey bee (Apis mellifera) using 125 shotgun metagenomic samples from gut tissue. All four criteria are clearly supported by explicit evidence with no contradictions or ambiguities.	none					No reporting errors detected. Paper and deposit are fully concordant on host identity (Apis mellifera) and sample type (gut metagenome).	1		False		True	False
PRJDB20287	Locusta migratoria	7004.0		Japan	1	WGS	pubmed_postdoc	41696020.0	41696020.0	Behavioural phase transitions in the migratory locust, Locusta migratoria, are related to changes in the gut bacterial composition.	Kim J	Mori H	Insecta	Locusta migratoria is a grasshopper species that can change its behaviour from solitary to gregarious. Previous studies have implicated metabolites such as serotonin and dopamine in the regulation of behavioural transition in this species. While many studies using cultured microbes have demonstrated that some microbes harbor the neuroactive metabolic potential of these neurotransmitters, the association between microbial community composition and phase transition remains poorly understood. Here, we employed 16S rRNA gene amplicon sequencing and shotgun metagenomic sequencing analyses to compare the composition of gut microbial communities of L. migratoria in different behavioural phases. We found that Serratia ureilytica was enriched in the gut of gregarious individuals in contrast to the decreased presence of Klebsiella aerogenes, one of the most abundant taxa in wild individuals. The gut microbiome of gregarious individuals was functionally characterised by enriched kynurenine and tryptophan synthesis pathways, and by reduced representation of GABA, indole, and dopamine metabolism pathways compared with that of solitary individuals. These compositional changes were consistent with the enrichment of S. ureilytica and depletion of K. aerogenes, which possess the corresponding genes. In particular, the genes for kynurenine synthesis encoded by S. ureilytica specific to the gregarious phase, are known to be involved in the tryptophan production and are associated with reduced serotonin synthesis. These results highlight a distinct shift in both the taxonomic and functional composition of the gut microbiome across behavioural phases and suggest a potential microbial contribution to the behavioural changes of L. migratoria.	2026.0	ISME communications	YES		METAGENOMIC	YES	Metazoa	Arthropoda	Orthoptera	Acrididae	Locusta	Locusta migratoria	10.1093/ismeco/ycag009				WGS:1	1	1	Locusta migratoria		insect gut metagenome		hindgut metagenome sample of the grasshopper Lab-23-Gre-Female-4	1.0	https://doi.org/10.1093/ismeco/ycag009	True	True	Locusta migratoria	True	Locusta migratoria	True	Locusta migratoria	False		False	ENA + paper	Gut microbial samples were sequenced directly from Locusta migratoria individuals in different behavioural phases using 16S rRNA and shotgun metagenomic sequencing.	UNIFORM	1	METAGENOMIC:1	1	1	KEEP	yes	high	gut_explicit	single_clear	Locusta migratoria	False	agree	GUT: yes (high) — Paper title explicitly states 'gut bacterial composition' in Locusta migratoria. Abstract confirms 'gut microbial communities' analyzed via '16S rRNA gene amplicon sequencing and shotgun metagenomic sequencing'. Sample title specifies 'hindgut metagenome sample'. Biome label: 'insect gut metagenome'. || BODY_SITE: gut_explicit — Sample title explicitly states 'hindgut metagenome sample of the grasshopper'. Abstract refers to 'gut microbial communities'. Biome label confirms 'insect gut metagenome'. || HOST: Locusta migratoria [single_clear] — Host field: 'Locusta migratoria'. Host species in samples: '1 distinct: Locusta migratoria'. Paper title and abstract consistently refer to this single grasshopper species. Sample title: 'the grasshopper Lab-23-Gre-Female-4' (individual specimen from single species). || METHOD: agree — Abstract states 'shotgun metagenomic sequencing analyses' performed. ENA library_strategy_mix shows 'WGS:1' (whole genome shotgun). library_source: 'METAGENOMIC' (community DNA) is consistent with shotgun metagenomics of gut bacterial community. WGS sample count of 1 aligns with single hindgut metagenome sample described. || SUMMARY: This is a genuine animal gut microbiome study using shotgun metagenomics to characterize bacterial community composition in Locusta migratoria hindgut across behavioural phases. All four criteria are clearly satisfied with explicit evidence and high confidence.	none					No reporting errors detected. Paper and deposit metadata are fully consistent for host identity, sample type, and completeness.	2	1.0	False		True	False
PRJNA1040965	C57/BL6			Canada: Montreal	20	WGS	pubmed_postdoc	39723822.0	39723822.0	Dextran sodium sulfate-induced colitis alters the proportion and composition of replicating gut bacteria.	Beauchemin ET	Maurice CF	Mammalia	The bacteria living in the human gut are essential for host health. Though the composition and metabolism of these bacteria are well described in both healthy hosts and those with intestinal disease, less is known about the metabolic activity of the gut bacteria prior to, and during, disease development-especially regarding gut bacterial replication. Here, we use a recently developed single-cell technique alongside existing metagenomics-based tools to identify, track, and quantify replicating gut bacteria both ex vivo and in situ in the dextran sodium sulfate (DSS) mouse model of colitis. We show that the proportion of replicating gut bacteria decreases when mice have the highest levels of inflammation and returns to baseline levels as mice begin recovering. In addition, we report significant alterations in the composition of the replicating gut bacterial community ex vivo during colitis development. On the taxa level, we observe significant changes in the abundance of taxa such as the mucus-degrading Akkermansia and the poorly described Erysipelatoclostridium genus. We further demonstrate that many taxa exhibit variable replication rates in situ during colitis, including Akkermansia muciniphila. Lastly, we show that colitis development is positively correlated with increases in the presence and abundance of bacteria in situ which are predicted to be fast replicators. This could suggest that taxa with the potential to replicate quickly may have an advantage during intestinal inflammation. These data support the need for additional research using activity-based approaches to further characterize the gut bacterial response to intestinal inflammation and its consequences for both the host and the gut microbial community.IMPORTANCEIt is well known that the bacteria living inside the gut are important for human health. Indeed, the type of bacteria that are present and their metabolism are different in healthy people versus those with intestinal disease. However, less is known about how these gut bacteria are replicating, especially as someone begins to develop intestinal disease. This is particularly important as it is thought that metabolically active gut bacteria may be more relevant to health. Here, we begin to address this gap using several complementary approaches to characterize the replicating gut bacteria in a mouse model of intestinal inflammation. We reveal which gut bacteria are replicating, and how quickly, as mice develop and recover from inflammation. This work can serve as a model for future research to identify how actively growing gut bacteria may be impacting health, or why these particular bacteria tend to thrive during intestinal inflammation.	2025.0	mSphere	YES		METAGENOMIC	YES	Animalia	Chordata	Rodentia	Muridae	Mus	musculus	10.1128/msphere.00825-24				WGS:20	20	0			mouse gut metagenome			20.0	https://doi.org/10.1128/msphere.00825-24	True	True	C57/BL6	False		True	mouse (model of colitis)	True	colitis	False	ENA + paper	[model of colitis] Metagenomics samples were sequenced from mice in a DSS-induced colitis model, not from human subjects.	UNIFORM	20	METAGENOMIC:20	20	20	KEEP	yes	high	gut_explicit	single_clear	Mus musculus (C57BL/6 mouse)	False	agree	GUT: yes (high) — Abstract explicitly states 'gut bacteria' and 'gut bacterial replication'; biome label 'mouse gut metagenome'; study design uses DSS mouse model of colitis to track replicating bacteria in gut environment. || BODY_SITE: gut_explicit — Paper title includes 'gut bacteria'; abstract repeatedly references 'gut bacteria,' 'gut microbiome,' and sampling 'in situ' within DSS-induced colitis model; biome label explicitly 'mouse gut metagenome'. || HOST: Mus musculus (C57BL/6 mouse) [single_clear] — Host field states 'C57/BL6' (a standard inbred mouse strain); biome label 'mouse gut metagenome'; abstract describes 'DSS mouse model of colitis'; no evidence of multi-host comparison or incidental sampling. || METHOD: agree — ENA fields show library_strategy_mix WGS:20 with 20 WGS shotgun samples and library_source METAGENOMIC, consistent with abstract description of 'metagenomics-based tools' applied to community DNA from mouse gut samples. || SUMMARY: This is a genuine single-host (C57BL/6 mouse) gut microbiome study using WGS metagenomics to investigate bacterial replication dynamics during DSS-induced colitis. All four criteria align clearly with the evidence provided.	none					No reporting errors detected. Mouse host in deposit correctly matches the DSS mouse model study design in the paper.	1		False		True	False
PRJNA712972	C57BL/6J			Japan:Tokyo	6	WGS	pubmed_postdoc	35752737.0	35752737.0	Gut microbiota alternation under the intestinal epithelium-specific knockout of mouse Piga gene.	Jangid A	Prakash T	Mammalia	Crosstalk between the gut microbiota and intestinal epithelium shapes the gut environment and profoundly influences the intestinal immune homeostasis. Glycosylphosphatidylinositol anchored proteins (GPI - APs) contribute to a variety of gut-associated immune functions, including microbial surveillance and defense, and epithelial cell polarity. Properly polarised epithelial cells are essential for the establishment of the barrier function of gut epithelia. The Piga gene is one among seven genes that encode for an enzyme which is involved in the first step of GPI-anchor biosynthesis. This is the first study reporting a knockout of the intestinal epithelial cell-specific Piga gene (Piga-/-) and its association with the gut microbiota in mice using a whole metagenome shotgun-based sequencing approach. An overall reduced microbiota diversity has been observed in the Piga-/- group as compared to the control group (ANOVA p = 0.34). The taxonomic biomarkers, namely: Gammaproteobacteria (class), Enterobacterales (order), Enterobacteriaceae (family), Escherichia (genus), Proteus (genus) and Escherichia coli (species), increased more in the Piga-/- mice as compared to in the control group. Further, the pathogenic E. coli strains, namely E. coli O157:H7 str. EDL 933 (EHEC), E. coli CFT073 (UPEC) and E. coli 536 (UPEC), were found in the Piga-/- mice which also harbored virulence factor transporters. In addition, the taxa responsible for short chain fatty acid production were decreased in the Piga-/- group. The Piga-/- mice gut harbored an increased number of microbial functions responsible for the survival of pathogens in the inflamed gut environment. Our observations clearly indicate that the Piga-/- mice gut might have an overall enhancement in pathogenic behaviour and reduced capabilities beneficial to health.	2022.0	Scientific reports	YES		METAGENOMIC	YES	Animalia	Chordata	Rodentia	Muridae	Mus	musculus	10.1038/s41598-022-15150-5				WGS:6	6	0			mouse gut metagenome	feces	Metagenomic sample (PIGA-ko-1); Metagenomic sample (PIGA-ko-2); Metagenomic sample (PIGA-ko-3); Metagenomic sample (WT_1); Metagenomic sample (WT_2)	6.0	https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-022-15150-5	True	True	C57BL/6J	True	mouse (model of intestinal barrier dysfunction)	True	mouse (model of intestinal barrier dysfunction)	True	intestinal barrier dysfunction	False	ENA + paper	[model of intestinal barrier dysfunction] Metagenomic shotgun sequencing was performed on fecal samples from intestinal epithelium-specific Piga-knockout mice and wild-type control mice.	UNIFORM	6	METAGENOMIC:6	6	6	KEEP	yes	high	gut_explicit	single_clear	Mus musculus (mouse)	False	agree	GUT: yes (high) — Paper title explicitly states 'Gut microbiota alternation...in mouse'; abstract describes 'gut microbiota and intestinal epithelium' study; isolation_source field states 'feces'; biome_label states 'mouse gut metagenome'; library_source is METAGENOMIC (community DNA, not single organism genome). || BODY_SITE: gut_explicit — Title and abstract both explicitly reference 'gut microbiota' and 'intestinal epithelium'; isolation_source explicitly states 'feces'; biome_label explicitly states 'mouse gut metagenome'. || HOST: Mus musculus (mouse) [single_clear] — Host field states 'C57BL/6J' (a well-defined inbred mouse strain); paper title states 'mouse Piga gene'; abstract references 'mice' and 'Piga-/-' knockout mice; sample titles identify genotypes (PIGA-ko vs WT) but all from single species. This is a comparative study OF multiple animals' guts (knockout vs wildtype), not a multi-host survey with mice as incidental; thus host_id is single_clear, not multi_host. || METHOD: agree — Abstract states 'whole metagenome shotgun-based sequencing'; library_strategy_mix field shows 'WGS:6' (6 WGS samples); library_source is 'METAGENOMIC'. All align with shotgun metagenomics approach described. || SUMMARY: This is a genuine mouse gut microbiome study using shotgun metagenomics on fecal samples comparing Piga knockout vs wildtype animals. Single host species (C57BL/6J mice), gut body site explicitly confirmed, methods match ENA fields.	none					No reporting errors detected. Paper describes a mouse gut microbiota study with intestinal epithelial Piga knockout; deposit metadata (C57BL/6J host, fecal samples, gut biome) are consistent and complete.	1		False		True	False
PRJDB20379	Balaenoptera acutorostrata	9767.0		Japan:Hokkaido, Menashi, Rausu	63	WGS	pubmed_postdoc	40785991.0	40785991.0	"Metagenomic Insights Into the Role of Gut Microbes in the Defensive Ink ""Tsunabi"" of Physeteroid Whales."	Takeuchi H	Hayakawa T	Mammalia	"Whales of the superfamily Physeteroidea, which includes the genera Physeter and Kogia, exhibit a unique visual defense mechanism involving the release of dark reddish-brown feces (locally called ""tsunabi-ink"" in Japan) into the water to obscure themselves from predators and other threats. However, the mechanism underlying pigmentation remains unknown. Because physeteroids possess an enlarged distal colon that retains fecal material, a possible explanation is that symbiont microbial metabolism contributes to the feces pigmentation. To investigate this, we provided a shotgun metagenomic catalog of gut microbiomes from the intestinal tracts of eight cetacean species, including two physeteroids: a sperm whale (Physeter macrocephalus) and a pygmy sperm whale (Kogia breviceps). The colonic microbiome of physeteroids exhibited relatively high abundances of tryptophan metabolism genes, particularly indolepyruvate ferredoxin oxidoreductases (iorA and iorB), suggesting that physeteroids accumulate indole-3-pyruvate-derived pigments in their colons. Furthermore, bacterial members of the phyla Bacillota and Bacteroidota were identified in the physeteroid colon as primary taxa conferring heavy-metal resistance, which may be related to the primary predation of physeteroids on cephalopods, which bioaccumulate high levels of heavy metals. Prolonged fecal retention can expose gut microbes to chronic heavy-metal stress and colonize them as heavy metal-tolerant microbial communities, some of which may produce pigments to reduce their toxicity. Thus, we propose that tsunabi-ink is a metabolic byproduct of shifts in the gut microbial community, influenced by the host's digestive physiology and foraging behavior through sustained ecological interactions with gut symbionts. Moreover, we believe that further empirical investigation would validate this hypothesis."	2025.0	Ecology and evolution	YES		METAGENOMIC	YES	Metazoa	Chordata	Artiodactyla	Balaenopteridae	Balaenoptera	Balaenoptera acutorostrata	10.1002/ece3.71910				WGS:63	63	8	Balaenoptera acutorostrata; Kogia breviceps; Mesoplodon stejnegeri; Phocoena phocoena; Phocoenoides dalli; Physeter macrocephalus; Sagmatias obliquidens; Ziphius cavirostris		cetacean metagenome	Cecum; Colon; Colon Ink Sac; Duodenum; Gut 1; Gut 2; Gut 3; Gut 4; Gut 5; Ileum; Jejunum; Rectum	Balaenoptera acutorostrata Cecum a; Balaenoptera acutorostrata Cecum b; Balaenoptera acutorostrata Colon a; Balaenoptera acutorostrata Colon b; Balaenoptera acutorostrata Duodenum a	63.0	https://doi.org/10.1002/ece3.71910	True	True	Balaenoptera acutorostrata	True	Physeteroid whales	True	sperm whale (Physeter macrocephalus); pygmy sperm whale (Kogia breviceps)	False		False	ENA + paper	Metagenomic samples were sequenced from the intestinal tracts and colons of eight cetacean species, including two physeteroids.	DIVERSE	63	METAGENOMIC:63	63	63	REVIEW						False		GUT:  () —  || BODY_SITE:  —  || HOST:  [] —  || METHOD:  —  || SUMMARY: PARSE_ERROR	none					PARSE_ERROR	1		False		True	True
PRJNA1061805	Holothuria glaberrima	31192.0		Puerto Rico: Fajardo	3	WGS	pubmed_postdoc	38690316.0	38690316.0	Sea cucumber (Holothuria glaberrima) intestinal microbiome dataset from Puerto Rico, generated by shotgun sequencing.	Rivera-Lopez EO	Rios-Velazquez C	Holothuroidea	The sea cucumber (H. glaberrima) is a species found in the shallow waters near coral reefs and seagrass beds in Puerto Rico. To characterize the microbial taxonomic composition and functional profiles present in the sea cucumber, total DNA was obtained from their intestinal system, fosmid libraries constructed, and subsequent sequencing was performed. The diversity profile displayed that the most predominant domain was Bacteria (76.56 %), followed by Viruses (23.24 %) and Archaea (0.04 %). Within the 11 phyla identified, the most abundant was Proteobacteria (73.16 %), followed by Terrabacteria group (3.20 %) and Fibrobacterota, Chlorobiota, Bacteroidota (FCB) superphylum (1.02 %). The most abundant species were Porvidencia rettgeri (21.77 %), Pseudomonas stutzeri (14.78 %), and Alcaligenes faecalis (5.00 %). The functional profile revealed that the most abundant functions are related to transporters, MISC (miscellaneous information systems), organic nitrogen, energy, and carbon utilization. The data collected in this project on the diversity and functional profiles of the intestinal system of the H. glaberrima provided a detailed view of its microbial ecology. These findings may motivate comparative studies aimed at understanding the role of the microbiome in intestinal regeneration.	2024.0	Data in brief	YES		METAGENOMIC	YES	Metazoa	Echinodermata	Aspidochirotida	Holothuriidae	Holothuria	Holothuria glaberrima	10.1016/j.dib.2024.110421				WGS:3	3	1	Holothuria glaberrima		metagenome	H. glaberrima eviscerated gut	Metagenome or environmental sample from metagenome	3.0	https://doi.org/10.1016/j.dib.2024.110421	True	True	Holothuria glaberrima	True	Holothuria glaberrima	True	Holothuria glaberrima	False		False	ENA + paper	Sequenced samples came from the intestinal system of sea cucumbers (Holothuria glaberrima) collected from Puerto Rico.	UNIFORM	3	METAGENOMIC:3	3	3	KEEP	yes	high	gut_explicit	single_clear	Holothuria glaberrima	False	agree	GUT: yes (high) — Abstract explicitly states 'total DNA was obtained from their intestinal system' and isolation source confirms 'H. glaberrima eviscerated gut'. Library source is METAGENOMIC (community DNA), confirming microbiome study, not host genome. || BODY_SITE: gut_explicit — Isolation source field states 'H. glaberrima eviscerated gut'; abstract describes sampling from 'intestinal system'. || HOST: Holothuria glaberrima [single_clear] — Title, abstract, and host field all consistently identify H. glaberrima (sea cucumber) as the single host species. Host species count shows 1 distinct species. No multi-host survey; this is a focused study of one species' gut microbiome. || METHOD: agree — Paper describes shotgun sequencing of intestinal metagenomes; ENA fields confirm WGS (shotgun) with 3 metagenomic samples and library_strategy_mix of WGS:3. No contradiction detected. || SUMMARY: This is a straightforward, single-host animal (non-human) gut microbiome study using shotgun WGS on sea cucumber intestinal samples. All metadata and narrative evidence align consistently.	none					No quality issues detected; host identity, sample type, and biological source are all consistent between paper and deposit.	1		False		True	False
PRJEB37383	Mus musculus			USA	12	WGS	pubmed_postdoc	35858888.0	35858888.0	Activity-based protein profiling identifies alternating activation of enzymes involved in the bifidobacterium shunt pathway or mucin degradation in the gut microbiome response to soluble dietary fiber.	Killinger BJ	Wright AT	Mammalia	"While deprivation of dietary fiber has been associated with adverse health outcomes, investigations concerning the effect of dietary fiber on the gut microbiome have been largely limited to compositional sequence-based analyses or utilize a defined microbiota not native to the host. To extend understanding of the microbiome's functional response to dietary fiber deprivation beyond correlative evidence from sequence-based analyses, approaches capable of measuring functional enzymatic activity are needed. In this study, we use an activity-based protein profiling (ABPP) approach to identify sugar metabolizing and transport proteins in native mouse gut microbiomes that respond with differential activity to the deprivation or supplementation of the soluble dietary fibers inulin and pectin. We found that the microbiome of mice subjected to a high fiber diet high in soluble fiber had increased functional activity of multiple proteins, including glycoside hydrolases, polysaccharide lyases, and sugar transport proteins from diverse taxa. The results point to an increase in activity of the Bifidobacterium shunt metabolic pathway in the microbiome of mice fed high fiber diets. In those subjected to a low fiber diet, we identified a shift from the degradation of dietary fibers to that of gut mucins, in particular by the recently isolated taxon ""Musculibacterium intestinale"", which experienced dramatic growth in response to fiber deprivation. When combined with metabolomics and shotgun metagenomics analyses, our findings provide a functional investigation of dietary fiber metabolism in the gut microbiome and demonstrates the power of a combined ABPP-multiomics approach for characterizing the response of the gut microbiome to perturbations."	2022.0	NPJ biofilms and microbiomes	YES		METAGENOMIC	YES	Animalia	Chordata	Rodentia	Muridae	Mus	musculus	10.1038/s41522-022-00313-z				WGS:12	12	0			Mus musculus		Mouse gut microbiome in response to high or low fiber diets	12.0	https://doi.org/10.1038/s41522-022-00313-z	True	True	Mus musculus	False		True	mouse	False		False	ENA + paper	Sequenced samples came from gut microbiomes of mice fed different dietary fiber diets.	UNIFORM	12	METAGENOMIC:12	12	12	KEEP	yes	high	gut_explicit	single_clear	Mus musculus	False	agree	GUT: yes (high) — Paper title and abstract explicitly state 'gut microbiome' study in mouse. Abstract: 'native mouse gut microbiomes' and 'Mouse gut microbiome in response to high or low fiber diets' (sample titles). || BODY_SITE: gut_explicit — Title includes 'gut microbiome'; abstract states 'mouse gut microbiomes'; sample titles state 'Mouse gut microbiome'. No ambiguity about body site. || HOST: Mus musculus [single_clear] — Host field explicitly states 'Mus musculus'. Abstract and sample titles consistently reference 'mouse' or 'mouse gut microbiome'. All 12 samples are from the same host species in a dietary intervention study. || METHOD: agree — Paper describes use of activity-based protein profiling (ABPP) on native mouse gut microbiomes; ENA metadata shows library_strategy_mix includes WGS (shotgun metagenomics), which is appropriate for metagenomic community DNA (library_source: METAGENOMIC). Twelve WGS samples are consistent with the described study design. || SUMMARY: This is a genuine single-host animal gut microbiome study using shotgun metagenomics to profile functional protein activity in mouse cecal/colonic microbiota under dietary fiber manipulation. All four criteria are clearly satisfied with high confidence.	none					No reporting errors detected. Paper and deposit are in full agreement: a mouse gut microbiome study with appropriate host specification and gut-sample c