PRJEB52269	Mus musculus			Sweden	64	WGS	pubmed_postdoc	36624306.0	36624306.0	High-fat diet and estrogen modulate the gut microbiota in a sex-dependent manner in mice.	Hases L	Williams C	Mammalia	A high-fat diet can lead to gut microbiota dysbiosis, chronic intestinal inflammation, and metabolic syndrome. Notably, resulting phenotypes, such as glucose and insulin levels, colonic crypt cell proliferation, and macrophage infiltration, exhibit sex differences, and females are less affected. This is, in part, attributed to sex hormones. To investigate if there are sex differences in the microbiota and if estrogenic ligands can attenuate high-fat diet-induced dysbiosis, we used whole-genome shotgun sequencing to characterize the impact of diet, sex, and estrogenic ligands on the microbial composition of the cecal content of mice. We here report clear host sex differences along with remarkably sex-dependent responses to high-fat diet. Females, specifically, exhibited increased abundance of Blautia hansenii, and its levels correlated negatively with insulin levels in both sexes. Estrogen treatment had a modest impact on the microbiota diversity but altered a few important species in males. This included Collinsella aerofaciens F, which we show correlated with colonic macrophage infiltration. In conclusion, male and female mice exhibit clear differences in their cecal microbial composition and in how diet and estrogens impact the composition. Further, specific microbial strains are significantly correlated with metabolic parameters.	2023.0	Communications biology	YES		METAGENOMIC	YES	Animalia	Chordata	Rodentia	Muridae	Mus	musculus	10.1038/s42003-022-04406-5				WGS:64	64	0			Mus musculus		DPN10_M; DPN16_M; DPN17_M; DPN18_M; DPN19_M	64.0	https://doi.org/10.1038/s42003-022-04406-5	True	True	Mus musculus	True	mice	True	mice	True		False	ENA + paper	[model organism, disease unnamed] Whole-genome shotgun sequencing was performed on cecal content samples from mice exposed to high-fat diet and estrogen treatments.	UNIFORM	64	METAGENOMIC:64	64	64	KEEP	yes	high	gut_explicit	single_clear	Mus musculus	False	agree	GUT: yes (high) — Abstract explicitly states 'whole-genome shotgun sequencing to characterize the impact of diet, sex, and estrogenic ligands on the microbial composition of the cecal content of mice.' Cecal content is definitively gut material. Library source is METAGENOMIC (community DNA), confirming microbiome study. || BODY_SITE: gut_explicit — Abstract states 'microbial composition of the cecal content of mice.' Cecum is part of the large intestine (gut). This is explicit anatomical designation, not inference. || HOST: Mus musculus [single_clear] — Host field explicitly lists 'Mus musculus'. Title confirms 'in mice'. Study design compares males vs females of the same species (sex-dependent analysis), which is a comparative study OF one host species's microbiota across treatment groups, not an incidental multi-host survey. || METHOD: agree — Paper describes 'whole-genome shotgun sequencing' (WGS) to characterize cecal microbiota. ENA fields show library_strategy_mix = WGS:64 and library_source = METAGENOMIC with 64 WGS samples, matching the stated shotgun metagenomic approach. || SUMMARY: This is a genuine single-host (mouse) gut microbiome study using WGS metagenomic sequencing on cecal content. The comparative sex-dependent analysis is within-species, not a multi-host survey. All metadata and methods align clearly.	none					No reporting errors detected. Paper and deposit are consistent: mouse (Mus musculus) gut microbiota study with appropriate animal-model design for investigating diet and sex-hormone effects.	1		False		True	False
PRJNA881985	Mus musculus	10090.0		Not applicable	119	WGS	pubmed_postdoc	40890565.0	40890565.0	Ketogenic Diet Modulates Gut Microbiota-Brain Metabolite Axis in a Sex- and Genotype-Specific Manner in APOE4 Mice.	Ivanich K	Lin AL	Mammalia	The apolipoprotein E4 (APOE4) allele is the strongest genetic risk factor for late-onset Alzheimer's disease (AD), associated with early brain metabolic dysfunction and gut microbiome alterations. Targeting these early changes through dietary interventions may reduce AD risk in asymptomatic carriers. This study evaluated whether a ketogenic diet (KD) could reshape the gut microbiome and enhance key brain metabolite levels in young APOE4 mice, using APOE3 mice as a neutral-risk comparison. Male and female APOE3 and APOE4 mice were fed either a control diet or KD for 16 weeks, starting at 12 weeks of age. We used shotgun metagenomics and targeted brain metabolomics to identify microbe-metabolite signatures linked to neuroprotection. KD increased beneficial species such as Lactobacillus johnsonii and Lactobacillus reuteri while reducing pathogenic Bacteroides intestinalis. These microbial shifts correlated with improved brain metabolites related to mitochondrial function, neurotransmitter balance, redox homeostasis, and lipid metabolism. Notably, Lactobacillus species and B. intestinalis exhibited inverse correlations with key brain metabolite levels, suggesting their roles as both modulators and biomarkers of brain health. APOE4 females showed the greatest benefits, including restored microbiome diversity and normalization of brain metabolite levels. In contrast, APOE3 mice showed microbiome changes but limited brain metabolic responses. These findings highlight KD's potential to reprogram the gut-brain axis in a genotype- and sex-dependent manner, supporting its use as a precision nutrition strategy to reduce AD risk, particularly in asymptomatic female APOE4 carriers.	2025.0	Journal of neurochemistry	YES		METAGENOMIC	YES	Metazoa	Chordata	Rodentia	Muridae	Mus	Mus musculus	10.1111/jnc.70216				WGS:119	119	1	Mus musculus		mouse gut metagenome		MIMS Environmental/Metagenome sample from mouse gut metagenome	119.0	https://doi.org/10.1111/jnc.70216	True	True	Mus musculus	True	APOE4 mice	True	mouse (model of Alzheimer's disease)	True	Alzheimer's disease	False	ENA + paper	[model of Alzheimer's disease] Shotgun metagenomic sequencing was performed on gut samples from APOE4 and APOE3 transgenic mice fed control or ketogenic diets.	UNIFORM	119	METAGENOMIC:119	119	119	KEEP	yes	high	gut_explicit	single_clear	Mus musculus	False	agree	GUT: yes (high) — Title states 'Gut Microbiota' study; abstract describes 'gut microbiome alterations' and 'reshape the gut microbiome'; biome label explicitly 'mouse gut metagenome'; sample titles reference 'mouse gut metagenome'. || BODY_SITE: gut_explicit — Biome label field: 'mouse gut metagenome'; sample titles: 'sample from mouse gut metagenome'; abstract explicitly discusses gut microbiome sampling and analysis. || HOST: Mus musculus [single_clear] — Host field explicitly 'Mus musculus'; host species in samples: 1 distinct (Mus musculus); study describes controlled experimental design with APOE3 and APOE4 mice strains; no incidental or trace sampling of other animal hosts noted. || METHOD: agree — Abstract states 'used shotgun metagenomics' for analysis; ENA library_strategy_mix shows 'WGS:119' (119 shotgun/WGS samples); library_source marked 'METAGENOMIC' (community DNA, consistent with metagenomics); 119 WGS samples aligns with shotgun metagenomics approach described. || SUMMARY: This is a genuine animal gut microbiome study using shotgun metagenomics on mouse (Mus musculus) gut samples. The study design is a controlled dietary intervention (ketogenic vs. control diet) across two mouse genotypes and both sexes, yielding 119 WGS metagenome samples. All metadata fields are consistent and explicit.	none					No reporting errors detected. Mouse host is appropriate for this ani