PRJNA913821	Rangifer tarandus platyrhynchus	3082113.0		Svalbard	9	WGS	pubmed_postdoc	38031339.0	38031339.0	Gut microbiome biogeography in reindeer supersedes millennia of ecological and evolutionary separation.	Kamenova S	Trosvik P	Mammalia	Ruminants are dependent on their gut microbiomes for nutrient extraction from plant diets. However, knowledge about the composition, diversity, function, and spatial structure of gut microbiomes, especially in wild ruminants, is limited, largely because analysis has been restricted to faeces or the rumen. In two geographically separated reindeer subspecies, 16S rRNA gene amplicon sequencing revealed strong spatial structuring, and pronounced differences in microbial diversity of at least 33 phyla across the stomach, small intestine, and large intestine (including faeces). The main structural feature was the Bacteroidota to Firmicutes ratio, which declined from the stomach to the large intestine, likely reflecting functional adaptation. Metagenome shotgun sequencing also revealed highly significant structuring in the relative occurrence of carbohydrate-active enzymes (CAZymes). CAZymes were enriched in the rumen relative to the small and large intestines. Interestingly, taxonomic diversity was highest in the large intestine, suggesting an important and understudied role for this organ. Despite the two study populations being separated by an ocean and six millennia of evolutionary history, gut microbiome structuring was remarkably consistent. Our study suggests a strong selection for gut microbiome biogeography along the gastrointestinal tract in reindeer subspecies.	2023.0	FEMS microbiology ecology	YES		METAGENOMIC	YES	Metazoa	Chordata	Artiodactyla	Cervidae	Rangifer	Rangifer tarandus	10.1093/femsec/fiad157				WGS:9	9	1	Rangifer tarandus platyrhynchus		metagenome	ID17_LI; ID17_RUMEN; ID17_SI; ID4_LI; ID4_RUMEN; ID4_SI; ID8_LI; ID8_RUMEN; ID8_SI	metagenome	9.0	https://doi.org/10.1093/femsec/fiad157	True	True	Rangifer tarandus platyrhynchus	True	reindeer	True	reindeer	False		False	ENA + paper	Sequenced samples came from gastrointestinal tract tissues and faeces of two geographically separated reindeer subspecies.	UNIFORM	9	METAGENOMIC:9	9	9	KEEP	yes	high	gut_explicit	single_clear	Rangifer tarandus platyrhynchus	False	agree	GUT: yes (high) — Abstract explicitly states 'gut microbiomes' in wild ruminants; samples from stomach, small intestine, large intestine, and rumen (all gut compartments); library_source=METAGENOMIC confirms community DNA analysis; WGS shotgun sequencing explicitly mentioned. || BODY_SITE: gut_explicit — Abstract specifies sampling across 'stomach, small intestine, and large intestine (including faeces)' and 'rumen'; isolation sources confirm anatomical gut sites: ID*_RUMEN, ID*_SI, ID*_LI (large intestine). || HOST: Rangifer tarandus platyrhynchus [single_clear] — Host field and host species both explicitly state Rangifer tarandus platyrhynchus (reindeer subspecies); paper title confirms 'reindeer'; 9 WGS samples from 3 individual animals (ID4, ID8, ID17, each with 3 gut sites sampled); single host species, not incidental. || METHOD: agree — Abstract states 'Metagenome shotgun sequencing' and mentions '16S rRNA gene amplicon sequencing'; ENA library_strategy_mix shows WGS:9 (9 shotgun samples); library_source=METAGENOMIC confirms metagenomic DNA extraction; consistent with stated methods. || SUMMARY: This is a rigorous single-host animal gut microbiome study using shotgun metagenomics (WGS) on reindeer rumen and intestinal samples across multiple anatomical sites. No conflicts between title, abstract, and ENA metadata.	none					No reporting errors detected. Paper and deposit are consistent: reindeer (Rangifer tarandus platyrhynchus) gut samples from rumen, small intestine, and large intestine.	1		False		True	False
PRJNA915351	Mus musculus	10090.0		USA	16	WGS	pubmed_postdoc	37140369.0	37140369.0	Microbiome Alterations Driven by Trypanosoma cruzi Infection in Two Disjunctive Murine Models.	Castañeda S	Ramírez JD	Mammalia	Alterations caused by Trypanosoma cruzi in the composition of gut microbiome may play a vital role in the host-parasite interactions that shapes physiology and immune responses against infection. Thus, a better understanding of this parasite-host-microbiome interaction may yield relevant information in the comprehension of the pathophysiology of the disease and the development of new prophylactic and therapeutic alternatives. Therefore, we implemented a murine model with two mice strains (BALB/c and C57BL/6) to evaluate the impact of Trypanosoma cruzi (Tulahuen strain) infection on the gut microbiome utilizing cytokine profiling and shotgun metagenomics. Higher parasite burdens were observed in cardiac and intestinal tissues, including changes in anti-inflammatory (interleukin-4 [IL-4] and IL-10) and proinflammatory (gamma interferon, tumor necrosis factor alpha, and IL-6) cytokines. Bacterial species such as Bacteroides thetaiotaomicron, Faecalibaculum rodentium, and Lactobacillus johnsonii showed a decrease in relative abundance, while Akkermansia muciniphila and Staphylococcus xylosus increased. Likewise, as infection progressed, there was a decrease in gene abundances related to metabolic processes such as lipid synthesis (including short-chain fatty acids) and amino acid synthesis (including branched-chain amino acids). High-quality metagenomic assembled genomes of L. johnsonii and A. muciniphila among other species were reconstructed, confirming, functional changes associated with metabolic pathways that are directly affected by the loss of abundance of specific bacterial taxa. IMPORTANCE Chagas disease (CD) is caused by the protozoan Trypanosoma cruzi, presenting acute and chronic phases where cardiomyopathy, megaesophagus, and/or megacolon stand out. During the course of its life cycle, the parasite has an important gastrointestinal tract transit that leads to severe forms of CD. The intestinal microbiome plays an essential role in the immunological, physiological, and metabolic homeostasis of the host. Therefore, parasite-host-intestinal microbiome interactions may provide information on certain biological and pathophysiological aspects related to CD. The present study proposes a comprehensive evaluation of the potential effects of this interaction based on metagenomic and immunological data from two mice models with different genetic, immunological, and microbiome backgrounds. Our findings suggest that there are alterations in the immune and microbiome profiles that affect several metabolic pathways that can potentially promote the infection's establishment, progression, and persistence. In addition, this information may prove essential in the research of new prophylactic and therapeutic alternatives for CD.	2023.0	Microbiology spectrum	YES		METAGENOMIC	YES	Metazoa	Chordata	Rodentia	Muridae	Mus	Mus musculus	10.1128/spectrum.00199-23				WGS:16	16	1	Mus musculus		gut metagenome	feces	Control Day 0 BALBc; Control Day 0 c57BL6; Control Day 10 BALBc; Control Day 10 c57BL6; Control Day 16 BALBc	16.0	https://doi.org/10.1128/spectrum.00199-23	True	True	Mus musculus	True	mouse	True	mouse (model of Chagas disease)	True	Chagas disease	False	ENA + paper	[model of Chagas disease] Shotgun metagenomic sequencing was performed on gut samples from BALB/c and C57BL/6 mice infected with Trypanosoma cruzi.	UNIFORM	16	METAGENOMIC:16	16	16	KEEP	yes	high	gut_explicit	multi_host	Mus musculus	False	agree	GUT: yes (high) — Paper title and abstract explicitly state 'gut microbiome' study in murine models. Biome label confirms 'gut metagenome'. Isolation source stated as 'feces', which is the standard source for gut microbiome sampling. || BODY_SITE: gut_explicit — Biome label explicitly states 'gut metagenome'. Isolation source field confirms 'feces'. Abstract discusses 'gut microbiome' composition and 'intestinal tissues'. No ambiguity about body site. || HOST: Mus musculus [multi_host] — Paper explicitly states 'two mice strains (BALB/c and C57BL/6)' and describes a comparative study evaluating impact of infection on gut microbiome across both strains. Sample titles confirm both strains represented (e.g., 'Control Day 0 BALBc', 'Control Day 0 c57BL6'). This is a genuine comparative study OF multiple mouse strains' guts, not an incidental multi-host survey. || METHOD: agree — Paper states 'shotgun metagenomics' as method. ENA records show library_strategy_mix of 'WGS:16' (16 whole-genome shotgun samples), library_source as 'METAGENOMIC' (community DNA, not single organism). This alignment is explicit and consistent. || SUMMARY: This is a rigorous animal gut metagenomics study using shotgun sequencing (WGS) of fecal samples from two murine strains (BALB/c and C57BL/6) infected with Trypanosoma cruzi. The multi-host design is comparative and genuine, not incidental.	none					No reporting errors detected. Paper and deposit are concordant for host (Mus musculus), body site (feces/gut), and study design (two mouse strains as experimental replicates in an animal model of T. cruzi infection).	1		False		True	False
PRJNA977416	Apis mellifera	7460.0		France: Beaumont	21	WGS	pubmed_postdoc	38258019.0	38258019.0	The Response of the Honey Bee Gut Microbiota to Nosema ceranae Is Modulated by the Probiotic Pediococcus acidilactici and the Neonicotinoid Thiamethoxam.	Sbaghdi T	Delbac F	Insecta	The honey bee Apis mellifera is exposed to a variety of biotic and abiotic stressors, such as the highly prevalent microsporidian parasite Nosema (Vairimorpha) ceranae and neonicotinoid insecticides. Both can affect honey bee physiology and microbial gut communities, eventually reducing its lifespan. They can also have a combined effect on the insect's survival. The use of bacterial probiotics has been proposed to improve honey bee health, but their beneficial effect remains an open question. In the present study, western honey bees were experimentally infected with N. ceranae spores, chronically exposed to the neonicotinoid thiamethoxam, and/or supplied daily with the homofermentative bacterium Pediococcus acidilactici MA18/5M thought to improve the honey bees' tolerance to the parasite. Deep shotgun metagenomic sequencing allowed the response of the gut microbiota to be investigated with a taxonomic resolution at the species level. All treatments induced significant changes in honey bee gut bacterial communities. Nosema ceranae infection increased the abundance of Proteus mirabilis, Frischella perrara, and Gilliamella apicola and reduced the abundance of Bifidobacterium asteroides, Fructobacillus fructosus, and Lactobacillus spp. Supplementation with P. acidilactici overturned some of these alterations, bringing back the abundance of some altered species close to the relative abundance found in the controls. Surprisingly, the exposure to thiamethoxam also restored the relative abundance of some species modulated by N. ceranae. This study shows that stressors and probiotics may have an antagonistic impact on honey bee gut bacterial communities and that P. acidilactici may have a protective effect against the dysbiosis induced by an infection with N. ceranae.	2024.0	Microorganisms	YES		METAGENOMIC	YES	Metazoa	Arthropoda	Hymenoptera	Apidae	Apis	Apis mellifera	10.3390/microorganisms12010192				WGS:21	21	1	Apis mellifera		bee metagenome		honeybee gut microbiota	21.0	https://doi.org/10.3390/microorganisms12010192	True	True	Apis mellifera	True	honey bee	True	honey bee	False		False	ENA + paper	Metagenomic sequencing was performed on gut samples from experimentally treated western honey bees (Apis mellifera).	UNIFORM	21	METAGENOMIC:21	21	21	KEEP	yes	high	gut_explicit	single_clear	Apis mellifera	False	agree	GUT: yes (high) — Abstract explicitly states 'honey bee gut communities' and study examines how treatments affect the microbiota; library_source is METAGENOMIC (community DNA); sample titles confirm 'honeybee gut microbiota'; 21 WGS samples from gut community. || BODY_SITE: gut_explicit — Sample titles explicitly state 'honeybee gut microbiota'; abstract repeatedly references 'microbial gut communities' and 'gut'; no other body sites mentioned. || HOST: Apis mellifera [single_clear] — Host field and host species both state 'Apis mellifera'; paper title and abstract confirm this is the sole experimental host; only 1 distinct host species recorded; all 21 samples from the same species. || METHOD: agree — ENA library_strategy_mix shows WGS:21 (21 whole-genome shotgun samples); library_source is METAGENOMIC (community DNA appropriate for gut microbiota); abstract describes experimental design collecting gut microbiota under different treatment conditions; methods align with shotgun metagenomic profiling of microbial communities. || SUMMARY: This is a straightforward, single-host experimental study of honey bee gut microbiota responses to parasitic infection, insecticide exposure, and probiotic supplementation. All four criteria are clearly satisfied with high confidence.	none					No reporting errors detected. Paper and deposit are in perfect alignment on host identity (Apis mellifera), sample type (gut microbiota), and study design.	1		False		True	False
PRJNA1243555	Escherichia coli			Ecuador	20	OTHER	pubmed_postdoc	40576355.0	40576355.0	Oral and parenteral treatment with a third-generation cephalosporin promotes the proliferation of diverse ESBL-producing Escherichia coli in the chicken intestinal tract.	López L	Trueba G	Aves	The global rise of antimicrobial resistance is a major public health threat, with Escherichia coli facilitating the spread of extended-spectrum beta-lactamase (ESBL) genes like blaCTX-M, which confer resistance to third-generation cephalosporins (3GCs). This study examines the impact of 3GC treatment on resistant E. coli clones and horizontal gene transfer (HGT) of ESBL genes in broiler chickens in Quito, Ecuador. Fifteen-day-old Ross broilers were divided into three groups: oral ceftriaxone (100 mg/kg), parenteral ceftriaxone (100 mg/kg intramuscular), and control (no treatment). The study included three phases: baseline, antimicrobial administration (5 days), and recovery (15 days). Fecal cultures on McConkey agar, with and without ceftriaxone (2 µg/mL), measured the ratio of 3GC-resistant lactose fermenters. Regardless of the administration route, ceftriaxone significantly increased resistant coliforms (>80%). Five E. coli colonies per animal and time point were analyzed using single-gene typing, with clonal candidates subjected to whole-genome sequencing. Clonal analysis revealed high genetic diversity, averaging three distinct clones per animal. A unique lineage (H34) emerged exclusively during treatment, and new clones appeared post-treatment. The blaCTX-M-55 variant was the most abundant ESBL gene, persisting despite fluctuations in other blaCTX-M variants. Comparative plasmid analysis suggested blaCTX-M-55 HGT, as plasmids were identified in two genetically distinct E. coli isolates from the same host. Most plasmids belonged to IncFII, with IncX1 and IncN also present. These findings highlight how 3GC treatments rapidly impact ESBL-producing E. coli diversity in the intestine.IMPORTANCEThe global rise of antimicrobial resistance (AMR) poses a critical public health challenge, with Escherichia coli playing a central role in the spread of extended-spectrum beta-lactamase (ESBL) genes like blaCTX-M, which confer resistance to third-generation cephalosporins (3GCs). This study highlights the significant impact of 3GC treatment on the frequency and diversity of 3GC-resistant E. coli clones and horizontal gene transfer of ESBL genes in the intestinal microbiota of broiler chickens. Understanding how antimicrobial treatments drive resistance dynamics in animal populations is crucial for developing strategies to mitigate AMR in both human and veterinary settings.	2025.0	mSphere	YES		OTHER	YES	Animalia	Chordata	Galliformes	Phasianidae	Gallus	Gallus gallus	10.1128/msphere.00227-25				OTHER:13; WGS:7	7	0			Escherichia coli	Chicken feces	One Health Enteric sample from Escherichia coli; pE018; pE022; pE023; pE035	20.0	https://doi.org/10.1128/msphere.00227-25	True	True	Escherichia coli	True	chicken	True	broiler chicken	False		False	ENA + paper	Fecal samples from broiler chickens were cultured and E. coli isolates were sequenced via whole-genome sequencing.	UNIFORM_WITH_GAPS	13	OTHER:13; GENOMIC:7	0	7	REVIEW	yes	high	gut_explicit	single_clear	Gallus gallus (broiler chicken)	False	mismatch	GUT: yes (high) — Title explicitly states 'chicken intestinal tract'; abstract describes fecal sampling from broiler chickens across three treatment groups; isolation source field states 'Chicken feces'; this is a genuine animal gut microbiome study examining E. coli proliferation in response to antimicrobial treatment. || BODY_SITE: gut_explicit — Title specifies 'chicken intestinal tract'; abstract states 'Fecal cultures' were measured; isolation source explicitly lists 'Chicken feces'; feces are intestinal output and represent gut microbiome sampling. || HOST: Gallus gallus (broiler chicken) [single_clear] — Title and abstract consistently identify 'broiler chickens' as the experimental subjects; 'Fifteen-day-old Ross broilers' specifies the breed and age; all samples derived from chicken feces; this is a single-host comparative treatment study, not a multi-host survey. || METHOD: mismatch — Abstract and title describe a culture-based approach ('Fecal cultures on McConkey agar') and targeted antimicrobial resistance assay, yet ENA records show library_strategy_mix includes 7 WGS (shotgun metagenomics) and 13 OTHER samples. The abstract excerpt provided does not mention WGS/metagenomic sequencing; the 7 WGS samples suggest whole-genome sequencing of isolates or community DNA was performed, but this contradicts the culture-based methodology emphasis in the abstract. || SUMMARY: This is a valid animal gut study of broiler chickens with explicit intestinal tract and fecal sampling. However, there is a notable discrepancy: the abstract emphasizes culture-based enumeration on selective media, but the ENA metadata indicates 7 WGS samples were generated, suggesting either isolate sequencing or metagenomics that is not clearly described in the provided abstract excerpt.	host_missing		paper names: Broiler chickens (Gallus gallus domesticus); deposit blank/generic			Host_missing issue: the deposit host field incorrectly lists the bacterial pathogen (E. coli) instead of the animal host (chickens), despite the isolation source correctly identifying chicken feces.	1		False		True	False
PRJNA655594	C57BL/6J			Japan:Tokyo	10	WGS	pubmed_postdoc	35468931.0	35468931.0	Shotgun metagenomic sequencing revealed the prebiotic potential of a grain-based diet in mice.	Jangid A	Prakash T	Mammalia	In the present study, we elucidated the effect of grain-based (GB) diet containing both soluble and insoluble fibers and purified ingredients-based (PIB) diet containing only insoluble fiber, namely cellulose on mice gut microbiome using whole shotgun based metagenomic sequencing. Although the fiber content in both diet types is the same (5%) the presence of soluble fiber only in the GB diet differentiates it from the PIB diet. The taxonomic analysis of sequenced reads reveals a significantly higher enrichment of probiotic Lactobacilli in the GB group as compared to the PIB group. Further, the enhancement of energy expensive cellular processes namely, cell cycle control, cell division, chromosome partitioning, and transcription is observed in the GB group which could be due to the metabolization of the soluble fiber for faster energy production. In contrast, a higher abundance of cellulolytic bacterial community namely, the members of family Lachnospiraceae and Ruminococcaceae and the metabolism functions are found in the PIB group. The PIB group shows a significant increase in host-derived oligosaccharide metabolism functions indicating that they might first target the host-derived oligosaccharides and self-stored glycogen in addition to utilising the available cellulose. In addition to the beneficial microbial community variations, both the groups also exhibited an increased abundance of opportunistic pathobionts which could be due to an overall low amount of fiber in the diet. Furthermore, backtracing analysis identified probiotic members of Lactobacillus, viz., L. crispatus ST1, L. fermentum CECT 5716, L. gasseri ATCC 33323, L. johnsonii NCC 533 and L. reuteri 100-23 in the GB group, while Bilophila wadsworthia 3_1_6, Desulfovibrio piger ATCC 29098, Clostridium symbiosum WAL-14163, and Ruminococcaceae bacterium D16 in the PIB group. These data suggest that Lactobacilli, a probiotic community of microorganisms, are the predominant functional contributors in the gut of GB diet-fed mice, whereas pathobionts too coexisted with commensals in the gut microbiome of the PIB group. Thus at 5% fiber, GB modifies the gut microbial ecology more effectively than PIB and the inclusion of soluble fiber in the GB diet may be one of the primary factors responsible for this impact.	2022.0	Scientific reports	NO	True	METAGENOMIC	YES	Animalia	Chordata	Rodentia	Muridae	Mus	musculus	10.1038/s41598-022-10762-3				WGS:10	10	0			mouse gut metagenome	feces	Metagenomic sample (AIN_a); Metagenomic sample (AIN_b); Metagenomic sample (AIN_c); Metagenomic sample (AIN_d); Metagenomic sample (AIN_e)	10.0	https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-022-10762-3	True	True	C57BL/6J	True	mice	True	mice	False		False	ENA + paper	Shotgun metagenomic sequencing was performed on gut microbiome samples from mice fed different diets (grain-based vs purified ingredients-based).	UNIFORM	10	METAGENOMIC:10	10	10	KEEP	yes	high	gut_explicit	single_clear	Mus musculus (C57BL/6J strain)	False	agree	GUT: yes (high) — Paper title states 'shotgun metagenomic sequencing revealed the prebiotic potential of a grain-based diet in mice' and abstract explicitly describes 'mice gut microbiome using whole shotgun based metagenomic sequencing.' Library source is METAGENOMIC (community DNA), and isolation source is 'feces.' || BODY_SITE: gut_explicit — Biome label: 'mouse gut metagenome'; isolation source: 'feces'; abstract: 'mice gut microbiome.' All three independently confirm gut origin. || HOST: Mus musculus (C57BL/6J strain) [single_clear] — Host field explicitly states 'C57BL/6J' (an inbred laboratory mouse strain). Paper title and abstract consistently refer to 'mice' as the single host. All 10 samples are from this single host strain in a controlled dietary comparison study. || METHOD: agree — Abstract describes 'whole shotgun based metagenomic sequencing'; ENA library_strategy_mix shows 'WGS:10' (10 whole-genome shotgun samples); library_source is METAGENOMIC. Method description and ENA metadata are consistent. || SUMMARY: This is a genuine, single-host animal (mouse) gut microbiome study using shotgun metagenomic sequencing of fecal samples. The study compares two dietary treatments in a controlled experimental design with clear gut sampling and appropriate molecular methods.	none					No reporting errors detected. Paper describes a mouse gut-microbiome study consistent with deposit metadata (C57BL/6J mouse, fecal samples, WGS metagenomic sequencing).	1		False		True	False
PRJNA1157353	Apis cerana	7461.0		Malaysia: Peninsular Malaysia	200	WGS	pubmed_postdoc	41083440.0	41083440.0	Evolution of gut microbiota across honeybee species revealed by comparative metagenomics.	Prasad A	Engel P	Insecta	Studying gut microbiota evolution across animals is crucial for understanding symbiotic interactions but is hampered by the lack of high-resolution genomic data. Honeybees, with their specialized gut microbiota and well-known ecology, offer an ideal system to study this evolution. Using shotgun metagenomics on 200 worker bees from five honeybee species, we recover thousands of metagenome-assembled genomes and identify several novel bacterial species. While microbial communities were mostly host-specific, we found both specialists and generalists, even among closely related bacterial species, with notable variation between honeybee hosts. Some bacterial generalists emerged host-specific only at the strain level, suggesting recent host switches. While we found some signal of co-diversification between hosts and symbionts, this was not more than expected by chance and was much less pronounced than what has been observed for gut bacteria of hominids and small mammals. Instead, symbiont gains, losses, and replacements emerged as important factors for honeybees. This highly dynamic evolution of the specialized honey bee gut microbiota has led to taxonomic and functional differences across hosts, such as the ability to degrade pollen-derived pectin. Our results provide new insights into the evolutionary processes that govern gut microbiota diversity across closely related hosts and uncover the functional potential of the previously underexplored gut microbiota of these important pollinators.	2025.0	Nature communications	YES		METAGENOMIC	YES	Metazoa	Arthropoda	Hymenoptera	Apidae	Apis	Apis cerana	10.1038/s41467-025-64115-5				WGS:200	200	5	Apis andreniformis; Apis cerana; Apis dorsata; Apis florea; Apis mellifera		insect metagenome		A1-1-hindgut-metagenome; A1-2-hindgut-metagenome; A1-3-hindgut-metagenome; A1-4-hindgut-metagenome; A1-5-hindgut-metagenome	200.0	https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-025-64115-5	True	True	Apis cerana	True	honeybee species	True	honeybee (worker bees from five honeybee species)	False		False	ENA + paper	Shotgun metagenomics was performed on 200 worker bees from five honeybee species.	DIVERSE	200	METAGENOMIC:200	200	200	KEEP	yes	high	gut_explicit	multi_host	Apis (five species: A. andreniformis, A. cerana, A. dorsata, A. florea, A. mellifera)	False	agree	GUT: yes (high) — Paper title explicitly states 'gut microbiota across honeybee species'; abstract confirms 'shotgun metagenomics on 200 worker bees'; sample titles explicitly label 'hindgut-metagenome'; library_source is METAGENOMIC (community DNA, not single-organism genomics). || BODY_SITE: gut_explicit — Sample titles uniformly contain 'hindgut-metagenome' (e.g., 'A1-1-hindgut-metagenome'); abstract discusses 'gut microbiota'; no alternative body sites mentioned. || HOST: Apis (five species: A. andreniformis, A. cerana, A. dorsata, A. florea, A. mellifera) [multi_host] — Paper title states 'across honeybee species'; abstract explicitly describes 'comparative metagenomics' on 'five honeybee species'; metadata lists 5 distinct host species; study is explicitly comparative across multiple honeybee hosts, not incidental sampling. Host field defaults to A. cerana but samples span all five species. || METHOD: agree — Stated method: 'shotgun metagenomics on 200 worker bees'; ENA fields: library_strategy_mix = 'WGS:200', library_source = 'METAGENOMIC', WGS/shotgun sample count = 200. Perfect alignment. || SUMMARY: This is a genuine multi-host animal (honeybee) gut microbiome comparative study using shotgun metagenomics on 200 hindgut samples across five Apis species. All four criteria are robustly supported by explicit, consistent evidence.	multihost_issue				Paper abstract states: 'Using shotgun metagenomics on 200 worker bees from five honeybee species' and discusses microbiota evolution 'across' multiple honeybee species as the core subject. Deposit sample-level metadata explicitly lists five distinct Apis species (A. andreniformis, A. cerana, A. dorsata, A. florea, A. mellifera) across the samples.	Multi-host issue detected: study encompasses five distinct honeybee species (explicit in deposit sample-level data), which is appropriately framed in the paper but represents a genuinely multi-host comparative design.	1		False		True	False
PRJNA1145617	Meleagris gallopavo	9103.0		Norway	104	WGS	pubmed_postdoc	40833800.0	40833800.0	Monensin phase-out in Norwegian turkey production decreases Bifidobacterium spp. abundance while enhancing microbial diversity.	Kaspersen HP	Urdahl AM	Aves	Intestinal tissue damage caused by coccidiosis is an important predisposing factor for necrotic enteritis in turkeys, and both diseases are common health issues in turkey production. In Norway, the in-feed ionophore coccidiostat monensin has been used as a preventive measure to combat coccidiosis since the late 1980s. In 2022, however, preventive use of monensin was phased out, which led to an undesired increase in antibiotic treatments among turkey flocks, largely due to necrotic enteritis. The aim of this study was to investigate the overall effects of the preventive monensin use and antibiotic treatment on the turkey caecal microbiota. A total of 102 flock samples from the Norwegian turkey population were included, and metagenomic datasets were generated through shotgun sequencing. All datasets were processed with the Taxprofiler pipeline, followed by diversity, redundancy and differential abundance analyses in R. A significant decrease in alpha and beta diversity was observed for the caecal samples from turkeys exposed to monensin, compared with the non-exposed. An increased abundance of Bifidobacterium spp. was observed in the samples from monensin-exposed turkeys, including Bifidobacterium pullorum, Bifidobacterium longum, Bifidobacterium pseudolongum, Bifidobacterium pseudocatenulatum and Bifidobacterium animalis. Additionally, a decrease in Megamonas and Megasphaera species was detected in these samples. Further, species within the Clostridium genus were higher in abundance among the samples from female turkeys compared with male turkeys. The results indicate that the use of monensin seems to decrease the overall diversity and promote the abundance of Bifidobacterium spp. in the caecum of turkeys, while decreasing the abundance of Megamonas and Megasphaera species. The use of monensin may be beneficial for the gut microbiota due to an increase in favourable Bifidobacterium spp. In contrast, treatment with phenoxymethylpenicillin (penicillin V) early in the turkey life cycle does not seem to cause long-term changes in the caecal microbiota composition. However, further studies are needed to investigate the effects of a decreased abundance of Bifidobacterium spp. and increased gut microbiota diversity in turkeys in the absence of monensin use.	2025.0	Microbial genomics	YES		METAGENOMIC	YES	Metazoa	Chordata	Galliformes	Phasianidae	Meleagris	Meleagris gallopavo	10.1099/mgen.0.001466				WGS:104	104	1	Meleagris gallopavo		gut metagenome		Metagenome or environmental sample from gut metagenome	104.0	https://doi.org/10.1099/mgen.0.001466	True	True	Meleagris gallopavo	True	turkey	True	turkey	False		False	ENA + paper	Metagenomic sequencing was performed on caecal samples from 102 turkey flocks in Norwegian turkey production.	UNIFORM	104	METAGENOMIC:104	104	104	KEEP	yes	high	gut_explicit	single_clear	Meleagris gallopavo	False	agree	GUT: yes (high) — Abstract explicitly states 'investigate the overall effects of the preventive monensin use and antibiotic treatment on the turkey caecal microbiota' and 'metagenomic datasets were generated through shotgun sequencing.' Caecal tissue is part of the intestinal gut system. Biome label confirms 'gut metagenome'. || BODY_SITE: gut_explicit — Abstract specifies 'turkey caecal microbiota' (caecum is part of the intestinal tract). Sample titles state 'Metagenome or environmental sample from gut metagenome'. Biome label explicitly lists 'gut metagenome'. || HOST: Meleagris gallopavo [single_clear] — Host field explicitly stated as 'Meleagris gallopavo' (turkey). Paper title and abstract confirm study is about turkey production and turkey flocks. Only 1 distinct host species present across 104 WGS samples. || METHOD: agree — Abstract states 'metagenom