The gut microbiome profile of COVID-19 patients was found to correlate with a 
viral load of SARS-CoV-2, COVID-19 severity, and dysfunctional immune responses, 
suggesting that gut microbiota may be involved in anti-infection. In order to 
investigate the role of gut microbiota in anti-infection against SARS-CoV-2, we 
established a high-throughput in vitro screening system for COVID-19 
therapeutics by targeting the endoribonuclease (Nsp15). We also evaluated the 
activity inhibition of the target by substances of intestinal origin, using a 
mouse model in an attempt to explore the interactions between gut microbiota and 
SARS-CoV-2. The results unexpectedly revealed that antibiotic treatment induced 
the appearance of substances with Nsp15 activity inhibition in the intestine of 
mice. Comprehensive analysis based on functional profiling of the fecal 
metagenomes and endoribonuclease assay of antibiotic-enriched bacteria and 
metabolites demonstrated that the Nsp15 inhibitors were the primary bile acids 
that accumulated in the gut as a result of antibiotic-induced deficiency of bile 
acid metabolizing microbes. This study provides a new perspective on the 
development of COVID-19 therapeutics using primary bile acids.

